# The annotations of the game of Zynaps 2 in 1 as block 2's set-up leaves it at $6800-$FFFF (src/game/setup.asm moves
# it there from block 2's $6385-$FB84), before the set-up's 128K patches: the single source of src/game/*.asm
# (kit/mkgame.py, case.json; tools/mkgame.sh installs them). The format is in kit/mkgame.py, class Annotations:
#
#   @module NAME ; title            the entries from here on go to NAME.asm, the title heads the file
#   @about a paragraph              the module file's header text, one paragraph a line
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name ; title            data (w words, t text, s space; "b Name 8" rows of 8)
#   @res graphics/NAME.bin          the entry is a resource: src/res/..., INCBIN
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry
#   @isub $ADDR LD HL,-6            the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment   a variable past the image: an EQU, its operands by name
#   @inline $ADDR                   a routine that reads its data after the CALL
#
# The game is Hewson's 48K Zynaps (1987) with 152 bytes changed in 11 places (docs/03-build-and-load.md 3.6,
# docs/13-differences-from-zynaps-48k.md 13.4): the default keys, seven delay counts and the banner raster's line loop
# retimed for the Pentagon, and zeros where the 48K tape had a leftover of its mastering machine ($FDE8-$FE4A) and of
# the ROM font ($FFD9-$FFFE); $FFFF, past the 48K tape's block, holds $73. The names and descriptions of the bytes both
# games share come from the 48K case (../zynaps), whose reverse they are; its recordings are named "48K c1", "48K c2",
# "48K r1" (../zynaps/work). This case's own recordings are c1 (stage 1's power-on recording, its game window:
# frames 3313-13702, the menu and six short games), r1 and r2 (the bot runs that witnessed the five hooks the play of
# c1 never reached, work/stage2/stubs): case.json's read_builds, and the counts of docs/11 and docs/12.
#
# On a 128K the set-up (src/game/setup.asm) and the glue (src/game/glue.asm) patch the game after the move: the beeper
# calls go to the hook stubs, the interrupt handlers step the page-4 engine, the beeper routines are cut off. The
# routines they patch say so ("On a 128K"); the bytes here are the game's as it is moved, the same on a 48K, where
# the 2 in 1 runs the game with its own beeper sound and only the options patched.

@equ $66E0 BigSpriteGlyphs ; 8-byte linear glyphs read by BuildBigSprite $8F0C when A = 1 (Start copies them from $C6E0 with the tiles to $5C00-$67FF)

@module leveldata ; The level data: enemy paths, waves, kinds, the level table and scripts, the scenery
@about All the level data is one resource, src/res/levels/levels.lev.txt (tools/res_levels.py, every byte decoded and proved both ways; the formats in src/res/levels/README.md). The code reads each table at a fixed address.
@about A level is an 8-byte record of LevelTable: LevelStart $B062 copies the record of LevelOrder[+6] into the player record's +$0D (then into ScriptState $81D7 by $AD56): the level script's start, the play flags, the scroll counter and the scenery script number.
@about The level script (WaveScriptStep $AD66, once in four passes, every 8 frames, while the ship lives) is the level's timeline: a step is a wait in counts (one count = one WaveScriptStep run, 8 frames) and a command: a wave launch (LaunchWave $91EC), the scroll on or off, a counter (the only one the scripts use is ScriptCounter $81E4), a conditional jump, the save of the state at a checkpoint (op 14, $AED2) and the level's end (op 5). The scenery script (ScriptMain $8D72, a coroutine) places object groups of shapes into the map ring 16 columns at a time.
@about A wave definition gives the kind of its enemies (EnemyKinds: speed, accelerations, handler, colour set, hit points), their path (the path bytecode run by RunPath $94CB) and their start positions; the script's byte after the wave number is the wave's row offset.

$6800 b PathStarts 14 ; The start of each of the 28 enemy paths: (address - $681C) / 2
> LaunchWave $91EC: E = PathStarts[the path number], the path pointer (record +$1D/+$1E) = $681C + 2E ($9324-$9331). A wave's enemies take the path of their kind unless the wave names one (bit 7 of its kind byte). 48K c2: the bytes of paths 7 and 15 read ($6807, $680F).
@res levels/levels.bin
$681C b PathCode 16 ; The path bytecode of the enemies, 28 paths
> RunPath $94CB (48K c2: 1405 entries): a byte with bit 7 set is the frame count of the command after it (0 = for ever); a command's bits 0-2 pick the routine from PathOps $8114, bits 3-6 are its parameter (7 = an extended op from PathExtOps $8124; extended op 3 is a loop with a word target). Every path starts on an even offset (the table holds offset / 2). 48K c2 reads $6864-$6866 and $6888-$688B (paths 7 and 15).
@res levels/levels.bin
$6926 b WaveIndex 16 ; Wave number -> the offset / 2 of its definition from $6943 (29 waves)
> LaunchWave $921A: n = WaveIndex[A], the definition at $6943 + 2n. 48K c2: waves 7, 15 ($692D, $6935) launched.
@res levels/levels.bin
$6943 b WaveDefinitions 16 ; The 29 wave definitions
> Each: the bonus byte (wave record +1: bits 6-7 the drop, the rest the bonus score), the kind (bit 7: a path number and one more byte follow), the enemy count, the gap (frames between the enemies, the delay +$1B grows by it), then x, y for each enemy (y $FF = random row 0-127). x is halved and added to $C0, y to the script's row byte ($9344-$936D). Every definition has an even length. 48K c2: $698B-$6996 and $69D9-$69DE (waves 7 and 15).
@res levels/levels.bin
$6A65 b EnemyKinds 8 ; The 22 enemy kinds, 8 bytes each
> Copied by LaunchWave ($9261-$92BB) into the wave template $803E: +0 -> +$18, +1 the colour select (with WeaponRatingLevel $80E9: the set of KindColours; AND $E3 -> +$21), +2 low nibble -> +$0C (score index), high -> +$17 (speed), +3 -> +$10 (X acceleration), +4 -> +$14 (Y acceleration), +5 -> +$07 (the handler kind of KindHandlers $80FA), +6 -> +$25 (colour), +7 -> +$0D (hit points, $FF invulnerable). Kinds $14 and up use sprite cache record 0. 48K c2: kinds 7 and 15.
@res levels/levels.bin
$6B15 b KindColours 3 ; 8 colour sets of 3 bytes: +$19, +$20, +$22 of the enemy
> Index ((WeaponRatingLevel + the kind's colour select) AND $1C) / 4 ($9271-$9293): a better armed ship meets the other set. 48K c2: $6B15-$6B1A.
@res levels/levels.bin
@isub $B078 LD HL,LevelTable-8
$6B2D b LevelTable 8 ; The 12 levels, 8 bytes each
> LevelStart $B062 copies LevelTable[LevelOrder[+6]] (HL = $6B25 + 8 (n + 1)) to the record's +$0D-+$14: +0 a byte (0 in all twelve, ScriptState $81D7, never read), +1/+2 the level script, +3 PlayFlags ($FF), +4/+5 ScrollCount (the start value $40, $30, $38 or $10: the number of map columns ReplayScroll $AF6B builds at the level start, not a speed), +6 the scenery script number ($81DD), +7 ScriptLevelBias ($81DE, added to WeaponRating).
@res levels/levels.bin
$6B8D b LevelScripts 16 ; The level scripts of the 12 levels (the wave timelines)
> Run by WaveScriptStep $AD66 (48K c2: 165 entries, the steps $6B8D-$6BA6 of level 1). A step: the wait (ScriptWaitCount $81E1 counts the WaveScriptStep runs, 8 frames each, up to it), the command: below $40 an op of ScriptCommands $81FF (ops 7-12 take one or two words); $40-$7F a jump on the condition (ScriptConditions $8223, on the flags ScriptFlags $81E2 of the last counter op) to the word; $80-$BF wave A AND $3F with the next byte as its row offset; $C0-$FF a conditional call. All twelve end with op 5 (the level's end) and a $FF wait.
@res levels/levels.bin
$72A9 b CellPatterns 8 ; 7 colour patterns of 8 attributes for WorkCell $FFD8
> ScriptSetCell $8EC5 copies pattern n (a scenery script byte $C0+n) to $FFD8-$FFDF; a shape row's odd attribute byte $B1+2k takes WorkCell byte k (the scenery's colour scheme). 48K c2: pattern 2 ($72B9-$72C0).
@res levels/levels.bin
$72E1 w SceneryScriptTable 8 ; The 39 scenery scripts, indexed by ScriptNumber $81DD
> RunScript $8D7A (48K c2: reads $72E3 = script 1, then $7321-$7324 = scripts 32 and 33 called by it). Levels use scripts 1-4, 6, 8-12 and 14; 15 is unused (it names groups 59 and 60, which point at ShapeTable).
@res levels/levels.bin
$732F b SceneryScripts 16 ; The scenery script bodies
> A byte below $80 places that object group (DrawGroup $8DCA) and yields until 16 more columns have scrolled; $80 n calls script n (the variant mask saved); $E0+n sets the variant mask ScriptVariant $8021 = n * 16; $C0+n loads colour pattern n; $FF ends (the top script restarts).
@res levels/levels.bin
$749A w GroupTable 8 ; The 61 object groups, word pointers
> DrawGroup $8DCA ($8DD0). 48K c2 reads entries 30-38 ($74D6-$74E7). Entries 59 and 60 point at ShapeTable ($7783): only scenery script 15 uses them, and no level runs it.
@res levels/levels.bin
$7514 b ObjectGroups 16 ; The group bodies: 3-byte objects, $FF the end
> An object: byte 0 bits 0-4 the row, bit 5 drawn bottom up, bit 6 mirrored; byte 1 the variant bits (high nibble, AND ScriptVariant: none set, skipped) and the column (low nibble); byte 2 the shape. A byte $80-$FE is a sub-group (byte 1) taken when its bits 0-6 shifted left meet the variant mask (none in the data). 48K c2: groups 30-38, $7615-$76DD.
@res levels/levels.bin
$7783 w ShapeTable 8 ; The 79 shapes, word pointers
> DrawGroup $8E15 (index * 2 added to $7783). Entries 30 and 31 point at the 2 bytes before shape 32 (no group uses them).
@res levels/levels.bin
$7821 b Shapes 16 ; The shape bodies: the row count, then per row an attribute, a cell mask and the tiles
> PlaceColumn $8EA1 (48K c2: 209 entries): the attribute byte (odd: WorkCell byte (A - $B1) / 2), then a mask whose set bits, from bit 7, are the columns that get a tile; one tile byte per set bit, written to the map ring (the attribute to the attribute ring).
@res levels/levels.bin

@module effectdata ; The sound effects and the sprite descriptors of the ship, the shots and the explosions
@about EffectPlay $B30C copies effect n (1-6) from EffectTable; the descriptors are the 13-byte sprite records an object's +0/+1 points at (the same layout as the first 13 bytes of a sprite cache record).

$7E5A b EffectTable 8 ; The 6 sound effects, 8 bytes each, from effect 1
> EffectPlay $B30C: entry $7E5A + 8 (n - 1) -> EffectState $7F8A-$7F90 and the segment lengths $7F91/$7F92: +0 pitch A, +1 its step, +2 pitch B, +3 its step, +4 the step change per segment, +5 the segments, +6 the flags, +7 the segment lengths (nibbles). 48K c2 reads effects 1, 2 and 4 ($7E5A-$7E69, $7E72-$7E79).
@res effects/effects.bin
$7E8A b EffectSpare 10 ; 10 zero bytes after effect 6
> No reader in 48K c1/c2 (the request numbers in the code are 1-6 and $32).

$7E94 b ShipDescriptor 13 ; The sprite descriptor of the ship (record $F36C +0/+1)
> +0/+1 the image ($E008), +4 and +6 the size of a shifted copy, +8 the height in rows (read by TestHitGrid $9150: $7E9C), +$0B the width (read $7E9F), +$0C the row mask.
$7EA1 b ExplosionAnim 13 ; The explosion's sprite and animation record
> Set by StartExplosion $A293 (record +0/+1); MarkEnemyGrid $90CB reads +8/+$0B ($7EA9, $7EAC).
$7EAE b SightDescriptor 13 ; The sight's sprite descriptor (record $F600, kind 6)
> TestHitGrid reads +8/+$0B ($7EB6, $7EB9).
$7EBB b PodAnim 13 ; The weapon pod's sprite and animation record
> Set by ExplosionToPod $A2D6; MarkEnemyGrid reads $7EC3, $7EC6.
$7EC8 b LaserDescriptor 13 ; The laser shot's sprite descriptor (the four records from $F4F8)
> TestHitGrid reads $7ED0, $7ED3.
$7ED5 b ShotDescriptor 13 ; The enemy shot's and the plasma ball's sprite descriptor
> MarkEnemyGrid and TestHitGrid read $7EDD, $7EE0.

$7EE2 b SpriteCache 16 ; The sprite cache: record 0 and records 1-6, 16 bytes each
> GetSpriteCache $93A7 (48K c2: 7 entries): kinds $14 and up use record 0 ($7EE2, its image at $C008, 7 x 7 cells) without caching; the others search records 1-6 for one of their kind (+$0E = kind OR $80, +$0D the use count) or take a free one. +0/+1 the image buffer ($E748, $E988, $EBC8, $EE08), +4/+6 the size ($48, $60), +8 the rows, +$0B the width, +$0C the row mask, +$0F the record bit. LevelSetUp $8D28 clears the six use counts.
$7EF2 SpriteCacheRecord1 ; record 1 (the use count +$0D at $7EFF, the search's first)
$7EFF SpriteCacheUse1 ; record 1's use count, the search's start ($93B3) and LevelSetUp's ($8D22)
$7F02 SpriteCacheRecord2
$7F12 SpriteCacheRecord3
$7F22 SpriteCacheRecord4
$7F32 SpriteCacheRecord5
$7F42 SpriteCacheRecord6
$7F52 b SpareCacheRecord 16 ; A seventh record of the same layout, out of the search (B = 6)
> No reader or writer in 48K c1/c2; its values follow the pattern of records 5 and 6 (buffer $E988, size $60).

@module gamevars ; The game variables: the play passes, the scroll, the sound, the current player
@about The variables the play interrupt and the scheduler share. Names follow the routines that own them (F1-F4); the evidence for each is its reader or writer.

$7F62 b PassCount 1 ; +1 each play pass (PlayInterrupt $AA03); bit 0 = an odd pass
> 48K c2 (TTD frames 300-316): +1 every 2 frames. Read by the explosion step $A1C8, the panel $AB13.
$7F63 b PassBit 1 ; Rotated left each pass ($AA0A): AND an object's bit +$24 picks the objects that act this pass
> EnemyFire $A0FE, the fuel tick (bit 0) in PlayerHandler $9A5B.
$7F64 b PassDepth 1 ; +1 on the entry of PlayInterrupt, -1 at its end: 1 = not nested
$7F65 b PassPhase 1 ; The play pass phase 1-4 then 0; 4 on entry: the pass is skipped ($A9FB)
> Phase 1 starts a pass ($AA49), 2 runs the level script ($AAAD), odd phases MarkEnemyGrid.
$7F66 w ChainStep 1 ; The drawing chain's current step: $AAE5, +5 each phase (PlayChain $AACC)
$7F68 w NodeFreeList 1 ; The head of the span node free list (pool $F800; LevelSetUp $8C3E)
$7F6A b ShiftPage 1 ; The glyph cache page of the fine scroll phase: $C0 $C8 $D8 $D0 (a Gray code)
$7F6B b FineScrollToggle 1 ; $08 or $10, XORed into ShiftPage by ScrollControl $A9D2
$7F6C b ScrollThisPass 1 ; $FF when the pass scrolls one fine step, 0 when not; PathOpScroll $9640 tests bit 0
$7F6D w TileSet 1 ; The tile set pointer $5C00 of BuildGlyph $89C9 (L replaced by the tile)
$7F6F b RowsLeft 1 ; The renderer's row counter, 18 down to 0 (DrawPlayfield $85CA)
$7F70 b EmptyRows 1 ; Rows without nodes counted by the scroll step ($8AED)
$7F71 b RandomSeed 5 ; The five bytes of the random number generator (Random $8544, seeded by $8516)
$7F76 b SkipDraw 1 ; Bit 0 set: DrawGroup places nothing (the catch-up loop $AF6B)
$7F77 b PanelBurst 1 ; Set to 6 by FireLaser $9C1D when 0; the panel ($AB13) counts it down, a pair of cells moving out
$7F78 b SpinnerFrame 1 ; The panel spinners' frame 0-15, +1 each odd pass ($AB13)
$7F79 b SpinnerColour 1 ; The spinners' ink, + SpinnerColourStep each odd pass, bouncing between 1 and 7
$7F7A b SpinnerColourStep 1 ; +1 or -1
$7F7B b DeathTimer 1 ; $FF while the ship lives; $16 at its death, counted down (PlayerDying); not $FF stops the scroll and the level script
$7F7C b EndPending 1 ; The end of play asked for: 1 a life lost, 2 the level done (script op 5), 4 abort
$7F7D b EndCode 1 ; EndPending moved here by $ABD5: ends the play loop ($B1E9)
$7F7E b RowBeep 1 ; The beeper bit toggled XOR $18 per playfield row (one of the three explosion-noise toggles; silent while the mask is 0)
$7F7F b RowBeepMask 1 ; Its AND mask: the bit shifted out of SoundPattern1 each pass ($AA30)
$7F80 w SoundPattern1 1 ; A 16-bit pattern shifted left each pass; $FD00 for an explosion (StartExplosion $A293)
$7F82 b ObjectBeep 1 ; The beeper bit toggled per object strip ($8F9B)
$7F83 b ObjectBeepMask 1 ; Its mask: the bit of SoundPattern2 ($AA3B)
$7F84 w SoundPattern2 1 ; $EDB2 for an explosion
$7F86 b SlotBeep 1 ; The beeper bit toggled per object slot (RunObjects $9463)
$7F87 b SlotBeepMask 1 ; Its mask: the bit of SoundPattern3 ($AA46)
$7F88 w SoundPattern3 1 ; $D6ED for an explosion
$7F8A b EffectState 7 ; The playing effect's +0-+6 (EffectPlay $B30C), stepped by $B379
$7F91 b SegLenA 1 ; The effect's segment length A (the low nibble of +7)
$7F92 b SegLenB 1 ; Segment length B (the high nibble)
$7F93 b PitchA 1 ; The effect's pitch A
$7F94 b PitchB 1 ; Pitch B
$7F95 b EffectFrames 1 ; The frames left in the segment
$7F96 b EffectTick 1 ; $80 from the title interrupt $A9E4 and a new effect request, $FF from PlayInterrupt
> The effect's tone loop runs until an interrupt changes it.
$7F97 b CurrentEffect 1 ; The effect playing (0 none, $80 stop); a request lower than it is refused (RequestEffect $B2F0)
$7F98 b CurrentPlayer 1 ; 1 or 2 (AddScore $A495, PrintPlayerScore $A4BF; XOR 3 at the change, $B16D)
$7F99 w PlayerRecord 1 ; The current player's record: $7F9C or $7FCC (10 readers in 48K c1/c2)
$7F9B b Players 1 ; 1 or 2 (SelectPlayers $A790; ChangePlayer $B149)

@module playerdata ; The two player records and the score scratch
@about A player record is 48 bytes: the score, the lives, the level order index, the weapons, the extra life count and, from +$0D, the 35-byte state of the level script and the scroll, saved at each checkpoint (script op 14, $AED2) and taken back by $AD56 when the player starts or restarts. PlayerRecord $7F99 points at the current one.

$7F9C b Player1 16 ; Player 1's record (48 bytes)
> +0-+3 the score (4 BCD bytes, high first; AddScore $A489), +4 the lives (DrawLives $A4EF), +5 the lives lost in the level (LevelStart clears it, PlayerDied $B119 adds 1), +6 the level order index (LevelDone $B174: +1 mod 12), +7 the pod selector 0-4, +8 the side weapon (0 none, 2 plasma, 3 missiles, 4 seeker; script op 5 clears +8/+9), +9 the double plasma flag, +$0A the laser level 0-3, +$0B the speed $40-$A0, +$0C the extra-life index into ExtraLifeScores, +$0D-+$2F the level state (LevelTable's 8 bytes, ScriptStateInit's 5, the rest of ScriptState).
$7FA0 Player1Lives ; +4
$7FA1 Player1LivesLost ; +5
$7FA2 Player1LevelIndex ; +6 the index into LevelOrder
$7FA3 Player1PodSelector ; +7
$7FA4 Player1SideWeapon ; +8
$7FA5 Player1DoublePlasma ; +9
$7FA6 Player1LaserLevel ; +$0A
$7FA7 Player1Speed ; +$0B
$7FA8 Player1ExtraLives ; +$0C the extra lives given
$7FA9 Player1LevelState ; +$0D-+$2F, 35 bytes: the copy of ScriptState $81D7-$81F9
$7FCC b Player2 16 ; Player 2's record (48 bytes), the same fields
> PlayersSetup $AFB7 zeroes both records (96 bytes from $7F9C), gives player 2 three lives when two play, else none.
$7FD0 Player2Lives
$7FD1 Player2LivesLost
$7FD2 Player2LevelIndex
$7FD3 Player2PodSelector
$7FD4 Player2SideWeapon
$7FD5 Player2DoublePlasma
$7FD6 Player2LaserLevel
$7FD7 Player2Speed
$7FD8 Player2ExtraLives
$7FD9 Player2LevelState
$7FFC b BcdScratch 4 ; PrintBcd's copy of the number it prints ($A43E)

@module gameentry ; The entry point the set-up jumps to
@about Three bytes in front of the variables: the set-up's jump target ($8000, JP GameStart at $610B in block 2) goes on to Start.

$8000 c GameEntry ; JP Start: the set-up's way in
> Entered once by block 2's set-up after the move (JP GameStart at $610B; c1 frame 3313), a tail jump to Start ($B6FF). Nothing else jumps here. In the 48K the tape loader's relocation code jumped here.

@module systemvars ; The level order, the scenery coroutine, the wave launcher and the print variables
@about Variables of the scenery coroutine, the sprite queue, the wave launcher and its template, the sprite cache masks, the print position and the menu raster, the shots and the weapons.

$8003 b LevelOrder 12 ; The order of the 12 levels: 0 1 2, six of 3-8 shuffled, 9 10 11
> PlayersSetup $AFB7 fills $8006-$800B with the levels 3-8 in a random order for each game; LevelStart $B06F takes LevelOrder[+6]. Block 2 holds 0 1 2 0 0 0 0 0 0 9 10 11.
$800F b ExtraLifeScores 6 ; The scores of the extra lives: the score's +1 byte (BCD) 01 03 05 07 09, then $FF
> $AC8A: when the score's +1 byte is at least ExtraLifeScores[+$0C], +$0C and the lives go up: extra lives at 10000, 30000, 50000, 70000 and 90000.
$8015 b GlyphAlloc 3 ; The glyph allocator: the slot index, the bitmap byte's low address ($E0-$FF), the bit mask
> Read and written by AllocGlyphs $8912.
$8016 GlyphAllocByte
$8017 GlyphAllocBit
$8018 w MainSP 1 ; The main stack pointer, saved while the scenery coroutine runs ($8D60, $8D69)
$801A w ScriptSP 1 ; The scenery coroutine's stack pointer (its stack top $FDFB, StartScript $8D35)
$801C w ScrollColumn 1 ; The map ring pointer of the leading column ($B760 at the level start; +1 per scroll step)
$801E b ScriptColumn 1 ; The column where the next group goes, +16 per group (ResumeScript $8D45)
$801F b ScriptRoom 1 ; The columns scrolled since the last group: +1 per scroll step, -16 per group
$8020 b ScriptBusy 1 ; $FF while a group is being placed, 0 after it
$8021 b ScriptVariant 1 ; The variant mask (high nibble) the group objects are tested with; $F0 in block 2
$8022 w ScrollMap 1 ; The scenery map position: cell = ScrollMap + 128 Y + X ($B730 at the level start; SceneryCell $99FF)
$8024 b DrawQueueRead 1 ; The sprite build queue's read index 0-7 ($ACAD)
$8025 b DrawQueueWrite 1 ; Its write index (QueueSpriteLoad $9441)
$8026 b DrawQueue 3 ; 8 entries of 3 bytes: the kind, the record address
$803E b WaveTemplate 16 ; The 44-byte object record LaunchWave builds once a wave and copies to each enemy
> Zeroed ($924A), filled from the kind ($9261-$92BB) and the sprite cache ($92C2), then copied into the record IY: +0-+8 and +$0B-+$2B ($9304-$9313; +9/+$0A stay the record's own).
$8067 WaveTarget ; +$29/+$2A the target, the player $F36C
$806A b WavePaths 8 ; The path number of each enemy of the wave being launched ($9231-$9247)
$8072 b FreeEnemySlots 1 ; Free slots of the 8 enemy records at $F398 (5 at the level start)
$8073 b WavesAllowed 1 ; Wave records that may be in use (6 at the level start); LaunchWave -1, the wave's end +1
$8074 b EnemyShotsLeft 1 ; Enemy shots that may still start (3 at the level start; EnemyFire $A113)
$8075 b WaveRecords 3 ; 6 wave records of 3 bytes: the live count ($FF free, bit 7 one escaped), the bonus byte, the kind
$8087 b SpriteCacheMask 1 ; A bit per used sprite cache record ($93A7)
$8088 b SpriteCacheFree 16 ; The free record of a use mask: low nibble, or the high one by bit 4 of the kind ($93D8)
$8098 b BigSpriteMap1 9 ; A 7 x 9 cell map (one bit a cell) for BuildBigSprite with A = 1
$80A1 b BigSpriteMap2 9 ; The cell map for BuildBigSprite with A <> 1
$80AA w PrintPos 1 ; The print position, an attribute address $5800-$5AFF (PrintChar $A33B, print codes 3-6)
$80AC b PrintAttr 1 ; The printed characters' attribute (print codes 0-2)
$80AD b PrintPending 1 ; Below $20: a print code waiting for its byte; $20 none (PrintChar $A326)
$80AE w RasterTable 1 ; The colour table of the menu raster interrupt ($A520): $FC20
$80B0 b RasterLines 1 ; The scan lines the menu raster colours: 168 or 112
$80B1 w RasterList 1 ; The colour list ExpandNextColours takes ($A5B0)
$80B3 b RasterSpare 6 ; Six bytes no routine reads or writes in 48K c1/c2
$80B9 b RasterFrames 1 ; Frames the menu raster still runs (-1 a frame, $A578)
$80BA b LaserShotsFree 1 ; Laser shots not in flight (4 at most, $B2D1); fire when it plus the laser level is 4 or more
$80BB b LaserHitMask 1 ; Per pass: the enemy bits hit by the ship or a laser (TestHitGrid $9150)
$80BC b ColourCycle 1 ; 8, $10, $18: +8 each pass ($AA1B); the pods' flashing colour
$80BD b HarmlessMaskA 1 ; Enemy bits that do not kill the ship nor stop a laser (StartExplosion, ExplosionToPod)
$80BE b HarmlessMaskB 1 ; See HarmlessMaskA
$80BF b ShipHistoryIndex 1 ; The index into ShipHistory (PlayerHandler $9AF3)
$80C0 b ShipHistory 8 ; The ship's last 8 positions, 4 bytes each (X fraction, X, Y fraction, Y); the sight follows the oldest
> PlayerSetUp $B21C fills it by pushing 16 words below $80E0 (SP = $80E0 at $B253).
$80E0 w LockTarget 1 ; The enemy record the sight locked on ($9FFC)
$80E2 b LockMask 1 ; Its collision bit, 0 no lock
$80E3 b MissileHitMask 1 ; Per pass: the enemies the homing missile hits (cleared by RunObjects $94B2)
$80E4 b SightMask 1 ; Per pass: the enemies under the sight
$80E5 b PlasmaHitMask 1 ; Per pass: the enemies the plasma or the seeker hit
$80E6 b Fuel 1 ; The fuel / power timer: -1 on passes with PassBit bit 0, $B4 from a full pod cycle, halved when a pod is lost
$80E7 b WeaponRatingMask 1 ; $FF or $30 (WeaponRating $9B75); EnemyFire $A0F6 reads it
$80E8 b WeaponRatingValue 1 ; The enemies' fire chance (WeaponRating $9B75: + ScriptLevelBias); read only by EnemyFire $A109 (Random below it: a shot)
$80E9 b WeaponRatingLevel 1 ; 0 or 8: picks the KindColours set (LaunchWave $9271)

@module handlertables ; The jump tables of the object kinds, the weapons and the path interpreter, the sine table
@about Word tables JumpTable $91E2 dispatches through (A an index, HL the table).

$80EA w SideWeaponFrames 1 ; The side weapon's routine per pass: $9C83 plasma, $9CCA homing, $9CFE seeker (index +8 - 2, $9B2E)
$80F0 w PodActions 1 ; The pod actions: $9D4C speed, $9D5D laser, $9D68 plasma, $9DA5 homing, $9DDD seeker (index = the selector; $A217, $B284)
$80FA w KindHandlers 1 ; The object handlers by kind (+$07): 0 player, 1 laser, 2 enemy, 3 explosion, 4 pod, 5 enemy shot, 6 sight, 7 seeker, 8 lockable, 9 plasma, 10 missile, 11 enemy, 12 big enemy
> RunObjects $946F/$9487 (48K c2: 6080 JumpTable entries in all).
$8114 w PathOps 1 ; The path commands 0-7 (bits 0-2 of a command): RunPath $94DC
$8124 w PathExtOps 1 ; The 16 extended path ops (bits 3-6 of command 7): PathOpExtended $950F
$8144 b SineTable 16 ; A signed sine, 64 steps a turn ($20 = 1): SetVelocity $98F1 reads it (48K c2: 21 entries)

@module controldata ; The controls, the key names, the life marks, the event table and the level script's state
@about The control state ReadControls $A6B6 leaves, the redefinable keys, the event scheduler's request and mask bytes and its handler table, and ScriptState: the 35 bytes the level script works in (copied from and to the player record).

$8184 b FireHeld 1 ; Passes fire has been held, 0 released (ReadControls $A725)
$8185 b FireChanged 1 ; $FF when fire was pressed or released since the last read ($A73C)
$8186 b ControlBits 1 ; Bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up ($A742)
$8187 b JoystickFlags 1 ; Bit 0 Kempston, bit 1 Sinclair rather than cursor
$8188 b KeyTable 2 ; 5 keys (fire up down left right) as port high byte and bit mask; SPACE Q A O P by default
> The defaults are this game's: $7F01 SPACE (fire), $FB01 Q (up), $FD01 A (down), $DF02 O (left), $DF01 P (right). The 48K had P, L, SYMBOL SHIFT, A, S. KEYBOARD DEFINITION (title key 3) writes them.
$8192 b KeyScanRows 6 ; The half-rows WaitKeyPress scans: $FE $FD $FB $DF $BF $7F
$8198 t KeyNames 5 ; The 40 key names in the order of the half-rows $FEFE..$7FFE; 0-3 = messages 9-12 (CS SS SP EN)
$81C0 b LifeSteps 5 ; The attribute steps between the life marks (DrawLives $A4EF reads 6 bytes: the 6th is EventRequests, unused)
$81C5 b EventRequests 1 ; A bit per job asked for (bit 7 first; EventDispatch $AC07)
$81C6 b EventMask 1 ; The enabled jobs ($FF from the play loop's start)
$81C7 w EventHandlers 1 ; The 8 job routines, bit 7 first
$81D7 b ScriptState 16 ; The level script's state (35 bytes, $AD56 from the player record, $AED2 back)
> +0-+7 come from LevelTable, +8-+$0C from ScriptStateInit, the rest is the call stack. +0 ($81D7) is LevelTable's first byte (0 in every level; nothing reads it).
$81D8 ScriptPC ; the step being waited for (word)
$81DA PlayFlags ; bit 5 the scroll on, bit 7 the stars shown
$81DB ScrollCount ; word, +1 per scroll step (the level's start value from LevelTable)
$81DD ScriptNumber ; the scenery script
$81DE ScriptLevelBias ; added to WeaponRating ($9B9B)
$81DF ScriptStackPtr ; word: the script's call stack ($81F6 at the start, two bytes a call)
$81E1 ScriptWaitCount ; the counts waited for the current step (one a WaveScriptStep run, 8 frames)
$81E2 ScriptFlags ; the flags of the last counter op (the conditions test them)
$81E3 ScrollRestart ; $FF (op 1): start the scroll again at phase 4 (ScrollControl $A9A8)
$81E4 ScriptCounter ; the word every script loops on (ops 7 and 9)
$81E6 ScriptCallStack ; the call stack, 16 bytes below $81F6 (no script calls)
$81FA b ScriptStateInit 5 ; Copied to the record's +$15-+$19 at a level's start: the stack pointer $81F6, wait 0, flags 0, restart 0
$81FF w ScriptCommands 1 ; The level script ops 0-17 (JumpTable from WaveScriptStep $AD86)
$8223 w ScriptConditions 1 ; The 7 jump conditions on ScriptFlags: always, Z, NZ, C, NC, P, M ($AE00)

@module scoredata ; The points, the high-score table and the menu colour lists
@about The award table AddScore reads, the five high scores (a print string and a BCD score each) and the run-length colour lists of the menu raster.

$8231 b PointsTable 4 ; 12 awards x 4 BCD bytes: 0 25 50 75 100 150 200 250 500 1000 2000 5000
> AddScore $A489 adds entry A from $8234 + 4A down (48K c2: 9 entries reading $8235-$823C: 25 and 50).
$8261 b HiScoreTable 14 ; The high-score table: 5 entries of a print string (position, initials) and a 4-byte BCD score
> PrintString $A401 prints the strings, PrintBcd ($A43E) the scores; HighScoreEntry inserts a new one. The editable text: src/res/text/hiscores.hsc.txt.
@res text/hiscores.bin
$82A7 b ColoursTop 13 ; A run-length colour list: 48 lines into $FC20 (MenuScreenSetup $A692, SelectPlayers $A799)
$82CE b ColoursMenu 9 ; 120 lines into $FC50 (MenuScreenSetup $A69D)
$82D7 b ColoursHighlight 9 ; 64 lines into $FC50 (SelectPlayers $A7A8, RedefineKeys $A5D5)
$82E0 b ColoursBar 14 ; The sweeping bar of SelectPlayers ($A7BB); its $FF at $82ED is also the message list's start
$82ED MessageListBase ; the $FF before message 0 (PrintMessage $A427 counts A + 1 $FF bytes from here)

@module texts ; The messages
@about 28 messages, each up to $FF, printed by PrintMessage $A423 (message n: n + 1 $FF bytes from $82ED) and PrintString $A401. The editable text is src/res/text/messages.msg.txt (tools/res_text.py: the print codes as escapes).

$82EE b Messages 16 ; The 28 messages
> The print codes (PrintChar $A321): a byte below $20 takes the next byte: 0 attribute, 1 ink, 2 paper, 3 column, 4 row, 5 column + n, 6 row + n; $1B n prints message n; $80 and up are the game's glyphs ($B54F). 48K c1: PrintMessage 98 entries, PrintString 103.
> On a 128K: the set-up writes "DAVE ROGERS " over "STEVE TURNER" at $8484: the CPC version's musician, whose music the 128K plays (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
@res text/messages.bin
$84BB PlayerDigit ; the digit of "PLAYER n" (message 23), written by $B034
# F1: code and data $8516-$90B4 (system set-up, playfield renderer, stars, scrolling, level set-up, scenery
# scripts, sprite builders and object strips). Evidence: L1 entry counts from tools/l1info.py over ../zynaps/work/c1
# (attract and start, 1 game) and ../zynaps/work/c2 (1500 frames of level 1), reads at 48K c2 frame 800 (instance 14120).

@module system ; Interrupt set-up, random numbers, screen clear and fades
@about The start-up routines that every other module leans on: the IM 2 vector table and its jump to the frame handler, the five-byte random number generator, the attribute fade and the screen clear.
@about The vector table fills $FE00-$FF00 with $FD, so whatever the bus holds the CPU jumps to $FDFD, where a JP to the frame handler sits. The generator is seeded from R mixed with the first five bytes of the ROM.

$8516 c SetUpInterrupts ; Install the IM 2 vector table and seed the random number generator
> Fills the 257 bytes $FE00-$FF00 with $FD, writes JP $A9E4 at $FDFD, sets I = $FE and IM 2. Interrupts stay disabled (DI on entry): the caller enables them.
> Then seeds RandomSeed ($7F71-$7F75): each byte is R XOR the matching ROM byte at $0000-$0004.
> Input: none. Output: A, B, DE, HL changed. Called once from the start-up (48K c1: 1 entry).
> With K-MOUSE the fill starts at $FEE8 (the operand at $851E), past the mouse driver at $FE00 (src/game/setup.asm).
$8522 FillVectorPage ; $FE00-$FEFF, then one more byte at $FF00
$853B SeedRandomLoop ; R XOR ROM byte, five times

$8544 c Random ; Next random number in A
> A shift-register generator over the five bytes $7F71-$7F75: rotates $7F75 right through carry, mixes the neighbour bytes (nibble swap, XOR) and shifts the bytes down; the new $7F71 byte is returned in A.
> BC, DE and HL are preserved by storing them into the operands of the three loads at the end ($8581, $8584, $8587), so the routine needs no stack beyond its own return address (it is called while SP points into data, e.g. from InitStars).
> Output: A random byte, F changed. Callers all over the game (stars, enemies, effects): the most called helper.
$8581 RandomRestoreBC ; operand written on entry: the caller's BC
$8584 RandomRestoreDE ; the caller's DE
$8587 RandomRestoreHL ; the caller's HL

$858B c ClearScreen ; Fade out, then clear the attributes and the bitmap
> Calls FadeAttributes, waits for one frame, sets the border black, clears the attributes $5800-$5AFF to 0 and the bitmap $4000-$57FF to 0 (LD (HL),L with L = 0).
> Input: none. Output: BC, DE, HL changed; interrupts enabled. 48K c1: 4 entries (between the screens of the attract cycle and the game start).

$85AD c FadeAttributes ; Fade the whole attribute area to black in four steps
> Four passes over $5800-$5AFF (3 pages): each pass ANDs every attribute with the mask 7, 3, 1, 0 (the ink fades, paper and bright vanish at once), three frames (HALT) apart. Enables interrupts.
> Output: BC, E, HL changed. Called by ClearScreen (48K c1: 4 entries).
$85B2 FadeStep ; one pass: 3 frames, then the mask over all 768 attributes
$85BA FadeCell

@module playfield ; The playfield renderer: attributes, glyph spans, sprite strips and stars, row by row
@about The playfield is 18 character rows of 32 columns. Nothing is drawn pixel by pixel: every row is a chain of span nodes, each naming a run of 0-4 glyphs out of the glyph cache ($C000-$DFFF) and the gap of empty columns before it.
@about The glyph cache holds 256 glyphs in four copies, shifted by 0, 2, 4 and 6 pixels (pages $C0, $C8, $D8, $D0). A glyph is line-interleaved: line k of glyph n is at page + k*256 + n. ShiftPage ($7F6A) picks the copy for the current scroll phase, so a span handler only copies bytes: LDI and LDD alternate (zigzag) across the run, one pixel line after another.
@about Each row has a 32-byte record at $F12C + 32*row (RowRecords): the map ring pointer, the attribute and screen addresses, the head node, counters for the scroll step and the column builder, the sprite-strip list and the row's star. The renderer runs with SP pointing at the record and POPs its fields.
@about Per row: one beeper toggle (RowBeep $7F7E XOR $18, AND the mask $7F7F, OUT $FE: one of the three explosion-noise toggles, silent while the mask is 0), the 32 attributes pushed from the map ring onto the attribute row, the span nodes, the sprite strips, the star, then a delay that pads every row to the cost of at least four glyphs, so the 18 toggles are evenly spaced.

$85CA c DrawPlayfield ; Draw the 18 rows of the playfield
> Saves SP into the operand of RenderDone, clears the padding balance (the operand of RowEnd), sets RowsLeft ($7F6F) = 18 and IX = SP = $F12C (the first row record), then enters RowStart.
> The rows run as a chain of RETs: RowStart pushes the attributes and returns into the row's head span node; each span handler returns into the next node; the sentinel node ($85E4) returns into RowEnd; RowEnd moves to the next record and jumps to RowStart or, after 18 rows, to RenderDone.
> Called from the play interrupt $A9ED once a pass (two frames) while playing (48K c2: 659 entries in 1500 frames, all from $A9ED).

$85E0 c RenderDone ; Restore the stack pointer and return to the frame handler
> The operand of LD SP is the SP that DrawPlayfield saved.

$85E4 b SentinelNode ; The span node that ends every row: handler RowEnd, then a 0 byte
> A three-byte node: the word $85E7 (RowEnd) and a 0. An empty row's head field (+6/+7 of its record) points here; the last node of every row links here. LevelSetUp writes it into all 18 records.

$85E7 c RowEnd ; End of a row: sprite strips, the star, padding, next row
> Reached by RET from the last span node. A = the padding balance (the operand, written at $86BD) + the row's glyph count (+8); kept in A'.
> When the row has sprite strips (+$15 the count, +$16/+$17 the list) SP moves to the strip list and every strip entry is drawn by StripDraw; then RowStar draws the row's star.
> Input: IX = the row record, B = the screen pixel high byte of the row. Output: falls through to the next row.
> 48K c2: 11592 entries (= 659 frames * 18 rows minus the frames cut short).

$8602 c StripNext ; Return into the next strip entry
> SP points at a strip entry whose first word is StripDraw: the RET starts it. The DJNZ at $8679 loops here once per strip.

$8603 c StripDraw ; Draw one sprite strip into a character row
> A strip entry (built by BuildObjectStrips) holds: the link to the next entry, a word B'/C' (C' = the sprite's colour), the start position (H = the column count, saved in the operand of StripCellsDone; L = the first attribute cell), then the cell bytes.
> Per cell: an attribute whose low six bits are 0 (an empty cell) gets the sprite colour and the eight lines are written plainly (StripCellPlain); an attribute below 8 (black ink on black paper) is skipped (StripCellSkip); otherwise the lines are ANDed into the scenery (StripCellMask) and the cell keeps the scenery colour.
> A' (the row's glyph count) is incremented for every drawn cell, so the padding counts strips as glyphs.
> 48K c2: 7168 entries.
$8611 StripCell ; the next cell: test its attribute
$8621 StripCellPlain ; empty cell: colour it, write 8 lines
$863E StripCellSkip ; dark cell: skip it
$8643 StripCellMask ; scenery cell: AND the 8 lines in
$866C StripCellsDone ; LD SP,n: n's low byte = the strip's H (written at $860A)
$867B c RowStar ; Draw the row's star, pad the row's time, step to the next row
> SP = the operand (saved at $86E2: the record + 8); SP + $14 = the record + $1C: POP HL = the star's position (L) and screen high byte, POP AF = its flags (carry: visible) and colour.
> A visible star on an empty attribute cell sets the attribute and writes a single dot on pixel line 3 (one byte B, the other lines cleared); on a used cell it XORs the dot into line 3 of the scenery.
> Then the padding: A' - 4 left over is the new balance; a row cheaper than four glyphs waits 12 DJNZ loops per missing glyph (the Pentagon timing: 10 in the 48K). IX += 32, SP = IX; RowsLeft - 1; RowStart or RenderDone.
> 48K c2: 11592 entries (as RowEnd).
$86A7 StarOnScenery ; XOR the dot into pixel line 3
$86B0 RowPad ; the row's cost against four glyphs
$86B6 RowPadGlyph ; wait one glyph's time: B = 12 (10 in the 48K)
$86B8 RowPadWait
$86BD NextRow ; store the balance, IX += 32

$86CE c RowStart ; Start a row: beeper toggle, attributes, then the row's span nodes
> Toggles the beeper (RowBeep $7F7E XOR $18, AND the mask $7F7F, OUT $FE), 18 times a pass: the row part of the explosion noise. The mask is 0 (no sound) except for the 16 passes after an explosion or a big enemy's hit (SoundPattern1 $7F80).
> A' = ShiftPage ($7F6A). POP DE = the map ring pointer (+0), HL = the attribute address of column 31 (+2), BC = the screen address of the row (+4), IY = the head node (+6); saves SP (the record + 8) in RowStar's operand.
> SP = the attribute address + 1, then 16 PUSH DE of map bytes read downwards (DEC L wraps in the ring page): the 32 attributes of the row land in one go. A = C = the screen low byte (column 0), C = $FF. SP = IY: RET into the head node.
> 48K c2: 11862 entries.
> On a 128K: the OUT ($FE),A at $86D7 is made NOP: NOP: the row part of the explosion noise is gone (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/13-differences-from-zynaps-48k.md).

$873E c SpanDraw4 ; Span node handler: a run of four glyphs
> Entered by RET with SP = node + 2. POP HL: L = the first glyph, H = the gap. A (the column) += gap, DE = the screen address, H = ShiftPage (A'): HL = the glyph's line 0.
> Eight lines, the four bytes copied LDI, LDI, LDI, LD/INC on even lines and LDD back on odd lines (zigzag), INC H / INC D a line. Leaves A = the column after the run; POP HL = the next node, SP = it, RET.
> The top cost of the game: 48K c2 18726 entries, 123 per mille of all instructions.
$8797 c SpanDraw3 ; Span node handler: a run of three glyphs
> As SpanDraw4 with three bytes a line. 48K c2: 5790 entries.
$87E0 c SpanDraw2 ; Span node handler: a run of two glyphs
> As SpanDraw4 with two bytes a line. 48K c2: 2306 entries.
$8819 c SpanDraw1 ; Span node handler: one glyph
> As SpanDraw4 with one byte a line (LD A,(HL); LD (DE),A). 48K c2: 5328 entries.
$8842 c SpanDraw0 ; Span node handler: an empty node
> A node whose last glyph has scrolled out but which still links the row: skips the glyph/gap word and returns into the next node. 48K c2: 522 entries.

@module stars ; The star field: one star per playfield row
@about Each playfield row carries one star in its record (+$18-+$1F): a random speed and position, a colour of $44-$47 (bright green paper bits XOR random), drawn by RowStar. The stars move only while the playfield scrolls (PlayFlags $81DA bit 5) and are shown only when PlayFlags bit 7 is set.

$8846 c InitStars ; Give each of the 18 rows a random star
> Walks the records from the top with SP at record + $20 and pushes, per row: +$1E/+$1F = F and the colour ($47 XOR Random AND 3), +$1C/+$1D = L the row's screen low byte ($20, $40, ...) and H = $20, +$1A/+$1B = the speed (B = 16-47 from Random, C = $E0), +$18/+$19 = E (Random bits 3-6 moved to 4-7, OR $80) and D = $FF.
> MoveStars rewrites the fields every pass: +$1C = the cell's low byte and +$1D = the dot byte that RowStar draws, +$1E carry = visible.
> Saves and restores SP through the operand of InitStarsDone. Output: all registers changed.
> Called by LevelSetUp ($8D30) and StarsSwitch.
$885A InitStarLoop
$8880 InitStarsDone ; LD SP,n: n = the caller's SP

$8884 c StarsSwitch ; Turn the stars on (A = 1) or off (A = other) and restart them
> LD HL,$81DA; DEC A; set (A = 1) or reset PlayFlags bit 7; CALL InitStars. Not entered in 48K c1 or 48K c2: no caller found in the image (a leftover or a cheat entry).

$888E StarsOn ; SET 7: the stars shown
$8890 StarsRestart

$8894 c MoveStars ; Move every star and respawn the ones that left the screen
> Writes the opcode of the dot-making instruction: XOR $F8 gives SET n,B (PlayFlags bit 7 set: stars shown), XOR $B8 gives RES n,B (no dot: the stars stay invisible). Saves SP into MoveStarsDone.
> Per row (SP = the star fields): while scrolling (PlayFlags bit 5) the 16-bit position += speed (wrapping); a position below $1000 respawns the star (new random colour and speed). The pixel bit within the byte comes from the position's low bits (written into StarDot's opcode), the screen byte from its high bits.
> Called by the frame handler (48K c2: 659 entries, 37 per mille).
$889F StarsShownMode ; store the XOR value into StarDotOpcode's operand
$88B5 MoveStarLoop
$88C2 StarCheckEdge ; off the left edge?
$88E7 StarPlace
$88F4 StarDotOpcode ; XOR n: n = $F8 (SET) or $B8 (RES), written at $889F
$88F9 StarDot ; SET n,B: the bit number written at $88F6
$890E MoveStarsDone ; LD SP,n: n = the caller's SP

@module scroll ; Scrolling: glyph allocation, the column builder and the scroll step
@about The playfield scrolls left two pixels a step. The map ring at $B700-$BFFF (9 pages, two character rows a page, row pointers $80 apart) holds the scenery tile codes and attributes; ScrollColumn ($801C) is the leading column.
@about When a new column enters on the right, the column builder turns each map cell into a glyph: it reads the tile's 8 lines from the tile set ($5C00, line k of tile n at $5C00 + k*256 + n; +$700 for the upside-down copy), mirrors it through BitReverse ($FB00) when asked, and writes the four shifted copies into the glyph cache, each combined with the glyph on its left (ADD HL,HL twice per two pixels).
@about Glyph slots are tracked by the 256-bit map GlyphBitmap ($FFE0-$FFFF); span nodes come from the pool at $F800 (95 nodes of 8 bytes, free list NodeFreeList $7F68). The scroll step retires the leftmost glyph of every row, frees its slot and node, and links the new ones.

$8912 c AllocGlyphs ; Find C free glyph slots in a row of the glyph bitmap
> Starting from the last position (GlyphAllocMask $8015 = the slot index, $8016/$8017 = the bitmap byte and the bit mask), scans GlyphBitmap $FFE0-$FFFF for a clear bit, wrapping to $FFE0 and rotating the mask. When the run of C slots would pass slot 255 it restarts at slot 0.
> Output: E = the first slot (the glyph index), H/L = its bit mask and bitmap address, D = $C0; the allocator state advanced by C.
> Called by NewSpanNode (48K c2: 307 entries).
$891C AllocScan
$892C AllocFound
$893D AllocStore
$8945 AllocWrap ; restart at slot 0

$894D c StartColumn ; Set up the column builder for a new column
> Returns at once unless PlayFlags bit 5 (scrolling) is set. Writes the builder's operands: IX = $F34C (the record of row 17), HL = ScrollColumn + $880 (row 17's cell 8 pages on, + $80), 18 rows to go.
> Called from the frame handler cycle (48K c2: 229 entries, one per scroll step).

$896D c BuildColumn ; Build up to six cells of the new column, bottom row first
> Spreads the 18 rows of a column over three frames: B = 6 rows a call. Per row calls BuildCell, then IX -= 32 (the record above), L += $80 and H - 1 every second row (two rows a ring page).
> The operands keep the state between calls: the record (IX), the map pointer (HL) and the rows left (C). Returns at once with C = 0.
> 48K c2: 688 entries.
$8971 BuildColumnCell ; LD HL,n: the map cell of the next row
$8974 BuildColumnLeft ; LD BC,$0600+n: n = the rows left
$897A BuildColumnLoop
$897E BuildColumnBack ; back from BuildCell: the cell above
$8987 BuildColumnStep ; L = the next cell row
$8990 BuildColumnNext
$8999 BuildColumnSave

$899E c BuildCell ; Turn one map cell of the new column into a glyph
> A = the cell's tile code. 0 (empty): when the row's previous column had a tile (+$14 bit 7) the gap starts and the following cell's flag byte is set to "no attribute" (bit 7 set, bit 2 clear) when it is empty too; otherwise the empty-column count +$13 grows (up to 32).
> A tile: BuildGlyph. Input: IX = the row record, HL = the map cell. 48K c2: 3948 entries.
$89B1 CellGapStart
$89C0 CellGapDone

$89C4 BuildCellDone ; the cell built: clear its pending bit
$89C9 c BuildGlyph ; Build one glyph of the new column in all four shifts
> The left neighbour is the row's last glyph (+$0F) when the previous column had a tile (+$14 bit 7), else a blank line of the tile set. Takes a new span node and glyph run when the current run is used up (+$12, NewSpanNode).
> The cell's flag byte (the map byte at the row pointer + 1): bit 0 = upside down (source + $700 and DEC D instead of INC D at GlyphLineStep), bit 1 = mirrored (each line through BitReverse $FB00: the LD A,(HL) at GlyphMirror, else NOP).
> Writes the 8 lines at shift 0 (page $C0 + line), shift 2 (SET 3,D), shift 4 (SET 4,D) and shift 6 (RES 3,D), each the 16-bit pair left:new shifted left 2, 4, 6 pixels. Sets the slot's bit in GlyphBitmap and +$14 bit 6 (glyph added: the scroll step widens the tail node).
> 48K c2: 1025 entries.
$89DC GlyphHaveLeft
$8A08 GlyphNoFlip
$8A15 GlyphNoMirror
$8A1C GlyphLine
$8A1D GlyphLineStep ; INC D or DEC D (written at $89F4, $8A05): down or up the source tile
$8A1F GlyphMirror ; LD A,(HL) (mirror through $FB00) or NOP, written at $89F8, $8A0F
$8A25 GlyphShift2 ; the copy shifted 2 pixels
$8A34 GlyphShift4 ; the copy shifted 4 pixels
$8A46 GlyphMarkBits ; set the slot bits in the bitmap $FFE0
$8A51 GlyphMarkSlot

$8A59 GlyphBuilt
$8A5A c NewSpanNode ; Link a new span node at the row's tail and reserve its glyph run
> Takes a node from the pool (PopNode), links it after the tail (+$0D/+$0E), sets its gap = the empty-column count (+$13), width 0, handler SpanDraw0, counts it (+$0A), resets +$13 to 1. The link store in the previous node is done with interrupts off (DI unless the frame phase $7F65 is 4).
> Measures the run of tiles that follows in the map (up to 4) into +$12 and reserves as many glyph slots (AllocGlyphs); the first glyph index goes to the node (+2), the slot mask and address to +$10/+$11.
> 48K c2: 307 entries.
$8AA3 RunMeasure
$8AA7 RunMeasureLoop
$8AAC RunMeasureNext
$8AB2 RunMeasureGap
$8ABA RunStore

$8AD0 c PopNode ; Take a span node from the free list
> Output: DE = the node, NodeFreeList ($7F68) = the node's link. HL preserved. 48K c2: 307 entries.

$8ADF c PushNode ; Return the span node HL to the free list
> The node's first word = the old NodeFreeList, NodeFreeList = HL. DE preserved. 48K c2: 230 entries.

$8AED c ScrollStep ; Scroll the playfield model one step (two pixels) left
> Returns unless PlayFlags bit 5. ScrollCount ($81DB) + 1, ScriptRoom ($801F) + 1, EmptyRows ($7F70) = 0.
> Per row record (IX from $F12C, C = 18): an empty row counts in EmptyRows. Else the head node's gap - 1; when it goes negative the leftmost glyph leaves: its bitmap bit is freed (+$0B/+$0C), the glyph count +8 - 1 and the node width - 1; a node of width 0 is unlinked and returned to the pool (PushNode), and when the row loses its last node the tail is reset to the record (+$0D = IX + 2). The handler follows the width (SetSpanHandler).
> When the column builder added a glyph (+$14 bit 6) the tail node grows by one.
> The new leading attribute: the map byte at the row pointer + 1 is a flag byte; bit 2 set: bit 7 set = paper and bright from it, ink from the previous cell; bit 7 clear = ink from bits 3-5 and bright from bit 6, paper from the previous cell; bit 2 clear: bit 7 set = 0 (black), bit 7 clear = the previous cell's attribute. The pointer + 1.
> Then ScrollColumn + 1, $8022 + 1, the column 31 places ahead (the cell (ScrollColumn + $1F) AND $3F in all four quarters of the 9 ring pages) cleared, and a delay of EmptyRows - 9 units that pads the step's time.
> Called from the frame handler cycle (48K c2: 228 entries).
$8B08 ScrollRow
$8B0F ScrollRowHead
$8B35 FreeMaskShift
$8B3D GlyphLeaves
$8B55 GlyphLeftFreed
$8B84 NodeNarrowed
$8B8A TailGrow
$8BA8 ScrollRowEmpty
$8BAC LeadAttribute ; the new leading attribute from the flag byte
$8BC5 LeadInk
$8BCE LeadPaper
$8BD4 LeadNoAttribute
$8BDB LeadKeep
$8BDC LeadStore
$8BFE ClearAheadPage
$8C14 ScrollPad ; B = $14 a step (the Pentagon timing: $13 in the 48K)
$8C16 ScrollPadWait

$8C1C c SetSpanHandler ; Point the node IY at the handler for its width
> Handler = SpanHandlers[(IY+6)] into the node's first word. 48K c2: 1559 entries.

$8C31 w SpanHandlers ; The span handler of each run width 0-4
> SpanDraw0, SpanDraw1, SpanDraw2, SpanDraw3, SpanDraw4.

@module levels ; The level set-up: the playfield model, the map ring and the per-level counters
@about LevelSetUp resets everything the playfield engine keeps between frames: the span node pool, the 18 row records, the map ring, the glyph bitmap, the mirror table, the level counters and the stars.

$8C3B c LevelSetUp ; Reset the playfield model at the start of a level or life
> Chains the 95 nodes of the pool $F800 (the free list in $7F68, $FAF8 = $FFFF the end), clears the 576 bytes of RowRecords ($F12C-$F36B) and fills each of the 18 records: +0/+1 the map pointer ($B71F, $B79F, $B81F, ... two rows a page), +2/+3 the attribute address of column 31 ($583F + 32 row), +4/+5 the screen address ($4020 ...), +6/+7 the head SentinelNode, +$0D/+$0E the tail = record + 2, +$13 = 32 empty columns, +$12 = 1.
> Clears the map ring $B700-$BFFF, ScrollColumn = $B760, $8022 = $B730, GlyphBitmap $FFE0-$FFFF = 0; builds BitReverse ($FB00: each byte with its bits reversed).
> Sets the six fields $8075 + 3k to $FF, $8072-$8074 = 5, 6, 3, clears $8087, $8024, $8025, $80BD, $80BE and the six bytes $7EFF + 16k; then, with interrupts off, calls $91C6 and InitStars.
> Called from the level start $AF49 (48K c2: 1 entry).
$8C44 ChainNodes
$8C78 InitRowRecord
$8CE9 BitReverseByte
$8CEB BitReverseBit
$8CFC InitLevelFields
$8D28 ClearObjectFlags

@module scenery ; The scenery scripts: a coroutine that writes the level's tiles into the map ring
@about The level's scenery is written by a script that runs as a coroutine on its own stack (top $FDFB): ResumeScript switches to it, YieldScript switches back. Every 16 scrolled columns (ScriptRoom $801F) the script runs until it has placed one group of objects 16 columns further on (ScriptColumn $801E).
@about A script (the word table $72E1 indexed by ScriptNumber $81DD) is a byte list: below $80 = draw the object group n (the word table $749A) and yield; $80 n = call script n; $A0-$AF = the variant mask ($8021) for the groups that follow; $81-$9F = copy the 8-byte cell pattern n ($72A9 + 8n) to WorkCell ($FFD8); $FF = end.

$8D35 c StartScript ; Set up the scenery coroutine for a level
> Script stack top $FDFB holds the entry ScriptMain, ScriptSP ($801A) = $FDFB, ScriptBusy ($8020) = 0; falls into ResumeScript. Called from the level start $AF49 (48K c2: 1 entry).

$8D45 c ResumeScript ; Run the scenery script when 16 more columns have scrolled
> When ScriptBusy is 0: returns unless ScriptRoom >= 16; then ScriptRoom - 16, ScriptColumn + 16, ScriptBusy = $FF. Saves the main SP ($8018), switches to ScriptSP and RETs into the script; it returns here through YieldScript.
> Called from the frame handler cycle $AA96 (48K c2: 524 entries) and from the catch-up loop $AF90/$AF96.
$8D60 SwitchToScript

$8D69 c YieldScript ; Switch from the script back to the main stack
> ScriptSP = SP, SP = the main SP: the RET returns to ResumeScript's caller. 48K c2: 90 entries.

$8D72 c ScriptMain ; The coroutine's body: run the level's script for ever
> A = ScriptNumber ($81DD), RunScript, again. Its first RET came from StartScript; 48K c2 shows no entry because the loop never ends during a level (1 dispatch at the start).

$8D78 ScriptMainAgain ; the main script ended: run it again
$8D7A c RunScript ; Run the script A
> HL = the script's address from the word table $72E1, then ScriptStep. 48K c2: 4 entries (the main script and its sub-scripts).

$8D86 c ScriptStep ; Interpret the script bytes at HL
> Below $80: DrawGroup n, ScriptBusy = 0, YieldScript, go on. $FF: return. $80 n: call script n (ScriptVariant saved and restored, ScriptReturn). $A0-$AF: ScriptVariant = the low nibble moved to the high nibble. Other $81-$9F: ScriptSetCell (the pattern n AND 7).
$8D9A ScriptCommand
$8DB0 ScriptPattern
$8DB9 ScriptCall
$8DC3 ScriptReturn ; back from a sub-script: the variant restored

$8DCA c DrawGroup ; Place the object group A into the map ring
> Returns at once when SkipDraw ($7F76) bit 0 is set (the catch-up after a life lost runs the scripts without drawing).
> The group (word table $749A) is a list: a byte with bit 7 set and not $FF = a sub-group (the next byte) taken only when its bit 6 AND ScriptVariant is non-zero (recursion); $FF = end; otherwise an object of 3 bytes: the first byte (bits 0-4 the row, bits 5-7 the flags: bit 0 = flipped, bit 1 = mirrored), a variant/column byte (low nibble the column; ANDed with ScriptVariant), the shape number (the word table $7783).
> For each object the shape's cells are written column by column (PlaceColumn), with the direction of the row and column steps written into $8E94, $8EBF and $8EC0 for the flip and mirror, then YieldScript after every column, so the placement spreads over the frames.
> 48K c2: 15 entries.
$8DDB GroupNext
$8DF4 GroupObject
$8E0C PlaceObject
$8E2E ObjectDirections
$8E47 ObjectRowClipped
$8E4A ObjectRowFlipped
$8E54 ObjectStart
$8E75 PlaceObjectColumn
$8E87 PlaceObjectColumnB
$8E94 ObjectRowStep ; INC D or DEC D (written at $8E3C, $8E50)
$8E99 ObjectPlaced

$8EA1 c PlaceColumn ; Write one shape column into the map ring
> The column's header byte: bit 0 set = an attribute follows from the table at H = $FF (OR with the flags at PlaceFlags, stored into PlaceAttr). Then per cell a bit of the mask byte: set = the tile code goes to (DE) and the attribute to (BC); E and C step by PlaceStepE/PlaceStepC (INC or DEC, written by DrawGroup). A 0 bit ends the column.
> 48K c2: 209 entries.
$8EAC PlaceFlags ; OR n: n = the object's flip/mirror bits (written at $8DFD)
$8EB3 PlaceCell
$8EBA PlaceAttr ; LD A,n: n = the column's attribute (written at $8EAE)
$8EBE PlaceCellNext
$8EBF PlaceStepE ; INC E or DEC E (written at $8E2E)
$8EC0 PlaceStepC ; INC C or DEC C (written at $8E33)

$8EC4 b PlaceColumnSpare ; A RET byte after the loop's JP: never reached

$8EC5 c ScriptSetCell ; Copy the 8-byte cell pattern A to WorkCell
> HL = $72A9 + 8A, 8 bytes to $FFD8. 48K c2: 1 entry.

@module sprites ; Sprite builders and the per-row sprite strips of the objects
@about The objects (16 of 44 bytes, $F36C-$F5FF, walked from $F600 down) are not drawn on their own: BuildObjectStrips cuts every visible object into one strip per character row and links the strips into the row records, and the renderer draws them with the scenery (StripDraw).
@about The builders below prepare the sprite images the strips read: complemented 8-byte cells with blank cells between the columns, built at run time from the glyph data.

$8EDB c BuildCellSprite ; Build a column-major sprite of C columns of B cells, complemented, with blank borders
> DE - 8 gets a blank (FillBlankCell), then per column B cells of 8 bytes copied from HL complemented (CPL), and a blank cell after every column; then B blank cells at the end.
> Input: HL = the source glyphs, DE = the destination, B = the cells a column, C = the columns. 48K c2: 5 entries (frames 102-1274, from $ACD9), writing $E748-$E757 and $E760-$E76F from $64E0-$64FF and $65E0-$65FF.
$8EE5 CellSpriteColumn
$8EE6 CellSpriteCell
$8EE9 CellSpriteByte
$8EFA CellSpriteTail

$8EFF c FillBlankCell ; Write a blank cell: eight $FF bytes at DE
> A and BC preserved. 48K c2: 30 entries.
$8F05 FillBlankByte

$8F0C c BuildBigSprite ; Build a 7 x 9 cell image at $C000 from a one-bit cell map
> A = 1: the glyphs come from $66E0 (linear, INC HL) and the cell map from the 9 bytes at $8098; otherwise from the tile set at $5CDC (interleaved: INC H, then LD H,C; INC L after each glyph) and the map at $80A1. The step instructions are written into BigSpriteStep and BigSpriteNext. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 runs it 6 times (frames 16855-66273), from LoadCellSprite $ACD9 for the big enemies (kinds $14 and up).
> Each map bit (7 a byte, 9 bytes) set = the next glyph complemented (BigSpriteCell), clear = a blank cell. Called from $ACD9 when its A >= $14. Not entered in 48K c1 or 48K c2: probably the end-of-level guardian.
$8F23 BigSpriteFromTiles
$8F2F BigSpriteSetUp
$8F38 BigSpriteRow
$8F3C BigSpriteBit

$8F4B c BigSpriteCell ; Copy one glyph complemented to DE
$8F4F BigSpriteByte
$8F52 BigSpriteStep ; INC HL or INC H (written at $8F2F)
$8F56 BigSpriteNext ; NOP NOP or LD H,C; INC L (written at $8F32)

$8F5A c RotateSprite ; Rotate a column-major image one pixel right, the last bit wrapping to the first byte
> Input: HL = the image, C = its height in lines (also the column stride), B = its width in bytes. Each line is shifted right one pixel across its B bytes; the bit that falls out of the last byte goes into bit 7 of the first.
> Called from $AD44 (48K c2: 35 entries).
$8F5B RotateSpriteRows
$8F5D RotateLine
$8F5E RotateLineSave
$8F5F RotateLineCarry ; the carry in: 0
$8F60 RotateByte
$8F61 RotateByteShift
$8F63 RotateByteOut
$8F65 RotateNextByte

$8F67 RotateLineWrap ; the bit out of the last byte goes into the first
$8F68 RotateLineMerge
$8F69 RotateLineStore
$8F6A RotateLineNext
$8F6B RotateLineRestore
$8F6C RotateLineCount
$8F70 c ClearStripCounts ; Clear the sprite-strip count of the 18 row records
> 0 into +$15 of every record ($F141 + 32k). 48K c2: 660 entries (from ObjectStrips).

$8F9B c ObjectStrips ; Cut every active object into sprite strips
> ClearStripCounts, then the 16 objects from IY = $F600 down by 44 bytes: an active object (+8 bit 7) goes through BuildObjectStrips. One beeper toggle per object (ObjectBeep $7F82 XOR $18, AND the mask $7F83): the strip part of the explosion noise, silent while the mask is 0.
> Called from the frame handler cycle $AA96 (48K c2: 660 entries).
> On a 128K: the OUT ($FE),A at $8FBD is made NOP: NOP: the strip part of the explosion noise is gone (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/13-differences-from-zynaps-48k.md).
$8FA4 ObjectStripsLoop
$8FAF ObjectStripsNext

$8FC2 c BuildObjectStrips ; Link the strips of the object IY into the row records
> The object's sprite definition (+0/+1) is a list of words read with POP: the base image, the frame stride (times the frame +2), a stride and mask for the sub-frame (+3 bits 5-7), the height and the line length. The rows are clipped against the playfield (+4 - 16, 32 rows); an object fully out has +8 bit 5 cleared (BuildStripsOff), else it is set (visible).
> The column (+5 bits 5-7) moves the start; +6 gives the shift; +$25 the colour. The strip entries go onto the object's strip buffer (+9/+10, as a stack); +$0B is a bit mask of the rows the object covers: each set bit adds a strip to that row's list (+$15 count + 1, +$16/+$17 linked).
> Saves SP into BuildStripsDone. 48K c2: 3327 entries, 46 per mille.
$8FD4 StripFrame
$8FE4 StripSubFrame
$8FE7 StripClip
$8FFA StripClipTop
$9004 StripClipBottom
$9012 StripClipped
$9013 StripVisible
$9029 StripShift
$905B StripSkipRow
$9067 StripFirstRow
$906F StripLinkRow
$9085 StripLinkNext
$9091 StripListHead ; LD (n),SP: n = the row's list field (written at $908E)
$9095 StripRowDone
$90A4 BuildStripsDone ; LD SP,n: n = the caller's SP
$90AA BuildStripsOff ; the object is out of the playfield: not visible

@isub $897F LD DE,-32
@isub $8FAF LD DE,-44
@isub $8EDC LD HL,-8
# Fragment F2: $90B5-$A0EA, the object grid, the waves, the object loop and paths, the player and the weapons.
@module objgrid ; the free-slot search, the collision grid and the jump-table dispatch
@about The collision test of Zynaps is a bitmap grid rebuilt every other pass (marked in phases 1 and 3, tested and cleared in phases 2-4): ClearHitGrid zeroes $FC00-$FD8F, MarkEnemyGrid ORs the collision bit (+$24) of each of the 8 enemy slots ($F398-$F4CC) into the X bytes ($FC00, one byte a quarter column) and the Y bytes ($FD00, one byte an eighth of a row) the object covers, and TestHitGrid ORs those bytes back over the 8 friendly records (the player $F36C, the four laser shots $F4F8-$F57C, the two side-weapon slots $F5A8/$F5D4 and the sight $F600). The AND of the X union and the Y union is the set of enemies that overlap the record; it goes into the record's +$0D, and the union for the player and the active laser shots goes into $80BB, which every enemy handler tests with its own bit.
@about The grid routines walk the records and the grid with SP (POP is the fastest read on the Z80): each saves SP into the operand of its final LD SP,$0000 first and runs with interrupts on, so the interrupt pushes land in the record or grid bytes below SP; the game's interrupt handler is short and those bytes are rebuilt the next frame.
@about JumpTable ($91E2) is the dispatch every table-driven routine uses: HL a table of words, A the index; it jumps to the word, so the target's RET returns to the caller of JumpTable.
$90B5 c FindFreeObjectSlot ; find a free record among the 16 from $F36C (unused)
> IY = the first of the 16 records of 44 bytes from $F36C whose +$08 bit 7 (active) is clear, NC; carry when all 16 are in use. Nothing in the image calls or jumps here and no recording ran it (0 executions in 48K c1, 48K c2): a left-over; the wave launcher uses TakeEnemySlot ($938D).
$90BF FindFreeSlotLoop ; active: the next record
$90CA FindFreeSlotDone ; NC: IY free; carry: none
$90CB c MarkEnemyGrid ; mark the 8 enemy slots' collision bits in the grid
> Called from PlayPass $AA96 in the odd phases (1 and 3), every other pass (331 entries in 48K c2's 659 passes). C = the collision bit, 1 for the slot $F398 and rotated left for each next slot, so slot n owns bit n. For each active and visible slot (+$08 bits 7 and 5): its sprite descriptor (+0/+1, IX) gives the width +$0B and the height +$08; the X position (+3/+4, in 1/4 columns) picks the first of the X bytes at $FC00, every 4th byte over the width plus the last gets the bit; the Y position (+5/+6, in 1/8 rows) picks the Y byte at $FD00, every 8th byte over the height gets the bit, the rows whose bit in the row mask +$0B is clear excepted (a big enemy's rows out of play).
> Reads the records with SP (POP IX, POP HL); SP is kept in the operand at $9144.
$90DB MarkGridObject ; the next slot: skip unless active and visible
$90FD MarkGridColumns ; the bit into every 4th X byte, one a column of the width
$9124 MarkGridSkipRows ; leading rows outside the row mask: not marked
$912C MarkGridRowLoop ; the next row: in the mask?
$9130 MarkGridRow ; the bit into the Y byte of this row
$9138 MarkGridLastRow ; the last Y byte of the object
$913C MarkGridNextObject ; the next slot's bit
$9143 MarkGridRestoreSp ; SP back (the operand written at $90CB)
$9147 MarkGridRowOff ; a row outside the mask: step over it
$9150 c TestHitGrid ; the overlap masks of the player, the shots and the weapons
> Called from $A9ED in phases 2-4 (494 entries in 48K c2's 659 passes, 2 % of play's time). For the 8 records from $F36C (the player, then $F4F8 = $F36C + $018C and every 44 bytes on: the four laser shots, the plasma/missile slots $F5A8 and $F5D4, the sight $F600): OR of the X bytes over the width (the player's width less one) and OR of the Y bytes over the height (4 bytes an eighth of a row step, read with POP); the AND of both is the set of enemy bits this record touches, stored in +$0D. The union for the first five records (the player and the lasers), active ones only, goes into $80BB, the laser-hit mask the enemy handlers test. SP is kept in the operand at $91C3.
$915F TestGridObject ; the next record: the descriptor and its width
$916F TestGridWidth ; the X position into the X bytes at $FC00
$917D TestGridColumns ; OR of 4 X bytes a column
$9196 TestGridRows ; OR of the Y bytes over the height
$91A5 ; +$0D: the enemies this record overlaps
$91AB ; only the player and the four lasers count for $80BB, and only when active
$91B7 TestGridNext ; the next record: 44 bytes on ($018C for the first step)
$91BF ; $80BB: the hit mask of the player and the lasers
$91C2 TestGridRestoreSp ; SP back (the operand written at $9150)
$91C6 c ClearHitGrid ; clear the collision grid $FC00-$FD8F
> Pushes 200 zero words down from $FD90 (20 x 10 PUSH HL): the X bytes $FC00-$FCFF and the Y bytes $FD00-$FD8F. Called from $8C3B, $A9ED and $B03A (496 entries in 48K c2). SP is kept in the operand at $91DF.
$91D2 ClearGridLoop ; 10 words a pass
$91DE ClearGridRestoreSp ; SP back (the operand written at $91C6)
$91E2 c JumpTable ; jump to the word number A of the table at HL
> HL = the word at HL + 2A, JP (HL); DE is spoilt. The routine reached returns to JumpTable's caller. Used by the object loop ($80FA), the path interpreter ($8114, $8124), the player's side weapon ($80EA), the pod ($80F0) and the routines at $AC0E, $AD66, $ADF4, $B0FF and $B21C. 6080 entries in 48K c2.
@module waves ; the wave launcher and the sprite cache
@about LaunchWave starts one wave of enemies from the level tables (the data module): $6926 the wave number -> the wave index, $6943 two bytes an index (the wave record's bonus byte, then a stream offset), $6A65 eight bytes a kind (the template), $6B15 three bytes a level of the weapon rating ($80E9), $6800/$681C the path numbers -> the path bytecode. The 44-byte template at $803E is filled once and copied into each free enemy slot, with a starting delay that grows from object to object, its own path, its X and Y.
@about A wave record (3 bytes at $8075, $FF free) counts the wave's live objects; $8073 is the number of wave records the game allows at once (EnemyGone gives one back) and $8072 the free enemy slots (FreeEnemySlot gives one back; ScatterDebris zeroes it so nothing new arrives during the player's explosion).
@about The sprite cache: the sprite descriptor of a kind is one of 6 records of 16 bytes at $7EF2 (record 0, $7EE2, serves the kinds from $14 up without caching). A record's +$0D counts the objects using it, +$0E is the kind with bit 7, +$0F holds the record number in its low nibble; $8087 is the mask of records in use and $8088 a table that gives a free record for a mask. A new kind is queued in the ring at $8025/$8026 (8 entries of 3 bytes) for the renderer to build, flagged by bit 7 of $81C5.
$91EC c LaunchWave ; start wave A: the template, the wave record, the objects
> A = the wave number. Refused (NC) when no enemy slot is free ($8072 = 0) or no wave record is allowed ($8073 = 0); else $8073 - 1, the first free wave record at $8075 (first byte $FF) is taken with count 0 and its index kept in A'. $6926[A] = the index n; at $6943 + 2n: the bonus byte (record +1: bits 6-7 the drop type, bits 0-5 the bonus score), then the sprite kind (bit 7: all objects take the next byte as their path, else the kind itself) and the object count; the path numbers fill $806A.
> The template $803E is zeroed, its target (+$29) = the player $F36C; $6A65 + 8 x kind gives +$18, the speed level (combined with the weapon rating $80E9 to pick 3 bytes of $6B15 for +$19, +$20/+$21, +$22), the score index (+$0C) and the speed (+$17) nibbles, the X acceleration (+$10), the Y acceleration (+$14), the handler kind (+$07), the colour (+$25) and the hit points (+$0D). GetSpriteCache gives the descriptor (+0/+1), its +$0F goes to +$23 and its +$0C to the row mask +$0B; +$02 = 1, +$1A = the wave record.
> Then for each object: TakeEnemySlot, count + 1, the template copied over the slot (bytes +$09/+$0A and the slot's own collision bit +$24 kept), the target of the next object = this one, the path pointer from $6800/$681C, the start delay +$1B = D (each next object E frames later), active and visible, X and Y from the stream (a Y of $FF = a random row). At the end the descriptor's use count +$0D grows by the objects launched, the border/speaker gets OUT ($FE),0, carry. 9 entries in 48K c2.
$9207 LaunchWaveFindRecord ; the first wave record with $FF
$9212 LaunchWaveRecord ; count 0; its index into A'
$923E LaunchWaveFillSame ; bit 7: B copies of the next byte
$9245 LaunchWaveFillKind ; bit 7 clear: B copies of the kind
$9249 LaunchWaveTemplate ; zero the 44-byte template at $803E, target = the player
$92BF ; the sprite descriptor of the kind
$92CD ; DE = -3: the descriptor's +$0C, the row mask
$92F0 LaunchWaveObject ; one object: a free slot, the template, the path, the position
$930A ; the template's first 9 bytes
$9313 ; the bytes +$0B-$2B (+$09/+$0A kept)
$931A LaunchWavePathList ; the operand: the next path number in $806A
$9362 LaunchWaveYOffset ; Y from the stream plus the stagger
$9363 LaunchWaveSetY ; Y: the integer row (+6) and the fraction (+5)
$9375 LaunchWaveDone ; the descriptor's use count + the objects launched
$9382 ; OUT ($FE),0: speaker and border off
$9386 LaunchWaveNoSlot ; no free slot left: end early
$9389 LaunchWaveRefused ; NC: nothing launched
$938D c TakeEnemySlot ; take one free enemy slot from $F398 on
> NC (Z) when $8072, the free enemy slots, is 0; else $8072 - 1 and IY = the first record from $F398 whose +$08 bit 7 is clear, carry. The count guarantees one is found. 25 entries in 48K c2.
$939B TakeEnemySlotLoop ; active: the next record
$93A5 TakeEnemySlotFound ; carry: IY free
$93A7 c GetSpriteCache ; the sprite descriptor of kind A, cached or queued
> A = the kind. From $14 up: HL = $7EE2 (record 0) with a load queued each time. Else the 6 records from $7EF2 are searched for a used one (+$0D not 0) of this kind (+$0E & $7F); found: HL = that record. Not found: a free record is picked from $8088 by the use mask $8087 (the low nibble, the high one for kinds with bit 4), the least used record evicted (EvictSpriteCache) when none is free; the record is queued for building (QueueSpriteLoad), +$0E = kind | $80, +$0F + $10, and its bit set in $8087. 7 entries in 48K c2.
$93B3 SpriteCacheSearch ; 6 records, 16 bytes apart
$93BB SpriteCacheSearchLoop ; used and of this kind?
$93CE SpriteCacheNext ; the next record
$93D2 SpriteCacheAllocate ; a free record number for the use mask $8087
$93E5 SpriteCacheNibble ; 0: none free, evict one and look again
$940A c SpriteCacheRecord ; HL = $7EE2 + 16 x B
$9410 SpriteCacheRecordLoop ; B steps of 16
$9413 SpriteCacheRet ; HL the record
$9414 c EvictSpriteCache ; free the least used sprite-cache record
> The used record with the smallest use count +$0D among the 6 from $7EF2 (read at $7EFF + 16 n) is cleared and its number (+$0F low nibble) removed from the use mask $8087. Not run in 48K c2.
$9420 EvictSpriteCacheLoop ; smaller use count than the best so far?
$942B EvictSpriteCacheNext ; the next record
$942E EvictSpriteCachePtr ; the operand: the record chosen
$9441 c QueueSpriteLoad ; queue the sprite record DE of kind C for building
> The ring at $8026 (8 entries of 3 bytes: the kind, the record address), index $8025 + 1 mod 8; bit 7 of $81C5 tells the renderer a request is waiting. HL = the record on return. 5 entries in 48K c2.
@module movers ; the frame's object loop, the path interpreter and its movement commands
@about RunObjects is the object half of a frame: the player's handler, then the 15 slots from $F600 down to $F398 (the sight, the two side-weapon slots, the four laser shots, the eight enemy slots), each active one through the handler table $80FA by its kind (+$07): 0 PlayerHandler, 1 LaserHandler, 2 and 11 EnemyHandler, 3 $A1C8, 4 $A1FD (the pod), 5 $A263, 6 SightHandler, 7 SeekerHandler, 8 LockableHandler, 9 PlasmaHandler, 10 MissileHandler, 12 BigEnemyHandler.
@about The slot part of the explosion noise is made here: after each slot $7F86 is XORed with $18 and ANDed with the enable mask $7F87, then OUT ($FE): the speaker (bit 4) and the MIC (bit 3) flip 15 times a pass, a buzz whose pitch follows the time the handlers take; the mask is 0 (silence) except for the 16 passes after an explosion or a big enemy's hit (SoundPattern3 $7F88), so this is no engine hum; the border stays black (bits 0-2 are 0). When fewer than 8 slots are active the loop pads with (C - 7) x 128 DJNZ turns so the frame time, and the buzz, stay even.
@about The enemies move by a path bytecode (RunPath, kept in each record: +$1D/+$1E the pointer, +$1C the command, +$1B the frames left). A byte with bit 7 set gives the repeat count (b & $7F, 0 = forever) of the command byte after it; without it a command runs one frame. Bits 0-2 of a command pick the routine from $8114, bits 3-6 are its parameter (P): 0 PathOpAccelerate (P = the acceleration flags), 1 PathOpSetLoop, 2 PathOpSetInterval, 3 PathOpBounce, 4 PathOpHomePlayer, 5 PathOpSetDirection, 6 PathOpHomeTarget, 7 PathOpExtended, whose P picks one of 16 routines from $8124 (0 PathOpScroll, 1 PathOpStop, 2 PathOpRandomDirection, 3 PathOpLoop, 4 PathOpDriftMiddle, 5 PathOpAimPlayer, 6 PathOpScrollAlways, 7 PathOpMove, 8 PathOpHome, 9 PathOpToCentre, 10 PathOpLand, 11 PathOpRandomSpeed, 12 PathOpBounceArena, 13 PathOpDive, 14 PathOpMoveFragile, 15 PathOpWait).
@about A routine that returns carry is an instant command (a setting): the interpreter goes on to the next command in the same frame; NC ends the object's commands for this frame. The coordinates are 8.8: X (+3/+4) in columns of the playfield (16-47 on the screen), Y (+5/+6) in rows 0-15; the velocities +$0E/+$0F (X) and +$12/+$13 (Y) are added each move, the accelerations +$10/+$11 and +$14/+$15 to the velocities; +$16 is the direction 0-63 (SetVelocity), +$17 the speed.
$9463 c RunObjects ; run every active object's handler, with the slot toggle of the explosion noise
> Called once a pass from $AA96 (659 entries in 48K c2, 5.7 % of play's time without the handlers). The landing flag $974C is freed (bit 7), the player ($F36C) runs by its kind, then the 15 slots from $F600 down in steps of -44 (DE = $FFD4); C counts the slots not active.
> After the loop the per-frame hit masks are cleared: $80BB (lasers and player), $80E3 (the homing missile), $80E4 (the sight), $80E5 (plasma and seeker), the player's +$0D ($F379) and the +$0D of the 7 records from $F4F8 (lasers, side weapons, sight; $F505 on).
> On a 128K: the OUT ($FE),A at $949C is made NOP: NOP: the slot part of the explosion noise is gone (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/13-differences-from-zynaps-48k.md).
$947C RunObjectsLoop ; active? run its handler by +$07
$948E RunObjectsNext ; the next slot down, and the speaker click
$9493 ; the beeper: $7F86 ^= $18, AND the enable mask $7F87, OUT ($FE)
$94A0 ; 8 or more slots idle: pad the frame
$94A7 RunObjectsPad ; (C - 7) x 128 DJNZ turns
$94A9 RunObjectsPadLoop ; the delay loop
$94AE RunObjectsClear ; clear the frame's hit masks
$94C6 RunObjectsClearMasks ; +$0D of the 7 records from $F4F8
$94CB c RunPath ; run the path bytecode of the object IY for one frame
> Frames left +$1B - 1: 0 fetches the next command; a count that was already 0 (forever) stays 0. The command +$1C goes to JumpTable($8114) with C = the command: carry from the routine runs RunPath again (the next command in the same frame), NC ends it. Called by EnemyHandler, LockableHandler and BigEnemyHandler (1405 entries in 48K c2).
$94D6 RunPathCommand ; the current command
$94D9 RunPathDispatch ; bits 0-2: the routine from $8114
$94E6 RunPathFetch ; one frame unless a count byte comes
$94F0 RunPathFetchLoop ; bit 7: a repeat count
$94FD RunPathStore ; the command and the new pointer
$9509 c PathOpExtended ; command 7: bits 3-6 pick one of 16 routines from $8124
> 1151 executions in 48K c2.
$9516 c PathReadWord ; DE = the next word of the path
> Used by PathOpLoop for its jump target. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it: 180 entries.
$9529 c PathOpAccelerate ; command 0: accelerate by the flags in C, then move
> C bit 3: Vx += Ax; bit 4: Vx -= Ax; bit 5: Vy += Ay; bit 6: Vy -= Ay. Then X += Vx, Y += Vy; NC. Also the plain move of the player (C from the joystick), PathOpBounce, PathOpDriftMiddle, PathOpToCentre and PlasmaHandler (C = $20, gravity). 1918 executions in 48K c2.
$953C PathAccelXDown ; bit 4: Vx - Ax
$9549 PathAccelXStore ; the new Vx
$954F PathMoveX ; X + Vx
$956F PathAccelYDown ; bit 6: Vy - Ay
$957C PathAccelYStore ; the new Vy
$9582 PathMoveY ; Y + Vy; NC
$9591 c PathOpSetLoop ; command 1: the loop counter +$1F = P
> Carry: an instant command. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$959C c PathOpSetInterval ; command 2: the homing interval +$27 = +$28 = P
> Carry. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$95AA c PathOpBounce ; command 3: move, bouncing off the scenery
> With scenery under the object (SceneryAt6): only X moves. Else Y moves with the Y acceleration added (C = $20, a fall); if that lands it on the scenery or below row 15 the move is undone and Vy reversed (a bounce). NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$95C4 PathBounceY ; free: fall by Vy + Ay
$95E2 PathBounceTest ; hit: Y back, Vy = -Vy
$95FC c PathOpHomePlayer ; command 4: home on the player, turn rate P
> The target +$29/+$2A = the player $F36C, the turn rate +$2B = P, then PathOpHome. NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it: 1 entries.
$9605 PathHomeSetRate ; +$2B = P, one homing step
$9613 c PathOpSetDirection ; command 5: direction (C & $78) / 2, then move
> +$16 = P x 4 (0-60), velocities from the speed +$17 (SetVelocity), one move; NC. 20 executions in 48K c2.
$9625 c PathOpHomeTarget ; command 6: home on the target, the player once it is gone
> The target record +$29: no longer active, or of kind 3, makes it PathOpHomePlayer; else turn rate P and PathOpHome. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$9640 c PathOpScroll ; extended 0: move with the landscape when it scrolls
> Bit 0 of $7F6C set (a frame the playfield scrolls one step): X - $40 (a quarter column); NC. 251 executions in 48K c2: the ground objects.
$9658 c PathOpStop ; extended 1: stop
> Vx = Vy = 0; the flags of XOR A: NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9666 c PathOpRandomDirection ; extended 2: a random direction, then move
> +$16 = random & $3F, the velocities for the speed +$17, one move; NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$9679 c PathOpLoop ; extended 3: loop back to the word target while +$1F counts down
> Reads the target word; +$1F - 1: not 0 = the path pointer goes to the target; carry either way (the next command runs at once). Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$9689 c PathOpDriftMiddle ; extended 4: accelerate towards row 8
> Above row 8: C = $20 (Vy + Ay), else $40 (Vy - Ay); PathOpAccelerate; NC. 1071 executions in 48K c2.
$9694 PathDriftMove ; accelerate and move
$9699 c PathOpAimPlayer ; extended 5: the direction to the player
> +$16 = DirectionTo the player, velocities for the speed; carry. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$96A8 c PathOpMove ; extended 7: X += Vx, Y += Vy
> NC. Also the move of SetDirection, RandomDirection, PathOpHome and MoveFragile. 38 executions in 48K c2.
$96D0 c PathOpScrollAlways ; extended 6: X - $40 every pass
> NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$96E2 c PathOpHome ; extended 8: turn towards the target, then move
> Every +$28 frames (+$27 counts down): the direction to the target (+$29/+$2A) by DirectionTo; a difference up to the turn rate +$2B is taken at once, else the direction turns by +$2B the shorter way round (mod 64), and SetVelocity for the speed +$17. Then PathOpMove; NC. 18 executions in 48K c2; the homing missile and the seeker use it too.
$9708 PathHomeCompare ; |difference| within the turn rate?
$970E PathHomeTurn ; the shorter way: left or right
$971D PathHomeTurnRight ; + the turn rate
$971E PathHomeWrap ; mod 64
$9720 PathHomeSetDirection ; the new direction and velocities
$9729 PathHomeMove ; one move
$972E c PathOpToCentre ; extended 9: accelerate towards column 32 and row 8
> X below 32: C = 8 (Vx + Ax), else $10; Y below 8: $20, else $40; PathOpAccelerate; NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$9739 PathToCentreY ; the Y flag
$9745 PathToCentreMove ; accelerate and move
$974C b LandingFlag ; PathOpLand's per-frame flag and retry count
> Bit 7: an object has used PathOpLand in this pass (RunObjects clears it each pass); bits 0-6: the failed tries since the last landing.
$974D c PathOpLand ; extended 10: put the object on the ground below it
> One object a frame (bit 7 of LandingFlag). After 8 failed tries in a row, or when another object landed this frame, it is parked at X = 0, Y = 0 (off the playfield, it leaves). Else Y fraction = $E0, Y goes down out of any scenery, then on down to the first row with all 4 cells under it filled: Y one up there, the repeat count 1, the tries reset; a row that is filled only in part parks it. NC. 1 execution in 48K c2.
$9763 PathLandRise ; out of the scenery first
$9774 PathLandFall ; down a row: the 4 cells below
$97A9 PathLandPark ; X = 0: off the playfield
$97AD PathLandParkY ; Y = 0
$97B3 c PathOpRandomSpeed ; extended 11: the speed +$17 one up or down at random
> A random byte below $55 keeps it, below $AA + 1, else - 1; kept in 1-7 (0 refused). Carry. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$97C1 PathRandomSpeedSet ; a new speed of 0 is refused
$97CB PathRandomSpeedDone ; carry: instant
$97CD c PathOpBounceArena ; extended 12: move, bouncing off the scenery and the edges
> X + Vx: outside columns 16-45 or on scenery: X back, Vx = -Vx. Y + Vy: Y 17 or more (also a negative Y) or on scenery: Y back, Vy = -Vy. NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$97F0 PathArenaFlipX ; X back, Vx reversed
$9802 PathArenaMoveY ; Y + Vy
$9823 PathArenaFlipY ; Y back, Vy reversed
$9835 PathArenaDone ; NC
$9837 c PathOpDive ; extended 13: scroll along, dive at the player when close
> PathOpScroll, then, once its column is no more than the player's plus half the row distance between them and a random byte is below 200: one frame of Vy = -$80, Vx = -$40 (a dive up and back). Else on scenery: SelfDestruct. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$984F ; a random byte below 200 (78 %) dives
$986B PathDiveTest ; on the scenery?
$9874 c PathOpMoveFragile ; extended 14: move; destroyed on scenery or leaving the playfield
> PathOpMove; Y 17 or more, X outside 16-46, or scenery under it: SelfDestruct. NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$988B PathFragileDestroy ; out or hit
$9890 c PathOpWait ; extended 15: do nothing this frame
> NC. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$9892 c SelfDestruct ; mark the object as hit by the player's laser
> +$0D = 1 (one hit point) and its bit (+$24) into $80BB: the enemy handler's laser test kills it this frame. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it: 36 entries.
$989F c DirectionTo ; A = the direction 0-63 from the object IY to IX
> The rounded column and row distances; their signs pick the octant, the ratio of the smaller to the larger (by subtraction in 8 steps) the direction inside it. Used by PathOpAimPlayer, PathOpHome and $A0EB. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it: 1431 entries: the homing interval of the objects there never ran out.
$98C5 DirectionToX ; the column distance's sign
$98D2 DirectionToOctant ; swap so the larger distance divides
$98DB DirectionToDivide ; the ratio in 8 steps
$98E7 DirectionToLoop ; one step
$98EE DirectionToDone ; the step XOR the octant
$98F1 c SetVelocity ; the velocities for direction A and speed B
> Vx = B x sine[A] and Vy = B x sine[(A - 16) & $3F] with the 64-entry signed sine table $8144 (B additions, so B is 1-7 in practice). 21 entries in 48K c2.
$9902 SetVelocityXLoop ; Vx = speed x sine
$9920 SetVelocityYLoop ; Vy = speed x the sine a quarter turn back
@module objutil ; the end of an enemy, the wave bonus, the scenery tests and the playfield clamps
@about An enemy leaves play in two ways: it flies off the playfield (EnemyEscaped) or it is destroyed (EnemyKilled). Both count the wave down; when the last object of a wave is gone the wave record is freed and $8073 lets the next wave in, and only a wave none of whose objects escaped pays its bonus (score index + 7 through $A489) and leaves its drop type in +$26 (bits 6-7 of the wave's bonus byte), which the explosion code turns into a pod.
@about The scenery is a map of one byte a cell, 128 cells a row, from the word $8022 (the scroll position): SceneryCell gives the cell under the object's top-left corner, the SceneryAt routines OR 1, 2, 4 or 6 cells (2 wide, 1 to 3 rows) and return carry when any is not 0.
$992A c FreeEnemySlot ; an enemy slot back: $8072 + 1, its bit out of $80BD/$80BE
> Its collision bit (+$24) is cleared from the masks $80BD and $80BE (the harmless enemies, set at $8D1A and by $A2C4/$A30E) and the slot deactivated. Used by the enemy handlers and $A1C8, $A1FD, $A2D6. 26 entries in 48K c2.
$9941 c FreeObject ; deactivate the object and $8074 + 1
> Used by $A263 (the kind 5 handler); $8074 is set at $8D0D and read at $A113. 1 entry in 48K c2.
$994A c EnemyEscaped ; the object left the playfield: count its wave down, no bonus
> C = $FF: the wave record gets bit 7 (an escape), so the wave pays no bonus. 7 entries in 48K c2.
$994E c EnemyKilled ; the object was destroyed: count its wave down
> +$26 = 0 (no drop), C = 0, then EnemyGone. 9 entries in 48K c2.
$9954 EnemyGone ; the sprite descriptor's use count - 1 when +$23 matches its +$0F
$9966 ; the use count - 1; 0: the sprite's record bit out of $8087
$9971 EnemyGoneWave ; the wave record $8075 + 3 x +$1A
$9984 EnemyGoneCount ; count - 1; 0: the wave is over
$9989 ; the wave is over: $8073 + 1, the record free ($FF); an escape (bit 7): no bonus
$99A5 ; the whole wave destroyed: the bonus score
$99A9 c SceneryAt1 ; carry if the cell under the object is not empty
> Used by PlasmaHandler, SeekerHandler and $A263. 18 entries in 48K c2.
$99B1 c SceneryAt2 ; carry if one of the 2 cells (X, X + 1) is not empty
> Used by LaserHandler (869 entries in 48K c2).
$99BB c SceneryAt4 ; carry if one of 2 x 2 cells is not empty
> Used by $A2D6. 2 entries in 48K c2.
$99CD SceneryAt4Row ; the second row
$99D5 c SceneryAt6 ; carry if one of 2 x 3 cells is not empty
> The ship's and most movers' test: rows Y, Y + 1, Y + 2 of columns X and X + 1 (a row is 128 bytes: + $80, with the carry into H on every second row). 1227 entries in 48K c2.
$99E4 SceneryAt6Row2 ; the second row (an entry point of the L1 recording: the interrupt returned here)
$99E7 SceneryAt6Row2Test ; the cells of row Y + 1
$99F7 SceneryAt6Row3 ; the cells of row Y + 2
$99FF c SceneryCell ; HL = the scenery cell of the object IY
> HL = ($8022) + Y x 128 + X with the integer parts +$06 and +$04. 2118 entries in 48K c2.
$9A13 c ClampRow ; keep the object in rows 0-15
> A negative row: Y = 0.0; 16 or more: Y = 15 + $E0/256. 2028 entries in 48K c2.
$9A23 ClampRowBottom ; 16 or more: the bottom row
$9A2F c ClampColumn ; keep the player in columns 16-45
> Below 16: X = 16.0; 46 or more: X = 45 + $E0/256. Only the player uses it (613 entries in 48K c2).
$9A40 ClampColumnRight ; the right edge
$9A4C c CycleColour ; the next ink colour 1-7 in +$25 (bits 3-5)
> The flash of the missiles, the plasma and the hit big enemy. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it: 291 entries.
$9A57 CycleColourSet ; 0 skipped
@module player ; the player's ship: movement, fuel, fire, hit and death
@about The ship is the record at $F36C, kind 0. Its weapon state lives in the player record $7F9C (pointer $7F99, the second player's at its own place): +$07 the pod selector 0-4 (the highlighted panel item), +$08 the side weapon (0 none, 2 plasma, 3 homing missiles, 4 seeker), +$09 the double plasma flag, +$0A the laser level 0-3, +$0B the speed $40-$A0 in steps of $20.
@about The fuel: $80E6 counts down in the passes whose PassBit $7F63 (rotated left each pass) has bit 0 set, one in 8 passes, so one step every 16 TV frames in play (measured: $54, $4A, $40 at 160-frame intervals); at 0 FuelOut sets it back to $5A and takes a laser level and a speed step away. A collected pod refills it ($BB with a speed-up, $B4 when the selector wraps), a missed pod halves it ($A25C). So the ship must keep scooping pods to keep its power: $5A steps, about 29 s, a level with nothing collected. Measured: $80E6 = 2 with laser 3 and speed $A0 gave $80E6 = $5A, laser 2 and speed $80 forty frames later (../zynaps/work/f2, play-imm.szx).
@about The death: a scenery hit or an enemy overlap (the enemies of $80BD/$80BE excepted) calls KillPlayer unless the byte at $FFD7 is not 0. That byte is 0 in the image and nothing writes it: a switch left for testing. Measured (../zynaps/work/f2, play-imm.szx, the ship put in the floor with Y = 14): $FFD7 = 0 starts the death timer ($7F7B = $12 ten frames later), $FFD7 = 1 keeps $7F7B = $FF, the ship immune. The death timer $7F7B ($FF while alive) counts $16 frames of explosion, then $7F7C = 1 tells the game loop.
$9A5B c PlayerHandler ; kind 0: the ship for one pass
> Not alive ($7F7B not $FF): PlayerDying. Else the fuel count; $A6B6; the input $8186 (bits 0/1 right/left, 2/3 down/up): up or down sets Vy = the speed - $20 at once, left or right accelerate X up to the speed + $20 (the acceleration masked at the limit), no horizontal input brakes Vx to 0. PathOpAccelerate moves, ClampRow and ClampColumn keep it on the screen, scenery under the ship undoes the row move (to the row's edge). A moving ship writes its position into the ring of 8 at $80C0 (index $80BF): the sight follows it from there.
> The colour +$25: $70, or the flashing $7F79 x 8 + $40 while fire has been held 8 passes or more ($8184, the pod-collect signal). The side weapon's frame routine from $80EA by +$08 - 2; a fire press ($8185 = $FF) FireLaser; then the hit test. 659 executions in 48K c2.
$9A6D ; the fuel, on the passes with PassBit bit 0 set (one in 8)
$9A74 PlayerInput ; the input bits
$9A7F ; no horizontal input: brake against the motion
$9A88 PlayerDrift ; Vx 0: nothing to brake
$9A8E PlayerNoDrift ; no brake flags
$9A90 PlayerVertical ; up/down: Vy = +-(speed - $20)
$9AAF PlayerHorizontal ; the X speed limit = speed + $20
$9AC9 PlayerSpeedLimit ; under the limit: accelerate
$9ACE PlayerMove ; the flags into bits 3-6, move, clamp, scenery
$9AE5 ; on the scenery: back to the old row
$9AEB PlayerHistory ; moving: X, Y into the ring at $80C0
$9B0F PlayerColour ; fire held 8 passes: flash
$9B20 PlayerWeapon ; the side weapon's frame routine
$9B34 PlayerFire ; a fire press: a laser shot
$9B3B ; the hit test: scenery under the ship
$9B40 ; or an enemy not of $80BD/$80BE in the overlap mask +$0D
$9B4C PlayerHit ; the test switch at $FFD7: not 0 = immune
$9B54 PlayerDying ; the death timer; at 0: $7F7C = 1
$9B5F c KillPlayer ; the ship destroyed: the timer and the explosion
> $7F7B = $16 (22 frames), all 16 records from $F36C deactivated, ScatterDebris. 2 executions in 48K c2.
$9B6C KillPlayerLoop ; +$08 bit 7 off in each record
$9B75 c WeaponRating ; the weapon rating $80E7-$80E9 for the enemy fire and the wave colours
> L = laser level + side weapon: $80E7 = $FF, $30 from 4; $80E9 = 0, 8 from 7; $80E8 = ((speed / 32 + L) x 4) & $F0 + ($81DE). LaunchWave adds $80E9 to a kind's colour select (the KindColours set); EnemyFire reads $80E7 (the first shot's column) and $80E8 (the fire chance), so a well armed, fast ship draws more fire. Called from $AC7A and $B03A (10 entries in 48K c2).
$9B89 WeaponRatingHigh ; 7 or more
$9B8F WeaponRatingStore ; the three values
$9BAB c ScatterDebris ; the 5 records from $F398 as debris around IY
> Each at a random column X - 1 + 0-3 and a random row near Y, active, +$26 = 0, $A293 (the explosion picture), ClampRow, +$27 = random 0-3; $8072 = 0, so no wave starts until they are gone. Used by KillPlayer and BigEnemyHandler. 2 entries in 48K c2.
> On a 128K: the LD ($8072),A at $9BFF becomes CALL HookShipLost ($B354): the store, then sounds $14 + $15 (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$9BC0 ScatterDebrisLoop ; one piece
$9C03 c FuelOut ; the fuel ran out: $80E6 = $5A, laser - 1, speed - $20
> The laser level +$0A of IX does not go below 0, the speed +$0B not below $40. Also called at $B119. 1 entry in 48K c2; measured, see the module notes.
$9C11 FuelOutSpeed ; the speed one step down, not below $40
$9C1D c FireLaser ; fire a laser shot from the ship
> $80BA counts the shots not in flight (4 at most, set at $B2D1; LaserHandler gives one back): a shot is fired only when $80BA + the laser level is 4 or more, so level 0 has one shot in the air, level 3 four. The first free of the 4 slots at $F4F8: X + 1 column, Y + $1E0/256 for two shots out of three, + $20/256 for the third (+$26 counts the guns); sound effect 2 ($B2F0); $7F77 = 6 when 0. 564 entries in 48K c2.
> On a 128K: its CALL RequestEffect at $9C77 (effect 2) becomes CALL HookShot ($B32F): the page-4 engine plays sound $0E (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$9C37 FireLaserFindSlot ; a free shot slot?
$9C42 FireLaserLaunch ; the gun: two low, one high
$9C5B FireLaserPlace ; the shot's position
$9C77 ; sound effect 2: the laser
@module weapons ; the side weapons, the pod actions, the sight, the laser shot, the plasma, the missile, the seeker
@about The side weapon fires from its frame routine (table $80EA, called by PlayerHandler with +$08 - 2) on a count of the frames fire is held ($8184), so a tap fires the laser only: plasma at 3 frames, a homing missile from 1 frame when a target is locked, the seeker at 7 frames. Slot $F5D4 is the side weapon's shot, $F5A8 the second plasma ball of the double plasma, $F600 the homing sight.
@about The pod actions (table $80F0, called by the pod handler $A1FD with the selector +$07 when the ship scoops a pod with fire held): 0 PodSpeedUp, 1 PodLaser, 2 PodPlasma, 3 PodHoming, 4 PodSeeker. A second plasma pod gives the double plasma.
@about The hit masks of a frame (cleared by RunObjects, read by the enemy handlers): $80BB the player and the lasers (one hit point a frame), $80E5 the plasma balls and the seeker (a kill), $80E3 the homing missile, $80E4 the sight. The homing lock: the first lockable enemy (kinds 8 and 12) under the sight is stored in $80E0 (its record) and $80E2 (its bit); the missile kills the locked enemy only.
$9C83 c PlasmaFrame ; side weapon 2: a plasma ball after 3 frames of fire
> Fire held exactly 3 frames: $F5D4 when free, else with the double plasma (+$09 = 1) $F5A8 when free; active, Vy = 0, 3 bounces (+$26), PlaceAtPlayer. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9CA1 PlasmaLaunch ; the ball into play
$9CB0 c PlaceAtPlayer ; the object IX two columns ahead of the ship, one row down
> Copies the ship's X (+2 columns) and Y (+1 row) from $F36F. Used by PlasmaFrame, HomingFrame, SeekerFrame. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
> On a 128K: its LD HL,$F36F becomes CALL HookSpecialLaunch ($B36C): the load, then sound $18 (every bomb, homing missile and seeker launch; r1 frame 1113) (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$9CCA c HomingFrame ; side weapon 3: a homing missile at the locked target
> Fire held, $F5D4 free and a lock ($80E2 not 0): active, the target +$29 = ($80E0), colour $38, direction 0 (ahead), life +$26 = $4B (75 frames), the turn interval 1. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
> On a 128K: the CALL $9CB0 at $9CFA becomes CALL HookHomingLaunch ($B35C): the launch, then sounds $1A + $1B (r2 frames 127 and 287) (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$9CFE c SeekerFrame ; side weapon 4: the seeker after 7 frames of fire
> Fire held exactly 7 frames and the weapon still 4: a new seeker at the ship flying ahead (direction 0, the speed +$17); an active one with +$26 = 0 is re-armed. +$26 = 1 and the target = the first enemy slot $F398: SeekerHandler hunts from there. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9D1F SeekerLaunch ; a new seeker: the position and velocities
$9D3E SeekerTargetFirst ; hunt from slot $F398
$9D4C c PodSpeedUp ; pod 0: fuel $BB and the speed one step up, to $A0
> Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9D5D c PodLaser ; pod 1: the laser one level up, to 3
> Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9D68 c PodPlasma ; pod 2: plasma (a second plasma pod: double)
> +$09 = 1 when the weapon is already plasma; +$08 = 2; the sight $F600 off; both slots $F5D4 and $F5A8 set up as plasma balls (ResetPlasmaBall). Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9D73 PodPlasmaSetup ; the weapon, the sight off, the two balls
$9D87 c ResetPlasmaBall ; the slot IX as an idle plasma ball
> Inactive, kind 9, Vx = $40, the X acceleration 0, the Y acceleration $10 (its gravity). Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9DA5 c PodHoming ; pod 3: homing missiles and the sight
> +$08 = 3, no double, the slot $F5A8 off; $F5D4 idle as kind 10 with speed 4, the turn interval 1 and the turn rate 2; the sight $F600 on (PlaceSight); no lock ($80E0 = 0, $80E2 = 0). Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9DDD c PodSeeker ; pod 4: the seeker
> The sight off, +$08 = 4, no double; $F5D4 idle as kind 7, speed 4, the turn interval 1, the turn rate 2. The second plasma slot $F5A8 is not cleared here (PodHoming does it). Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9E03 c SightHandler ; kind 6: the homing sight ahead of the ship
> Colour $78 without a lock, $70 with one; its overlap mask +$0D into $80E4 (the lockable enemies test it); then PlaceSight. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9E0D SightColour ; the colour and the mask
$9E16 c PlaceSight ; the sight at the ship's position of the history ring, 10 columns ahead
> The entry of the ring $80C0 at the index $80BF (the oldest one: the sight lags the ship by 8 moves) into $F603-$F606, X + 10 columns.
$9E32 c LaserHandler ; kind 1: a laser shot
> Gone on an enemy in its overlap mask (the harmless enemies of $80BD/$80BE and bits 5-7 excepted) or scenery (SceneryAt2), and at column 48: $80BA + 1 (the shot back). Else X + $140/256 (1.25 columns) a frame. 882 executions in 48K c2.
$9E57 LaserRemove ; off, the shot back to $80BA
$9E60 LaserStore ; the new X
$9E67 c PlasmaHandler ; kind 9: a plasma ball, falling and bouncing
> Flashes every second frame; its mask into $80E5 (a kill for any enemy it overlaps). Gone at column 48, below row 16 or after its bounces (+$26, 3). Each frame Vy + the gravity; a landing on scenery (SceneryAt1): sound effect 3, Vy = -Vy / 2, a bounce counted; still in the scenery after the bounce: gone. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
> On a 128K: the CALL $99A9 at $9EC2, after a bounce, becomes CALL HookBombBounce ($B33C): the call, then sound $11 (r1: 22 bounces from frame 1141) (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$9E71 PlasmaMove ; fall
$9EA5 ; sound effect 3: the bounce
$9EC6 PlasmaBounceDone ; out of the scenery and bounces left: stay
$9ECE PlasmaRemove ; off
$9ED3 PlasmaOffBottom ; below the playfield
$9ED7 c MissileHandler ; kind 10: the homing missile
> Life +$26 - 1, flashing. No lock: it burns out (MissileFade). Its mask into $80E3; overlapping the locked enemy ($80E2): gone (the enemy handler sees the hit in $80E3). Else PathOpHome on the target, ClampRow, gone outside columns 16-47. Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9EEC ; $80E3: the missile's overlap mask
$9EFF MissileExpire ; burn out
$9F03 MissileFade ; the colour one step down a frame; 0: gone
$9F0E MissileRemove ; off
$9F13 c SeekerHandler ; kind 7: the seeker, killing enemy after enemy
> Flashes. A target (+$29) that is active and of kind 2: PathOpHome on it, its mask into $80E5 (a kill), ClampRow, gone on scenery or outside columns 16-47. A lost target with +$26 not 0: the next active kind-2 slot after +$26 (up to 5) becomes the target, none: the player, +$26 = 0 (it comes home). The kind test is written twice (CP $02 ... CP $02): the second was probably meant for another kind (11 shares the handler of 2). Not run in 48K c1/c2.
$9F28 ; kind 2? (the same test again at $9F2C)
$9F30 SeekerHome ; home, kill, clamp
$9F4B SeekerRemove ; off
$9F50 SeekerRetarget ; the target is gone
$9F59 SeekerSearch ; the next slot number
$9F65 SeekerSearchSlot ; active and of kind 2?
$9F7A SeekerFound ; the new target
$9F7D SeekerSetTarget ; +$29/+$2A and the slot number +$26
@module enemies ; the enemy handlers: the common enemy, the lockable enemy, the big enemy
@about Every enemy handler: out of the columns of play (12-55 for the common and the lockable ones, 7-55 for the big one) the object escapes (EnemyEscaped, FreeEnemySlot); else RunPath, ClampRow and $A0EB (the animation and the aimed fire), then the hits: a bit in $80E5 (plasma, seeker) kills at once, a bit in $80BB (laser) takes one hit point (+$0D); a hit point count of $FF is invulnerable. At 0: EnemyKilled, the explosion ($A293) and the score of +$0C ($A489).
$9F88 c EnemyHandler ; kinds 2 and 11: a common enemy
> 1412 executions in 48K c2.
$9F94 EnemyLeaves ; out of the playfield: escaped
$9F9B EnemyMove ; path, clamp, $A0EB, hits
$9FBB EnemyDies ; destroyed: the wave, the explosion, the score
$9FC8 c LockableHandler ; kind 8: an enemy the homing sight can lock
> As EnemyHandler without the plasma kill; with no lock ($80E2 = 0) and its bit in the sight's mask $80E4 it becomes the lock ($80E0 = IY, $80E2 = its bit) and flashes ($7F79 x 8 + $40). The locked one dies of a missile hit ($80E3); lasers take hit points. Leaving play clears its lock bit. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$9FD4 LockableLeaves ; its lock bit off, escaped
$9FE4 LockableMove ; path, clamp, $A0EB, the lock
$A004 LockableFlash ; locked: flash
$A014 LockableHits ; a missile hit on the lock, or a laser hit
$A028 LockableDies ; destroyed: the lock off, the wave, the explosion, the score
$A03E c BigEnemyHandler ; kind 12: the big (boss) enemy
> Columns 7-55. Its row mask +$0B = $FE shifted left once per row below row 11 (its lower rows out of the collision grid near the floor). Lockable as kind 8; a missile hit takes 4 hit points and the lock, a laser hit one with a colour flash. Every hit sets the sound pointers $7F80 = $FD00, $7F84 = $EDB2, $7F88 = $D6ED (the hit noise). Below 0 hit points: EnemyKilled, X + 2, ScatterDebris (the debris of a big explosion) and the score. Not run in 48K c1/c2; 48K r1 (all levels) runs it.
$A04A BigEnemyLeaves ; the lock off, escaped
$A055 BigEnemyMove ; path, clamp
$A066 BigEnemyRowMask ; one row less for each row below 11
$A06A BigEnemyRowSet ; the mask, $A0EB, the lock
$A087 BigEnemyFlash ; locked: flash
$A097 BigEnemyHits ; a missile hit: - 4 and the lock off
$A0B0 BigEnemyLaser ; a laser hit: - 1 and a flash
$A0BD BigEnemyHitSound ; the hit sound; below 0: destroyed
@isub $92CD LD DE,-3
@isub $948E LD DE,-44
@isub $964C LD DE,-64
@isub $96D6 LD DE,-64
# F3: $A0EB-$AB12 (enemy fire and the object kinds 3-5, print, score, menu screens, input, high scores, panel
# spinners, the play interrupt and its pass scheduler)

@module objects ; Enemy fire, explosions, weapon pods and enemy shots
@about The per-kind handlers of the moving objects that are not enemies proper. An object record is 44 bytes (IY); +0/+1 points at its animation record ($7EA1 explosion, $7EBB pod), +2 is the animation frame, +3..+6 the X and Y (fixed point, high bytes the cell), +7 the kind, +8 flags, +$24 the object's bit in the per-pass masks, +$25 the colour, +$26/+$27 per-kind counters.
@about The handler of an object is found by its kind in a pointer table: kind 3 (explosion) is ExplosionStep (pointer at $8100), kind 4 (weapon pod) is PodStep ($8102), kind 5 (enemy shot) is EnemyShotStep ($8104). An enemy turns into an explosion with StartExplosion; an explosion with a pod to leave (+$26 not zero) turns into a pod with ExplosionToPod when its 9 frames are out.
@about EnemyFire is called by the enemy movers: it spends one of the three enemy shot records at $F474 when the enemy's fire rate wins against a random number, with a fixed, a random or an aimed direction (the player at $F36C).

$A0EB c EnemyFire ; Let the enemy at IY fire a shot
> IY: an enemy record. An enemy fires when its fire rate +$18 is not zero and either it has not fired yet (+8 bit 6 clear) and stands in the trigger column $80E7, or in a pass its bit +$24 selects ($7F63 PassBit) a random number (Random $8544) less $80E8 is below the rate.
> A shot costs one of the shots left in $8074 (none left: no shot) and takes the first free of the three shot records at $F474 (44 bytes each, +8 bit 7 the busy flag). It starts at the enemy's position + $0180 (1.5 cells right and down, Y clamped to row 15), takes its speed from +$19 (bits 0-2 the step, bits 3-7 the speed index), its colour from +$20, its lifetime from +$22 and its direction from +$21:
> +$21 bits 0-1 = 00: the fixed direction in bits 2-7; 10: a random direction ($7F71 & $3F); x1: aimed at the player (DirectionTo $989F from the shot to the player record $F36C), with bit 1 set spread by -4..+3 ($7F71 & 7 - 4). The direction (0-63) goes to +$16, SetVelocity $98F1 turns it into the velocity, sound 1 (StartSound $B2F0) plays, the enemy's +8 bit 6 records the shot.
> Callers: the enemy movers at $9FA2, $9FEB and $A06E (EnemyHandler). Evidence: 48K c2 L1 entries at $A0EB during waves with firing enemies; the record layout read off the stores below.
> On a 128K: its CALL RequestEffect at $A1C4 becomes CALL HookEnemyMissile ($B374): sound $19 (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$A0EB ; no fire rate: the enemy never fires
$A0F0 ; fired already: only the random test
$A0F6 ; the first shot when the enemy reaches the trigger column
$A0FE EnemyFireRandom ; the enemy's turn this pass ($7F63 PassBit AND its bit)
$A106 ; Random - $80E8 below the fire rate: fire
$A113 EnemyFireSpend ; a shot left?
$A11A ; the three enemy shot records
$A123 EnemyFireFindSlot ; the first record without bit 7 of +8
$A12D EnemyFireShot ; take it
$A131 ; the shot starts 1.5 cells right and down from the enemy
$A149 ; past row 15: keep it on row 15
$A14F EnemyFireSetY
$A158 ; +$19: bits 0-2 the step count, bits 3-7 the speed index
$A15E ; colour of the shot
$A177 ; lifetime of the shot
$A17D ; +$21: the direction mode
$A180 ; IY = the shot from here on
$A18C ; mode 00: a fixed direction in bits 2-7
$A192 EnemyFireRandomDir ; mode 10: any of the 64 directions
$A199 EnemyFireAimed ; mode x1: at the player, bit 1 adds a spread of -4..+3
$A1A7 EnemyFireAim
$A1A7 ; the player's record
$A1B3 EnemyFireSetDir ; +$16 the direction 0-63, then the velocity from it
$A1BE ; the enemy has fired
$A1C2 ; sound 1: the enemy shot

$A1C8 c ExplosionStep ; Advance an explosion (object kind 3)
> IY: the explosion. Runs in odd passes only ($7F62 bit 0). +$27 counts down the passes a frame is held: while it lasts the colour +$25 is 0 (the frame is drawn black, a flash); when it runs out the animation frame +2 advances.
> After frame 8 the explosion ends: removed (RemoveObject $992A) when +$26 is 0, otherwise it leaves a weapon pod (ExplosionToPod). Handler pointer at $8100.
$A1C8 ; every other pass
$A1CE ; hold the frame
$A1D4 ; black while held
$A1D9 ExplosionNextFrame
$A1DD ; past frame 8: the end
$A1E1 ; a pod to leave?

$A1EB b UnusedRet ; A RET no code reaches
> $C9: a RET after the JP of ExplosionStep; no jump or call leads here (a leftover of an edit of the routine).

$A1EC c ExplosionAdvance ; Store the explosion's frame and fade its colour
> A: the next frame (1-8). The colour +$25 steps down by 8 (one INK step, the bright bit kept off by AND $78) until it reaches $50. Used by ExplosionStep.
$A1EF ; colour $50: stop fading

$A1FD c PodStep ; A weapon pod (object kind 4): collected or carried by the scroll
> IY: the pod. Collected when the player's hit mask $F379 has the pod's bit +$24. IX = the current player's record ($7F99).
> Collecting with fire held for 8 or more passes ($8184 FireHeld) activates the weapon the selector record+7 points at: the selector goes back to 0 and the weapon's routine from the table $80F0 is called (CallFromTable $91E2), sound 6.
> Collecting without fire moves the selector to the next weapon (sound 5); past the fifth it wraps to 0, awards 1000 points (AddScore 9) and sets the power timer $80E6 to $B4. Either way the HUD bit 2 of $81C5 is set, 100 points are awarded (AddScore 4) and the pod is removed.
> Not collected: the pod moves with the scenery (ScrollObject $9640), its colour follows the colour cycle $80BC; once +8 bit 5 is clear (off the playfield) the power timer $80E6 is halved and the pod is removed. Handler pointer at $8102.
> On a 128K: the CALL $91E2 at $A21A (a weapon selected) becomes CALL HookWeaponSelect ($B379): the call, then sound $0F (r1 frames 1097 and 1713); the CALL $A489 at $A247 becomes CALL HookPodTaken ($B364): the call, then sound $16 (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$A1FD ; the player touches the pod?
$A209 ; fire held 8 passes or more: activate the selected weapon
$A210 ; the selector back to the first weapon
$A217 ; the weapon routines: $9D4C $9D5D $9D68 $9DA5 $9DDD
$A21D ; sound 6: weapon on
$A224 PodSelectNext ; sound 5: next weapon
$A22D ; past the fifth weapon: a bonus
$A232 ; 1000 points
$A237 ; the power timer
$A23D PodSetSelector
$A240 PodCollected ; the weapon panel needs a redraw
$A245 ; 100 points
$A24C PodDrift ; not collected: move with the scenery
$A24F ; flashing with the colour cycle
$A255 ; still on the playfield: keep it
$A25A ; lost: halve the power timer
$A25F PodRemove

$A263 c EnemyShotStep ; Move an enemy shot (object kind 5)
> IY: the shot. It ends (FreeShot $9941) when its lifetime +$26 runs out, when it hits the player ($F379 AND +$24), when the playfield collision test $99A9 reports carry, or when after the move (MoveObject $96E2) its Y is outside rows 0-16 or its X is outside columns 16-48 (the visible window). Handler pointer at $8104.
$A263 ; lifetime
$A268 ; the player's hit mask
$A271 ; hit the scenery?
$A276 ; move by the velocity
$A279 ; above the top or below row 16: gone
$A284 ; left of column 16 or right of column 48: gone
$A28F EnemyShotEnd

$A293 c StartExplosion ; Turn the object at IY into an explosion
> Sets the three sound bit patterns ($7F80 = $FD00, $7F84 = $EDB2, $7F88 = $D6ED, shifted one bit a pass by PlayInterrupt: the explosion noise), kind 3, the animation record $7EA1 from frame 1, +$0B = $E0, +$27 = 0, colour $70 and X fraction 0. The object's bit +$24 leaves the mask $80BD and joins $80BE (from the shootable to the harmless objects).
> Callers: the hit handling at ScatterDebris and the enemy movers (EnemyHandler).
> On a 128K: its LD HL,$D6ED at $A29F becomes CALL HookExplosion ($B386): the load, then sound $1D (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$A293 ; the explosion noise on the three channels
$A2A5 ; kind 3: explosion
$A2AA ; the explosion animation
$A2BF ; the bit out of $80BD, into $80BE
$A2CD ; the explosion colour

$A2D6 c ExplosionToPod ; Turn the finished explosion at IY into a weapon pod
> Rounds the Y to the nearest cell (+5 bit 7 carries into +6), then asks $99BB whether the cell is free (carry: no room, the object is removed). Otherwise: +$0B = $C0, kind 4, +$26 down by 2, the pod animation $7EBB (both branches of the JR Z load the same record), the bit +$24 back from $80BE to $80BD, colour 8, sound 4.
> Used by ExplosionStep.
> On a 128K: its CALL RequestEffect at $A31D becomes CALL HookPodAppears ($B381): sound $1C (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$A2D6 ; round Y to the cell
$A2E3 ExplosionToPodPlace
$A2EE ; kind 4: pod
$A2FB ; the pod animation (the same in both cases)
$A303 ExplosionToPodSet
$A309 ; the bit out of $80BE, into $80BD
$A31B ; sound 4: a pod appears

@module print ; Print codes, strings, messages and numbers
@about One print routine draws every text of the game: PrintChar takes a byte, PrintString a string of them, PrintMessage the n-th string of the message list. The print position $80AA is an attribute address ($5800-$5AFF): the character cell and its attribute at once; the attribute byte $80AC colours every character printed.
@about Bytes $20-$7F print the ROM font ($3C00 + 8 code) in a "split bold" style: the glyph's rows 1-7 with each row's left nibble made bold (OR with the row shifted left, mask $F0), a blank pixel line after the first row. Bytes $80-$FF print the game's own 8x8 glyphs (code $80 at $B54F) as they are. Bytes $00-$06 are codes that take the next byte as their operand: 0 attribute, 1 ink, 2 paper, 3 column, 4 row, 5 column + n, 6 row + n. In a string $FF ends it and $1B n prints message n there (strings nest).
@about PrintBcd prints 4 BCD bytes (8 digits) with the leading zeros as a fill character.

$A321 c PrintChar ; Print a character or take a print code
> A: a byte. When a code is pending ($80AD below $20) A is its operand and the code's routine from PrintControlTable runs (codes 7-$1F do nothing), then the pending mark goes back to $20.
> Otherwise a byte below $20 becomes the pending code; a byte from $20 is printed at the print position $80AA with the attribute $80AC, the position moves one cell right. All registers are kept.
> Callers: PrintString, PrintBcd, PrintKeyName, HighScoreEntry.
$A322 PrintCharSave
$A326 ; a code waiting for its operand?
$A32E PrintCharTest ; a code: store it as pending
$A330 PrintCharKind
$A332 ; DE = 8 A (the glyph offset)
$A33B ; the attribute first, the position one cell on
$A347 ; the screen address of the attribute cell: $58-$5A -> $40, $48, $50
$A34D PrintCharGlyph
$A34F ; $80-$FF: the game's glyphs
$A354 ; the ROM font
$A357 PrintRomGlyph ; HL = the ROM glyph
$A358 ; B 6 rows, C the bold mask of the left nibble
$A35B ; row 0 of the ROM glyph skipped: it is always blank
$A362 ; the blank second pixel line
$A365 PrintCharRow ; rows 2-7, bold
$A366 PrintCharBold
$A36E PrintCharDone ; the byte becomes the pending mark: $20 or more for a character, the code itself
$A376 PrintGlyph ; the game's glyph $80-$FF: code $80 at $B54F, 8 rows as they are
$A37C PrintGlyphRow
$A384 PrintCode ; a code with its operand in C
$A386 ; 7 and up: no routine
$A393 ; the routine returns to PrintCodeDone

$A399 c PrintCodeDone ; End a print code: nothing pending
> The return of every print code routine (pushed by PrintChar): $80AD = $20, the registers PrintChar saved restored.

@isub $A376 LD BC,Glyphs-$0400
@isub $A842 LD HL,-14
@isub $A84F LD HL,-10
@isub $AA58 LD HL,$C9F3

$A3A3 w PrintControlTable 7 ; The routines of the print codes 0-6
> Each takes its operand in C: PrintSetAttr, PrintSetInk, PrintSetPaper, PrintSetColumn, PrintSetRow, PrintColumnRight, PrintRowDown.

$A3B1 c PrintSetAttr ; Print code 0: the attribute
> C: the attribute byte for the next characters ($80AC).

$A3B6 c PrintSetInk ; Print code 1: the ink
> C: the ink 0-7 into the attribute $80AC.

$A3BF c PrintSetPaper ; Print code 2: the paper
> C: the paper 0-7 into bits 3-5 of the attribute $80AC.

$A3CD c PrintSetColumn ; Print code 3: the column
> C: the column 0-31 of the print position.

$A3D6 c PrintSetRow ; Print code 4: the row
> C: the row 0-23: the print position's high byte $58 + C / 8, bits 5-7 of the low byte C AND 7; the column is kept. Also the end of PrintRowDown.

$A3ED c PrintColumnRight ; Print code 5: move the column
> C: the columns to add (a negative operand, $FE, moves left).

$A3F4 c PrintRowDown ; Print code 6: move the row
> C: the rows to add to the current row (the position's row from bits 5-9 of its offset).

$A401 c PrintString ; Print a string
> HL: the string. Bytes go to PrintChar; $FF ends the string, $1B n prints message n (PrintMessage) and goes on. The byte after a print code is its operand, never taken as $FF or $1B. HL is kept.
> Callers: PrintMessage and the title loop (HiScorePage).
$A403 PrintStringNext
$A40A ; an operand: print it as it is
$A414 PrintStringChar
$A419 PrintStringMessage ; $1B n: message n
$A420 PrintStringEnd

$A423 c PrintMessage ; Print message A
> A: the message number. The messages are the $FF-terminated strings from $82EE (after the $FF at $82ED); message n starts after the n+1-th $FF from $82ED. Registers kept.
> Callers: PrintString, the menu screens (RedefineKeys, PrintKeyName, SelectPlayers, HighScoreEntry) and LevelStart, CreditsPage, HiScorePage. The list of the messages: ../zynaps/work/stage1/texts.txt.
$A42F PrintMessageFind ; skip to the next $FF

$A43C c PrintBcdSpaces ; Print a BCD number with spaces for its leading zeros (no caller)
> LD C,$20 before PrintBcd: an entry no code calls (the callers load C themselves).

$A43E c PrintBcd ; Print an 8-digit BCD number
> HL: 4 BCD bytes (the high digits first); C: the character printed for each leading zero; A': the attribute of the digits after the leading zeros (the zeros print with the attribute in $80AC). The number is copied to the scratch $7FFC and its digits are taken out a nibble at a time by RLD (the $0F put in by the first RLD marks a byte used up). Up to 7 leading zeros are replaced, the last digit always prints.
> Returns HL past the number. Callers: PrintPlayerScore and the title loop (CreditsPage, HiScorePage).
$A44C ; '0'
$A44E ; at most 7 leading zeros
$A452 PrintBcdLeading
$A454 ; $0F back: the byte is used up, the next one
$A45B PrintBcdDigit
$A45F ; the first digit that is not zero: the digits' attribute
$A467 PrintBcdZero ; a leading zero: the fill character
$A46B PrintBcdLeadNext
$A46E ; the last digit always prints
$A474 PrintBcdRest
$A47D PrintBcdRestDigit
$A47E PrintBcdOut ; '0' + digit

$A482 PrintBcdRestNext
@module score ; Score and lives on the panel
@about The two players' scores are 4 BCD bytes at the start of their records ($7F9C player 1, $7FCC player 2; $7F99 points at the current one, $7F98 is its number). AddScore adds an award from the points table $8231 and marks the panel for a redraw; PrintPlayerScore and DrawLives draw the panel; CompareScore ranks a score in the high-score table.

$A489 c AddScore ; Add award A to the current player's score
> A: the award number 0-11 of the points table $8231 (4 BCD bytes each: 0, 25, 50, 75, 100, 150, 200, 250, 500, 1000, 2000, 5000). The award is added from the low byte up to the score of player $7F98 (the low byte at $7F9F or $7FCF); bit 1 of $81C5 asks for the panel score redraw.
> Callers: the enemy kill (EnemyGone), the enemy movers (EnemyHandler), PodStep.
$A48E ; HL = the award's low byte
$A492 ; player 1's score, low byte
$A49B ; player 2's
$A49E AddScoreBytes
$A4A0 AddScoreByte
$A4A8 ; the score on the panel is out of date

$A4AE c PrintPlayerScore ; Print player A's score on the panel
> A: the player, 1 or 2. Player 1's score at column 2, player 2's at column 22 of the top row; bright white ($47) for the player in play, dark cyan ($05) for the other, leading zeros as spaces. Callers: the panel drawing (ScoreJob) and the player change (RoundPlaySetup).
$A4BF PrintPlayerScoreColour
$A4C9 PrintPlayerScoreAt ; the leading spaces in blue ink

$A4D8 PrintPlayerScoreDone
$A4DA c CompareScore ; Is the score at DE above the score at HL?
> DE, HL: 4 BCD bytes each. Carry set when DE's score is greater (equal scores: no carry). Registers kept. Used by HighScoreEntry.
$A4DF CompareScoreByte
$A4E5 CompareScoreNext
$A4EA CompareScoreDone ; equal to the end: SCF then CCF, no carry

$A4EF c DrawLives ; Draw the current player's lives on the panel
> Six life marks from the attribute $5AD8 (row 22, column 24), the steps between them in $81C0: the first record+4 marks red on black ($42), the rest black. Callers: ScoreJob, RoundPlaySetup.
$A4FD ; six marks
$A501 DrawLivesMark
$A505 ; past the lives: black
$A507 DrawLivesNext

@module menu ; Menu screens: the raster colours, the key definition
@about The menu screens (the selections, the key definition, the high-score table) colour the text with a raster: MenuRasterInterrupt, the interrupt handler while a menu is shown, waits for the beam to reach the top of the screen and rewrites 20 attribute cells (columns 6-25) of the current attribute row for each scan line, so each scan line of the text can have its own colour.
@about The colours of the lines come from the colour buffer $FC00 (the table pointer $80AE, the line count $80B0) that ExpandColours fills from run-length lists ($82A7, $82CE, $82D7, $82E0). The menu routines animate a highlight by expanding a list into the buffer again a line further each frame.
@about RedefineKeys asks for the five keys (fire, up, down, left, right) into the key table $8188; WaitKeyPress scans the six half-rows of $8192 (not 1-5 nor 6-0: those are the joystick and menu keys).

$A512 c MenuRasterInterrupt ; Interrupt handler of the menu screens: a colour per scan line
> Saves every register and SP (into the LD SP at $A575), sets the border black, waits about 77 x 15 DJNZ and 23 more for the beam (61 and 16 in the 48K: the Pentagon's interrupt comes at another place in the frame), then for each of $80B0 scan lines takes a colour from the table at ($80AE) and PUSHes it into 20 attribute cells ending at HL (HL steps a row of 32 every 8 lines: A' rotates a single bit). Both paths of a line take the same time; a line is 12 T-states longer than the 48K's (LD B,(HL) twice, two NOPs and JR NZ where the 48K had three NOPs and JP NZ). Then SP back, $80B9 (frames left, counted by WaitMenuRasterEnd) down by one unless 0, EI.
> Installed by SetMenuRaster in the interrupt handler pointer $FDFE. Evidence: 48K c2 L1 has $A512 in frames 690-765 (the selections screen), 26 permille of the frame time.
> On a 128K: the glue makes the POP BC: POP DE: POP HL at $A581 JP PlayAtA581 ($5B28): a frame of the engine, then the POPs (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$A51C ; the stack pointer kept in the LD SP below
$A520 ; DE' the colour table, C' the lines
$A52A ; one cell above row 0, column 26: the first ADD makes it $581A
$A530 ; a bit rotating once a line: every 8th line the next attribute row
$A534 ; border black
$A537 ; wait for the beam: $4D passes ($3D in the 48K)
$A539 MenuRasterWait
$A53B MenuRasterDelay
$A544 ; and a little more: $17 ($10 in the 48K)
$A546 MenuRasterAlign
$A54C MenuRasterLine ; the colour of this line
$A552 ; every 8th line: the next attribute row
$A559 MenuRasterSameRow ; the same time as the ADD and JP
$A55C MenuRasterPush ; 20 cells, columns 6-25, in this line's colour
$A568 ; the line's padding: LD B,(HL) twice and two NOPs, 22 T (12 in the 48K)
$A56E ; the next line
$A575 MenuRasterSavedSp ; LD SP,nn: the stack pointer saved at the start
$A578 ; the frames left for WaitMenuRasterEnd
$A581 MenuRasterDone

$A58D c SetMenuRaster ; Install MenuRasterInterrupt as the interrupt handler
> Writes $A512 to the handler pointer $FDFE, EI. Callers: MenuScreenSetup, SelectPlayers.

$A595 c WaitMenuRasterEnd ; Wait for the menu raster's frames to run out, then the title interrupt
> Waits (HALT) until $80B9 is 0, clears the colour buffer, then installs TitleInterrupt ($A9E4) in $FDFE. Callers: RedefineKeys, SelectPlayers, HighScoreEntry, RoundPlaySetup.

$A5A7 c ClearColourBuffer ; Clear the raster colour buffer $FC00-$FCFF
> Callers: WaitMenuRasterEnd, MenuScreenSetup, SelectPlayers.
$A5AB ClearColourBufferByte

$A5B0 c ExpandNextColours ; Expand the colour list ($80B1) into the buffer at DE
> As ExpandColours with HL from $80B1.

$A5B3 c ExpandColours ; Expand a run-length colour list into the buffer
> HL: the list; DE: the destination; B: the bytes to write. A byte below $80 is a colour, $80+n followed by a colour is n of that colour, $FF starts the list again (it repeats until B bytes are written). Returns DE past the bytes, HL at the list's start.
> Callers: MenuScreenSetup, SelectPlayers, RedefineKeys (by ExpandNextColours).
$A5B4 ExpandColoursRestart ; $FF: from the list's start again
$A5B6 ExpandColoursNext
$A5BD ; a single colour: C = 2, one store
$A5C2 ; $80+n: n of the next byte
$A5C8 ExpandColoursStore

$A5D1 c RedefineKeys ; The key definition screen (menu key 3)
> MenuScreenSetup, message 13 (KEYBOARD DEFINITION), then for each of the five keys of the key table $8188 (fire, up, down, left, right; a half-row port high byte and a bit mask each): its prompt (messages 14-18), its current name, a new key (WaitKeyPress), the new name, 10 frames, the key stored; the line's 8 scan lines get the highlight colours ($82D7 expanded into $FC60 + 24 a key).
> After the last key: 100 frames, WaitMenuRasterEnd, ClearScreen $858B. Used by MenuKeys.
> On a 128K: the CALL $A685 at $A5D1 becomes CALL HookScreenWipe ($B39C): the music off, the wipe, sounds $1E + $1F; the glue makes the CALL $858B at $A61F CALL ClearThenMusic ($5B46): the screen cleared, the music on (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$A5D4 ; the highlight colours and their place in the buffer
$A5DF ; KEYBOARD DEFINITION
$A5E7 ; the key table
$A5EA RedefineKeysNext ; the next prompt
$A5F0 ; the current key: C the port, B the mask
$A5F6 ; a new key
$A601 ; stored
$A604 ; the highlight under this line
$A60C ; the next line of the buffer, 3 text rows down
$A617 ; 2 seconds

$A623 c PrintKeyName ; Print the name of a key and come back to its place
> C: the half-row port high byte; B: the bit mask. The key number 5 x the half-row (the 0 bit of C) + the bit is looked up in the key names $8198: a letter prints as it is, 0-3 print the messages 9-12 (CS, SS, SP, EN). A space after it; the print position is put back.
$A626 ; 5 x the half-row
$A628 PrintKeyNameRow
$A62F PrintKeyNameBit ; + the bit
$A637 ; the key names, one per key
$A640 ; 0-3: the name is a message
$A64B PrintKeyNameChar
$A64E PrintKeyNameDone

$A65B c WaitKeyPress ; Wait for one key, then for its release
> Scans the six half-rows of $8192 ($FE $FD $FB $DF $BF $7F) until exactly one key of a half-row is down. Returns C = its half-row port byte, B = its bit mask; waits until no key at all is down.
$A65E WaitKeyPressScan
$A663 WaitKeyPressRow
$A66B ; a single bit?
$A66C WaitKeyPressBit
$A672 WaitKeyPressNextRow
$A677 WaitKeyPressFound
$A679 WaitKeyPressRelease

$A685 c MenuScreenSetup ; Clear the screen and start the menu raster
> ClearScreen $858B, ClearColourBuffer, the colours $82A7 (48 lines) into $FC20 and $82CE (120 lines) into $FC50, 168 raster lines from $FC20, SetMenuRaster. Callers: RedefineKeys, HighScoreEntry, LevelStart.

$A6AE c WaitFrames ; Wait A frames
> A: the frames (HALT A times, interrupts enabled). Callers: RedefineKeys, HighScoreEntry, EndOfPlay.
$A6B1 WaitFramesLoop

@module input ; The controls: keys, Kempston and Sinclair or cursor joystick
@about ReadControls reads the five defined keys and one joystick into the control bits $8186 (bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, the Kempston order) and the fire counters $8184 (passes fire has been held, 0 when released) and $8185 ($FF in a pass fire changed).
@about The joystick is the Kempston (port $1F) when $8187 bit 0 is set (TitleFireCheck sets it when the title is left with Kempston fire), otherwise the keys 5-0, read as the cursor joystick (5 left, 6 down, 7 up, 8 right, 0 fire) or the Sinclair joystick (6 left, 7 right, 8 down, 9 up, 0 fire). 9 selects Sinclair (sticky in $8187 bit 1), 5 goes back to cursor.

$A6B6 c ReadControls ; Read the controls into $8184-$8186
> C collects the five keys of $8188 (fire into bit 4, then up, down, left, right), ORed with the joystick bits. $8184 counts the passes fire is held, $8185 = $FF when fire was pressed or released since the last read, $8186 the direction bits (fire removed). Registers kept.
> Callers: the player mover (PlayerHandler) and HighScoreEntry. Evidence: the bits and counters seen in 48K c2 while the bot plays.
> With K-MOUSE the IN A,($1F): AND $1F at $A6D4 becomes CALL MouseRead ($FE1D): NOP: the mouse moves the ship (src/game/setup.asm).
$A6BA ; five keys, the first into bit 4
$A6C1 ControlsKeyLoop
$A6C6 ; pressed (0): carry
$A6CD ; the joystick flags
$A6D4 ; Kempston: fire bit 4, up 3, down 2, left 1, right 0
$A6DC ControlsKeys56789 ; the keys 6-0 and 1-5 rows
$A6E8 ; 9: the Sinclair joystick
$A6F0 ControlsCursor ; cursor keys
$A6F2 ; 5: left
$A6F8 ControlsCursorFire ; 0: fire
$A6FE ControlsCursorRight ; 8: right
$A703 ControlsCursorUp ; 7: up
$A708 ControlsCursorDown ; 6: down
$A70D ControlsCursorDone
$A710 ControlsSinclair ; 5 down: back to cursor
$A714 ; Sinclair from now on
$A716 ; fire, up, down out of bits 0-2 into bits 4-2
$A722 ; left and right are left in E bits 1-0
$A723 ControlsMerge
$A725 ; the fire counter
$A72B ; fire held: count, released: 0
$A730 ControlsFireCount
$A733 ; $8185 = (held now) XOR (held before)

$A748 c TitleFireCheck ; Is fire pressed on the title screen? Also picks the Kempston
> Carry set when the Kempston reads exactly fire ($10: then $8187 bit 0 stays set and the game uses the Kempston), or, with the Kempston flag cleared, when the fire key ($8188) or 0 is down. Callers: HighScoreEntry and the title loop (CreditsPage, HiScorePage, Pause, TitleTune).
> With K-MOUSE the start test at $A74E becomes LD A,$FA: IN A,($DF): AND 2: the mouse's left button starts play (src/game/setup.asm).
$A74E ; the Kempston fire alone
$A756 ; not the Kempston
$A762 ; or 0
$A769 ; no fire: no carry
$A76C TitleFireYes

@module options ; The selections: one or two players
@about MenuKeys reads the keys 1, 2 and 3 of the title: one player, two players, the key definition. SelectPlayers stores the number in $7F9B and shows the selections screen with a colour bar sweeping the chosen line.

$A76F c MenuKeys ; Read the menu keys 1-3
> 1: SelectOnePlayer, 2: SelectTwoPlayers, 3: RedefineKeys. Registers kept. Used by the title loop (CreditsPage).
$A773 ; 1, 2, 3 are bits 0-2 of $F7FE

$A788 c SelectOnePlayer ; Select the one player game
> A = 1, then as SelectTwoPlayers.

$A78D c SelectTwoPlayers ; Select the two player game
> SelectPlayers with A = 2.
> On a 128K: the LD HL,$82E0 at $A7BB becomes CALL HookSelections ($B38F): the music off, the load, then sounds $1E + $1F (r1 frame 23); the glue makes the CALL $858B at $A7F2 CALL ClearThenMusic ($5B46) (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$A790 SelectPlayers ; A: the players
> Stores A in $7F9B, clears the screen, colours $82A7 (48 lines) into $FC20 and $82D7 (64 lines) into $FC50, 112 raster lines, message 19 (SELECTIONS, 1 PLAYER, 2 PLAYERS). Then 8 x 16 steps of 2 frames: the bar $82E0 expanded twice (32 lines) a line higher each step under the chosen line ($FC68 one player, $FC88 two), about 5 seconds. Then WaitMenuRasterEnd and ClearScreen.
$A7BB ; the bar's colours
$A7C1 ; under 1 PLAYER
$A7CA ; under 2 PLAYERS
$A7CD SelectPlayersShow ; SELECTIONS
$A7D4 SelectPlayersSweep
$A7D8 SelectPlayersStep ; every 2 frames
$A7E7 ; a line higher

@module hiscore ; The high-score table: rank and initials
@about The table ROLE OF HONOUR at $8261 holds 5 entries of 14 bytes: a 10-byte print string (row, column, three initials, $1B 8 for the dots, $FF) and the score in 4 BCD bytes. A new entry moves the lower ones down by copying 10 bytes of each (the initials, the dots and the score: the row and column stay) and takes the player's score; the initials are picked with left and right, A-Z, and fire.

$A7F7 c HighScoreEntry ; Rank the current player's score, take the initials
> MenuScreenSetup, message 25 (ROLE OF HONOUR, PLAYER n). The score (the record ($7F99) + 0) is compared with the five entries from the top: below all of them message 26 (UNRANKED) for 150 frames or until fire.
> Ranked: message 27 (ENTER YOUR INITIALS), the lower entries move down, the score is copied in, then three letters at row 13 column 13 (attribute $59AD, 2 cells apart): each starts at A, right/left (ReadControls) step it through A-Z with wrap-around every 5 frames, fire (or 100 steps without movement, 10 seconds) takes it and prints a dot after it; 20 frames between the letters, 50 after the last. Ends with WaitMenuRasterEnd, ClearScreen.
> Used by the game over (PlayerDied).
> On a 128K: its first CALL $A685 (at $A7F7) becomes CALL HookScreenWipe ($B39C): the music off, the wipe, sounds $1E + $1F; the CALL $A423 at $A81B becomes CALL HookHonour ($B344): the call, then sound $12; the CALL $A595 at $A8B7 becomes CALL HookHonourResult ($B34C): the call, then sounds $14 + $15 (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$A7FA ; ROLE OF HONOUR
$A7FF ; the score at +0 of the player's record
$A807 ; the first entry's score
$A80C HighScoreRank
$A812 ; the next entry
$A819 ; UNRANKED
$A81E ; 3 seconds or fire
$A820 HighScoreUnranked
$A82C HighScoreInsert ; ENTER YOUR INITIALS
$A833 ; the first copy (the last entry) goes to the ROM: dropped
$A836 ; the initials of the last entry
$A839 HighScoreMoveDown ; an entry's initials, dots and score one entry down
$A842 ; the entry above
$A848 ; the player's score into the free entry
$A84F ; HL = the free entry's initials
$A853 ; row 13, column 13
$A859 HighScoreLetter
$A85A ; 'A'
$A85C ; 100 steps without input take the letter
$A860 HighScoreLetterShow
$A870 ; fire: take it
$A876 ; time out: take it
$A87B ; left or right: the time out again
$A886 HighScoreLetterMove ; right +1, left -1
$A890 ; past Z: A
$A896 ; before A: Z
$A89D HighScoreLetterLoop
$A89F HighScoreLetterTaken ; the initial and a dot
$A8B7 HighScoreDone

@module panel ; Panel spinners, saving the panel
@about Two spinners turn on the panel's sides (row 20, columns 1 and 29; drawn by the panel update PanelFrame each pass): 16x16 images symmetric left to right, kept as the left 8 pixels of 5 shapes; the right half is the bit mirror (the table $FB00), the odd offsets read the shape bottom up.
@about SavePanel and RestorePanel keep the screen third $5000-$57FF and the attribute rows 19-23 in $E748 while another screen covers them.

$A8BE c DrawSpinner ; Draw a 2x2 cell spinner frame
> HL: the attribute address of the top left cell; A: its attribute (four cells); C: the frame 0-15. The frame's offset from SpinnerFrames picks the shape in SpinnerImages: an even offset is the start of a shape read top down, an odd one the last byte of a shape read bottom up (the shape flipped upside down). Each row is drawn as the byte and its mirror (the bit-reverse table $FB00) side by side.
> Callers: the panel update PanelFrame ($AB97, $AB9F).
$A8C9 ; the shape's offset
$A8D6 ; the screen address of the cell
$A8DE ; H' = the bit-reverse table
$A8E1 ; two cells down
$A8E3 ; odd offset: upside down
$A8E8 DrawSpinnerFlippedCell
$A8EB DrawSpinnerFlippedRow ; the byte left, its mirror right
$A8F9 ; the cell below
$A901 DrawSpinnerCell
$A904 DrawSpinnerRow

$A919 b SpinnerFrames 8 ; The 16 frames of a spinner: offsets into SpinnerImages
> Even: a shape top down; odd: the end of a shape, bottom up.

$A929 b SpinnerImages 16 ; The spinner shapes: 5 x 16 rows, the left 8 pixels
> From a thin diagonal line ($A929) to a full ring ($A969): the turning disc seen from its edge to its face.

$A979 c RestorePanel ; Restore the bottom third and the panel attributes from $E748
> $E748 -> $5000 (2048 bytes) and $EF48 -> $5A60 (160 bytes, attribute rows 19-23). Called at $B04F (the player change, RoundPlaySetup).

$A98D c SavePanel ; Save the bottom third and the panel attributes into $E748
> The reverse of RestorePanel. Called at $B1F9.

@module interrupts ; The interrupts: the title, the play passes and their scheduler
@about In play the interrupt handler pointer $FDFE holds PlayInterrupt. One play interrupt is one pass of the game, and a pass takes two frames (the next frame's interrupt is lost under DI). The phase $7F65 cycles 1-4: phase 1 scrolls a column (ScrollStep $8AED) and starts the next one ($894D), phases 2-4 test and clear the hit grid ($9150, $91C6); each then runs the stars, the panel, the playfield, the play logic (PlayPass) and the next step of the drawing chain.
@about The chain is three CALL BuildColumn ($896D) each followed by DI; RET (ChainBuildColumn1-3), then ChainEnd. BuildColumn enables the interrupts while it builds glyphs (EI at $8AA2, not in phase 4), so the next interrupt arrives inside a chain step: at depth 1 ($7F64) a pass runs its chain step itself; a nested pass (depth 2) instead overwrites the DI; RET of the step before its own with NOPs and leaves at once, so the interrupted step, when it returns from BuildColumn, falls into the next one. A slow frame thus draws the strips back to back; ChainEnd sets the phase to 0 and phase 1 restores the three DI; RET ($C9F3).
@about The timing waits (DJNZ loops) put the border changes and the column building behind the beam. Evidence: TTD 48K c2 frames 300-316: $7F62 +1 every 2 frames, $7F65 1, 2, 3, 4, 0, $7F66 $AAE5, $AAEA, $AAEF, $AAF4, $AAF9; L1: PlayInterrupt 659 entries in 48K c2, BuildColumn 688 (165/165/166 from the chain steps + 192 from $AFA3).

$A9A1 c ScrollControl ; Start, stop and step the fine scroll
> At phase 4 when the restart flag $81E3 is $FF: it becomes 0 and the scroll starts ($81DA bit 5). When $7F7B is not $FF the scroll stops (bit 5 cleared). Otherwise with the scroll on, or still between two cells ($7F6A not $C0), the fine scroll counter steps: $7F6B toggles $08/$10 and $7F6A takes it by XOR, a 2-bit Gray code $C0, $C8, $D8, $D0; $7F6C = $FF says the pass scrolls (0: it does not).
> Called by PlayPass. Evidence: TTD 48K c2 frames 300-316: $7F6A C8, D8, D0, C0, $7F6B 08/10, $7F6C FF.
$A9A8 ; the restart flag
$A9B0 ; the scroll on
$A9B5 ScrollControlRunning
$A9BB ; the scroll off
$A9C1 ScrollControlStep
$A9CC ; stopped: only up to the cell boundary
$A9D2 ScrollControlGray ; the next fine position

$A9E4 c TitleInterrupt ; Interrupt handler of the title and between screens
> Only sets the interrupt flag $7F96 = $80. Installed by WaitMenuRasterEnd and the game's screens ($852D, $ABE6, $ABF8, $B18B, $B1F2).
> On a 128K: the glue makes the LD HL,$7F96 at $A9E5 CALL PlayAtA9E5 ($5B21): a frame of the engine, then the load (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).

$A9ED c PlayInterrupt ; Interrupt handler in play: one phase of a game pass
> Saves all registers. Phase 4 (a pass whose chain has not ended): nothing more. Otherwise $7F62 +1 (PassCount), $7F63 rotated (PassBit, the object bit of this pass), $7F64 +1 (depth), the phase $7F65 +1; $7F96 = $FF; the colour cycle $80BC 8, $10, $18; the three sound bit patterns $7F80, $7F84, $7F88 shift one bit, the bit out into $7F7F, $7F83, $7F87.
> Phase 1: $8AED, the chain from its start ($7F66 = $AAE5), the three DI; RET restored, $894D, a wait of $0B (scroll on) or $2F x 16 DJNZ. Phases 2-3: $9150, $91C6, a wait of $10. Then the wait before the border black is $77 DJNZ. (The 48K: 2, $26, 5 and $10; the Pentagon's counts are larger, its interrupt comes earlier in the frame and nothing is contended.) Then $8894, the panel update $AB13, a wait, the border black, $85CA, and PlayPass.
> Installed at $B1DF. Evidence: 48K c2 L1 659 entries, 12 permille own time.
> On a 128K: the glue makes the PUSH IX: PUSH IY at $A9F7 JP PlayAtA9F7 ($5B31) and a NOP: one engine step; with PlayAtAB03 the engine steps twice a pass in play (c1: 3220 times each), and a pass takes two TV frames, so one step a TV frame on average (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.8).
$A9FB ; a pass still in its chain: skip
$AA03 ; PassCount
$AA07 ; PassBit
$AA0C ; depth
$AA10 ; the phase
$AA16 ; the interrupt flag
$AA1B ; the colour cycle: 8, $10, $18
$AA27 PlayInterruptColour
$AA28 ; the sound bit patterns, one bit a pass
$AA49 ; phase 1: a new pass
$AA52 ; the chain from its start
$AA58 ; F3 C9: DI; RET after each chain step again
$AA67 ; the wait with the scroll on: $0B ($02 in the 48K)
$AA70 ; and without
$AA74 PlayInterruptPhase ; phases 2-4
$AA7A ; phases 2-3: $10 ($05 in the 48K)
$AA7C PlayInterruptWait
$AA7E PlayInterruptDelay
$AA87 ; the panel spinners, power and weapon marks
$AA8C ; $77 ($10 in the 48K)
$AA8E PlayInterruptDelay2
$AA90 ; border black

$AA96 c PlayPass ; The play logic of a phase
> $ABD5, ScrollControl, $9463, $8F9B; in odd phases $90CB, in phase 2 $AD66; at depth 1 in phases 2-3 $8D45. Falls into PlayChain.

$AAC3 c PlayChain ; Advance the drawing chain
> A wait, the border black, the chain step $7F66 + 5. At depth 1 the step runs (RunChainStep) and returns to PlayInterruptExit; nested, the DI; RET before the step are replaced by two NOPs so the interrupted step continues into it.
$AAC7 PlayChainDelay
$AACC ; the next chain step
$AAD6 ; nested?
$AADD ; NOP the DI; RET of the step before
$AAE5 RunChainStep ; run it, returning to PlayInterruptExit

$AAEA c ChainBuildColumn1 ; Chain step 1: build cells of the new column
> CALL BuildColumn $896D then DI; RET (two NOPs when a nested pass chained step 2 on).
$AAED ChainStep1End ; DI; RET or NOP; NOP

$AAEF c ChainBuildColumn2 ; Chain step 2: build cells of the new column
$AAF2 ChainStep2End ; DI; RET or NOP; NOP

$AAF4 c ChainBuildColumn3 ; Chain step 3: build cells of the new column
$AAF7 ChainStep3End ; DI; RET or NOP; NOP

$AAF9 c ChainEnd ; The end of the chain: phase 0
> DI, $7F65 = 0, so the next interrupt starts a new pass.

$AAFF c PlayInterruptExit ; Leave the play interrupt
> Depth $7F64 down by one, all registers back, EI.
> On a 128K: the glue makes the POP IY: POP IX at $AB03 JP PlayAtAB03 ($5B3C) and a NOP: a frame of the engine (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$AB03 PlayInterruptRestore
# F4: $AB13-$B72E and $FF01-$FFFE. The panel's frame work, the event scheduler and its handlers, the wave script VM,
# the level start and the player records, the title pages, the game's top loops, the object set-up, the sound effects,
# the title tune, the pod icons and the print font, the start-up; past F6's range the tune's data, the developer switch
# Invulnerable and the leftover of the ROM's character set. Evidence: tools/l1info.py over ../zynaps/work/c1 and ../zynaps/work/c2
# (48K c1: the title and the attract pages, 48K c2: play), ../zynaps/work/f4/vmwalk.py (the level scripts), the listing ../zynaps/work/mkgame.
@module hud ; the frame work of the panel: colour cycles, the speed bar, the fuel gauge
@about PanelFrame runs once a pass from the play interrupt ($AA87 in the handler $A9ED) and only writes attributes of the panel's rows 20-23 ($5A80-$5AFF): it never touches pixels, so it costs nothing in the drawing code. 659 entries in 48K c2, one a pass of play.
@about What it shows: two cells at $5AC6 whose ink steps every fourth pass; the speed bar at $5AEE (one to four lit cells for the speeds $40-$A0, the player record's +$0B); the fuel gauge, two pairs of lamps at $5AC1 and $5ADD that go dark one by one as Fuel ($80E6) falls below $40 and $20; two colour blocks at $5A81 and $5A9D through $A8BE with a phase and a bouncing value ($7F78-$7F7A); and every other pass the burst of $7F77 (set to 6 by FireLaser at $9C80 when it is 0: a laser shot): a pair of cells that walks out from column 15 of rows 21-22 while the counter runs down.
@about CheckPauseAndEnd, the play interrupt's check of the key 4 (pause) and of a pending end code (from the phase dispatch at $AA96, once a pass of play), and StopAndSettle, which silences the effect and lets two frames pass under TitleInterrupt, are kept here: the pause runs inside the interrupt, so the play loop and the effect stand still under it.
$AB13 c PanelFrame ; the panel's attribute work of one pass of play
> Called from PlayInterrupt $A9ED (at $AA87) once a pass; 48K c2: 659 entries in 659 passes, 630704 T own (6 permille of the recording). Reads PassCount $7F62, the player record ($7F99: +$0B speed), Fuel $80E6, $7F77-$7F7A; writes only the attribute rows 20-23.
> 1. The ink of $5AC6-$5AC7 + 1 every fourth pass (PassCount & 3 = 0), kept in 0-7 with the paper and bright bits (AND $C7 drops the carry into paper).
> 2. The speed bar: $5AEE-$5AF1 = $01, then (speed >> 5) - 1 cells of $31 from $5AEE: one lit cell at $40 up to four at $A0, the top speed (FuelOut keeps it from going below $40, which would make B = 0 and the DJNZ write 256 cells).
> 3. The fuel lamps: DE = $3131 at $5AC1 and $5ADD; Fuel < $40 darkens D, < $20 darkens E too.
> 4. The two colour blocks: PanelPhase $7F78 + (PassCount & 1) in 0-15 (C), PanelBounce $7F79 + PanelStep $7F7A on odd passes, the step reversed at 7 or 1 of the low 3 bits; $A8BE draws A at $5A81 and $5A9D with C.
> 5. On even passes: rows 21-22 columns 11-20 ($5AAB, $5ACB) cleared; PanelBurst $7F77 not 0: counted down, its new value n written as the attribute at columns 15+n and 16-n of both rows (the pair moves out as n falls and goes black at 0).
$AB13 ; PassCount
$AB1A ; C = 1 every fourth pass, else 0
$AB1D PanelInkCells ; the two cells at row 22, columns 6-7
$AB22 PanelInkStep ; ink + C, the bright and paper bits kept
$AB2A ; the current player's record
$AB2E ; the speed: $20 a step in the top 3 bits
$AB33 ; three RLCAs: the top 3 bits into bits 0-2
$AB36 ; the lit cells: the speed step less one
$AB38 ; the four bar cells unlit first
$AB41 ; then B cells of $31 (blue ink on cyan paper: lit)
$AB44 PanelSpeedBar
$AB49 ; both lamps of each pair lit
$AB4C ; Fuel: $5A at a level start and after each FuelOut, $B4 when the pod selector wraps past the fifth pod ($A237)
$AB53 ; below $40: the first lamp of each pair dark
$AB59 ; below $20: the second as well
$AB5B PanelFuelLamps ; the pairs at row 22 column 1 and row 22 column 29
$AB63 ; PanelPhase + (PassCount & 1), 0-15
$AB70 ; PanelBounce: moved by PanelStep on odd passes
$AB7F ; at 7 or 1 of its low bits the step turns
$AB89 PanelTurn ; PanelStep = -PanelStep
$AB91 PanelBlocks ; A = PanelBounce, C = PanelPhase: $A8BE paints the blocks at row 20 columns 1 and 29
$ABA2 ; the burst only on even passes
$ABA8 ; clear rows 21-22, columns 11-20
$ABB1 PanelClearRow
$ABB7 ; PanelBurst: 0 = idle
$ABBD ; one step less; the new value is both the colour and the distance
$ABC6 ; column 15 + n on row 21 and row 22
$ABCD ; column 16 - n on both rows
$ABD5 c CheckPauseAndEnd ; key 4 pauses; a pending end code ends the play loop
> Called by the play interrupt once a pass (the phase dispatch in the routine at $AA96; 48K c2: 659 entries in 659 passes). Key 4 (half row $F7FE, bit 3) calls Pause ($B197). Then a pending end code in EndPending ($7F7C: 1 a life lost, 2 the level done, 4 abort) is moved to EndCode ($7F7D), which ends the play loop at $B1E9, the interrupt handler ($FDFE) becomes TitleInterrupt $A9E4 (it only sets $7F96 = $80, no frame work) and StopAndSettle silences the effect. Returns with A = 0 when nothing is pending.
> On a 128K: the glue makes the CALL Z,$B197 (key 4) at $ABDB CALL Z,Key4MusicOff ($5B7B): the music off first (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$ABD5 ; the half row 1-5
$ABD9 ; key 4 down (0)
$ABDE ; EndPending: set by the death code ($9B5B), the level's end command ($AE40) and Pause (Q)
$ABE3 ; EndCode: the play loop sees it
$ABE6 ; TitleInterrupt: no frame work from here on
$ABF0 c StopAndSettle ; silence the effect and let two frames pass under TitleInterrupt
> Requests effect 0 (RequestEffect $B2F0 takes 0 as $80: the stop), keeps InterruptHandler ($FDFE), installs TitleInterrupt $A9E4, two HALTs with interrupts on, DI and Handler back. Called by CheckPauseAndEnd and Pause; 48K c2: 2 entries (one pause and one end in the play recording).
$ABF0 ; effect 0: stop the running one
$ABF4 ; the handler in use
$ABFE ; two frames under TitleInterrupt: its EffectTick = $80 makes a playing effect take the stop
$AC01 ; back to the old handler with interrupts off
@module events ; the event scheduler: one request bit a job, the sprite builds, the pod icon, the score, the effects
@about The play loop ($B1E6) does nothing but call EventDispatch until EndCode is set; the work it runs is posted by setting a bit in EventRequests ($81C5) from the interrupt, the collisions and the weapons. EventMask ($81C6, $FF from the play loop's start) enables the bits; the highest requested and enabled bit wins (bit 7 first), its request bit is cleared and its handler runs from the table EventHandlers ($81C7, 8 words, bit 7 first) through JumpTable $91E2. The mask is kept over the handler, so a handler that narrows it (the redraw jobs allow only bit 7 while they run) narrows it for its own run only: a long job can be cut by a higher one posted meanwhile, since the effect player $B30C calls EventDispatch from its tone loop. 48K c2: 68560 calls, 38 of them ran a handler.
@about The bits: 7 the sprite cache's build ring (SpriteBuildJob, posted by QueueSpriteLoad $945B when a new kind needs its sprite), 6-3 unused (the RET at EventNone), 2 the pod selector's icon (PodIconJob, posted at $A243 and at the play loop's start), 1 the score and the extra life (ScoreJob, $A4AB), 0 an effect (EffectJob, posted by RequestEffect $B2F0).
@about The build ring: DrawQueueRead $8024 (this side) and DrawQueueWrite $8025 (QueueSpriteLoad's side) index 8 entries of 3 bytes at $8026: the kind and the address of its sprite descriptor (the records of 16 bytes at $7EF2). Each entry gets its image built (LoadCellSprite: from the glyphs at $6400 by BuildCellSprite, or a big one by BuildBigSprite) and its pre-shifted copies made (SpriteBuildShifts), then bit 7 of the descriptor's +$0E (waiting) is cleared and the objects of that kind can be drawn. The builds run in the play loop's time, so a new wave's sprites cost no interrupt time.
$AC07 c EventDispatch ; run the highest requested and enabled job; carry set when one ran
> In: EventRequests $81C5, EventMask $81C6. Out: NC (Z) when nothing was pending; carry after a handler ran. Clobbers AF, BC, DE, HL and what the handler uses. Callers: the play loop ($B1E6, 48K c2: 68083 calls) and the effect player's tone loop ($B376, so a long effect does not stop the scheduler). The handler is jumped to through $91E2 with the index of the bit (0 = bit 7), its bit cleared in EventRequests first.
$AC0A EventPending ; the requests
$AC0D EventEnabled ; the enabled ones
$AC0E EventAny ; none: Z and NC
$AC0F ; the mask is kept over the handler
$AC11 ; B counts the shifts: the index of the highest set bit, 0 = bit 7
$AC15 EventFindBit ; A halves until it is not above the requests: B shifts = the bit's index
$AC1A ; AND C: the requests below the winner's bit stay
$AC1E ; the bit's index selects the handler
$AC22 ; jump-by-index
$AC25 EventDone ; the mask back
$AC29 ; carry: a job ran
$AC2B c EventNone ; the handler of the unused bits 6-3: RET
$AC2C c PodIconJob ; event bit 2: the pod selector's icon and its colours
> Allows only bit 7 meanwhile (EventMask AND $80). Attributes $46 (bright yellow) at $5A86-$5A87 and $45 (bright cyan) at $5AA6-$5AA7; the 16 x 16 icon n = the player record's +7 (0-4, the selected pod) from PodIcons + 32 n drawn as two 2-cell rows at $5086 and $50A6 (screen third 2, rows 20-21, columns 6-7) by IconRow. 48K c2: entries through $91E2 (indirect); posted by the play loop's start ($B1C7) and by the pod pick-up ($A243).
$AC33 ; the upper cells bright yellow on black
$AC39 ; the lower cells bright cyan on black
$AC3F ; the player record: +7 the pod selector, 0-4
$AC46 ; three RRCAs and the split: DE = n * 32
$AC4F ; PodIcons + n * 32
$AC54 ; the upper row of the icon, row 16 columns 6-7
$AC5A IconLowerRow ; HL is one cell further twice: + $1E = the next character row
$AC5E c IconRow ; two character cells of 8 lines from DE to the screen at HL
> In: DE the bytes (8 of the left cell, then 8 of the right), HL the screen address of the left cell. Out: DE past the 16 bytes, HL two cells right. Called twice by PodIconJob (the second time by falling in).
$AC60 IconCell
$AC63 IconLine ; one line a pixel row down (INC H)
$AC6F c EffectJob ; event bit 0: play the requested sound effect
> Drops bit 0 from the mask (a second effect request waits; the effect player posts no more of itself) and calls the effect player $B30C, which keeps the scheduler going from its tone loop. Posted by RequestEffect ($B2F0).
$AC79 EffectJobDone
$AC7A c ScoreJob ; event bit 1: print the score, the extra life
> Allows only bit 7 meanwhile. Prints the current player's score (PrintPlayerScore $A4AE with A = CurrentPlayer $7F98) and recomputes WeaponRating ($9B75: the enemy fire and the waves' colours). The extra life: when the record's +1 (the score's high part) is at least ExtraLifeScores[+$0C] ($800F: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, then $FF), +$0C steps on, the lives (+4) + 1 and DrawLives $A4EF shows them. Posted by the score adder ($A4AB).
> On a 128K: the CALL $A4EF at $ACA2, after an extra life is given, becomes CALL HookExtraLife ($B334): the call, then sounds $0F + $10 (witnessed in r1 frame 6590) (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$AC81 ; the score of the player in play
$AC87 ScoreJobRating ; WeaponRating
$AC8A ; the next extra life's threshold: $800F + the record's +$0C
$AC97 ; the record's +1 against it
$AC9C ; one threshold on, one life more
$ACA6 c SpriteBuildJob ; event bit 7: build the sprites waiting in the ring
> Sets the mask to 0 (nothing interrupts it), then builds every entry between DrawQueueRead ($8024) and DrawQueueWrite ($8025): entry k at $8026 + 3 k is the kind and the descriptor's address; LoadCellSprite the image, SpriteBuildShifts the shifted copies, then bit 7 of the descriptor's +$0E (waiting) is cleared. The read index wraps at 8 (RES 3). 48K c2: 5 builds (BuildCellSprite 5 entries, RotateSprite 35).
$ACAD SpriteBuildNext ; the read index against QueueSpriteLoad's
$ACB4 SpriteBuildEmpty ; the ring is empty
$ACB6 ; one entry on, wrapping at 8
$ACB9 ; the entry: 3 bytes an entry
$ACC3 ; A = the type, HL = the record
$ACD0 SpriteBuildDone ; +$0E of the descriptor: bit 7 waiting, cleared
$ACD9 c LoadCellSprite ; build a kind's image from its glyphs
> In: A the kind, HL the descriptor (+0/+1 the image's address). Kinds $14 and up go to BuildBigSprite $8F0C with kind - $13 (not reached in 48K c1 or 48K c2; 48K r1: 6 entries, frames 16855-66273: the big enemies). Else the glyphs are at $6400 + 32 kind (kinds 16-19: 48 bytes each, + 16 (kind - 16) + 32 more), B = 2 cells a column, C = 2 columns or 3 for bit 4 of the kind; BuildCellSprite $8EDB builds the complemented image with blank borders. HL is kept. 48K c2: 5 entries.
$ACDA ; kinds $14 and up: the big sprite
$ACE5 SpriteBuildSmall ; DE = the record's screen address
$ACE9 ; BC = kind * 32
$ACF4 ; kinds 16 and up: 48 bytes each
$AD02 SpriteBuildSource ; + $6400: the kinds' glyphs
$AD08 ; 2 cells by 2 columns; 3 columns for bit 4
$AD11 SpriteBuildDraw
$AD16 c SpriteBuildShifts ; the pre-shifted copies of a sprite: seven one-pixel shifts, a new copy where the pattern says
> In: HL the descriptor. Its +4/+5 (the image's length), +9 (the shift pattern) and +$0A/+$0B (BC for RotateSprite: B the width in bytes, C the height in lines) are written into the operands of LD HL ($AD3E), LD A ($AD47) and LD BC ($AD4A): self-modified code. DE = the image (+0/+1). Seven rounds, one a pixel: the first always, and later each set bit of the pattern (RRA), copy the current image to the next slot (LDIR of the length) and continue on the copy; then RotateSprite $8F5A shifts it one pixel right. A clear bit shifts the last copy on without a new slot, so the pattern chooses which of the 8 pixel positions get an image (8, 4 or 2 copies). 48K c2: 5 entries, RotateSprite 35 times (7 a sprite).
$AD20 ; the length into the LD HL operand
$AD29 ; the bit pattern into the LD A operand
$AD2F ; the count into the LD BC operand
$AD33 ; the first pattern bit; seven rounds
$AD38 SpriteShiftRound
$AD39 ; the next bit of the pattern
$AD3A SpriteShiftTest ; clear: no new copy, shift the last one on
$AD3D SpriteShiftLength ; LD HL,length: the operand written at $AD20
$AD44 SpriteShiftCopy
$AD46 SpriteShiftPattern ; LD A,pattern: the operand written at $AD29
$AD48 SpriteShiftStep
$AD49 SpriteShiftCount ; LD BC,count: the operand written at $AD2F
$AD50 SpriteShiftNext
$AD53 SpriteShiftLoop ; seven rounds
$AD56 c RestoreCheckpoint ; the wave script's state back from the player record
> Copies the player record's +$0D-+$2F (35 bytes, the checkpoint that CmdCheckpoint $AED2 and SetLevelStart $B062 write) to the VM state ScriptState $81D7-$81F9. Called by RoundSetup ($B023), so a level restarts at its last checkpoint after a lost life. 48K c2: 1 entry.
@module wavescript ; the wave script VM: the level's timeline of waves, scroll stops and checkpoints
@about Each level is a script (the twelve at $6B8D-$72A4, F5's data) run by WaveScriptStep once in four passes (8 frames) of play: at the interrupt's phase 2 ($AA96) and only while the player is alive (DeathTimer $7F7B = $FF; the death count-down stops the timeline). The state is 35 bytes at ScriptState $81D7: +1 the PC ($81D8), +3 the scroll flags ($81DA, bit 5 the scroll runs, bit 7 a mode flag), +4 the replay count ($81DB, the columns LevelStart scrolls through at a restart), +8 the call stack pointer ($81DF, growing down from $81F6), +$0A the wait counter ($81E1), +$0B the saved flags ($81E2), +$0C a flag ($81E3). The player record keeps a copy at +$0D (the checkpoint), and RestoreCheckpoint brings it back at each level start.
@about An instruction is a delay byte and an opcode. The wait counter counts frames up to the delay; then the opcode runs and the next instruction is looked at in the same frame (so a run of delay-0 instructions runs at once). Opcodes $00-$3F: a command through ScriptCommands ($81FF, 18 words); $40-$7F: a jump on the condition opcode & $3F to the following word; $80-$BF: a wave: LaunchWave $91EC with A = opcode & $3F and B = the following byte (C = $C0); $C0-$FF: a call on the condition to the following word (the return address pushed on the script stack).
@about The conditions (ScriptConditions $8223, 7 words) test the flags of the last word command, kept in $81E2 and taken into AF': 0 always, 1 Z, 2 NZ, 3 C, 4 NC, 5 P, 6 M. The commands: 0 nothing, 1 $81E3 = $FF, 2 the scroll stops, 3/4 set/clear bit 7 of the scroll flags, 5 the level is done (EndPending = 2), 6 nothing, 7 store a word, 8 copy a word, 9 decrement a word, 10 increment a word, 11 test a word, 12 add to a word, 13 return, 14 the checkpoint, 15 reset the 18 records at $F12C, 16 let the first enemy escape (EnemyEscaped, FreeEnemySlot), 17 test for living enemies.
@about What the twelve scripts use (../zynaps/work/f4/vmwalk.py, a walk of every path from the twelve level entries: 491 instructions cover $6B8D-$72A4 but for the two bytes $FF $00 after each script's level end): 204 waves, 80 stores and 65 decrements of the counter at $81E4, 80 jumps all on NZ (the loops 'repeat n times' and 'wait while enemies live'), 15 enemy tests (17), 11 checkpoints (14), 9 scroll stops (2), 8 record resets (15), 5 escapes (16), 12 level ends (5), 2 nops. Never used: commands 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11, 12, 13, the conditions other than NZ and every call opcode. Their handlers are code no recording ran. 48K r1 (75001 frames, 11 level ends) agrees: it ran the handlers of commands 0, 2, 5, 7, 9, 14, 15, 16 and 17 and no other (WaveScriptStep 9138 entries).
$AD66 c WaveScriptStep ; one frame of the level's script
> Called from the play interrupt at phase 2 ($AA96; 48K c2: 165 entries, one in four of its 659 passes), returns at once unless DeathTimer ($7F7B) is $FF. When the wait counter ($81E1) equals the delay byte at the PC ($81D8): the counter is zeroed, the opcode is read and run, and the next instruction is looked at at once; else the counter + 1 and return. Callees: JumpTable $91E2 (commands), LaunchWave $91EC (waves), ScriptWord, ScriptByte, ScriptTest.
$AD69 ; $FF + 1 = 0: alive
$AD6B ScriptNext ; the wait counter against this instruction's delay
$AD75 ; the delay is over: the counter from 0 for the next one
$AD79 ; the opcode after the delay byte
$AD7F ; $00-$3F: a command
$AD83 ; ScriptCommands, jump-by-index
$AD89 ; and the next instruction in the same frame
$AD8B ScriptWait ; one frame more
$AD90 ScriptDecode ; $40-$7F: a jump
$AD94 ; $80-$BF: a wave
$AD98 ; $C0-$FF: a call: DE = the target
$AD9D ; the condition (opcode & $3F) false: NC
$ADA2 ; the return address on the script stack ($81DF, growing down)
$ADB0 ; PC = the target
$ADB6 ScriptJump ; DE = the target
$ADBB ; the condition false: on with the next instruction
$ADC6 ScriptWave ; A = the wave's kind, C = the following byte
$ADCB ; B = the byte, C = $C0
$ADCE ; LaunchWave
$ADD3 c ScriptSaveFlags ; keep F of the last word command for the conditions
> PUSH AF / POP HL takes F into L, stored in ScriptFlags $81E2 (the conditions read $81E2/$81E3 as an AF pair). Called by the word commands 9-12 and 17.
$ADDC c ScriptWord ; DE = the next word of the script
> Reads the word at the PC ($81D8) and steps the PC past it; HL kept.
$ADE9 c ScriptByte ; C = the next byte of the script
> Reads the byte at the PC and steps it; HL kept.
$ADF4 c ScriptTest ; carry when the condition A holds
> In: A the condition (0-6). Out: carry when it holds; A, BC, DE, HL kept (A is the condition again). The saved flags $81E2/$81E3 are loaded into AF' and the test routine (ScriptConditions $8223 through $91E2) takes them with EX AF,AF'.
$ADF9 ; ScriptFlags and $81E3 as AF
$ADFD ; into AF' for the test routine
$AE00 ; ScriptConditions, jump-by-index
$AE0C c CondAlways ; condition 0: always (SCF)
$AE0E c CondZero ; condition 1: Z of the saved flags
$AE13 c CondNonZero ; condition 2: NZ of the saved flags (the only one the scripts use)
$AE18 c CondPositive ; condition 5: P of the saved flags
$AE1D c CondNegative ; condition 6: M of the saved flags
$AE22 c CondCarry ; condition 3: the saved carry
$AE24 c CondNoCarry ; condition 4: the saved carry inverted
$AE27 c CmdNop ; command 0: nothing (2 uses)
$AE28 c CmdSetFlag ; command 1: $81E3 = $FF (never used by the scripts)
> $81E3 is the byte after ScriptFlags, so it is A of the AF pair the conditions load; SetLevelStart zeroes it.
$AE2E c CmdScrollStop ; command 2: the scroll stops (bit 5 of $81DA cleared; 9 uses)
> Also called by CmdLevelEnd. The scroll ($8AED area) runs while bit 5 is set; a level's guardian section stops it.
$AE34 c CmdModeOn ; command 3: bit 7 of the scroll flags $81DA set (never used)
$AE3A c CmdModeOff ; command 4: bit 7 of the scroll flags cleared (never used)
$AE40 c CmdLevelEnd ; command 5: the level is done
> The scroll stops, EndPending ($7F7C) = 2 (the play loop ends with code 2: LevelDone $B174 moves to the next level), the player record's +8 and +9 zeroed (the side weapon and the double plasma do not go on to the next level). The last instruction of all twelve scripts.
$AE54 c CmdNop2 ; command 6: nothing (never used)
$AE55 c CmdStore ; command 7: (word 1) = word 2 (80 uses, all on the counter $81E4)
$AE60 c CmdCopy ; command 8: (word 1) = (word 2) (never used)
$AE6D c CmdDecrement ; command 9: (word) - 1, the flags kept for the conditions (65 uses)
> The word is read, ADC HL,DE with HL = $FFFF gives word - 1 with Z when it reaches 0 (and carry unless it was 0); the flags go to ScriptFlags. With a following 'jump NZ' it is the loop 'repeat n times'.
$AE84 c CmdIncrement ; command 10: (word) + 1, the flags kept (never used)
$AE9B c CmdTest ; command 11: the flags of (word) (0 + word with ADC; never used)
$AEAC c CmdAdd ; command 12: the flags of (word 1) + word 2 (never used)
> It keeps the flags of the sum but stores word 2 itself, not the sum (LD (HL),E / LD (HL),D after POP HL): a command no script uses, so the slip never shows.
$AEC3 c CmdReturn ; command 13: return from a script call (never used)
$AED2 c CmdCheckpoint ; command 14: the checkpoint (11 uses)
> Copies the VM state $81D7-$81F9 (35 bytes, PC past this instruction) to the player record's +$0D: a lost life restarts the level here (RestoreCheckpoint, then LevelStart replays the scroll count). Measured (../zynaps/work/f4/invuln.py): level 0's checkpoint moved from $6B8D (replay $40) to $6BC4 with the replay count $122 between frames 1000 and 2000; with no checkpoint passed a death restarted the script at $6B8D.
$AEE3 c CmdResetRecords ; command 15: reset the 18 records of 32 bytes at $F12C (8 uses)
> For each record: +8 = 0, +6/+7 = $85E4; then of the 32 bytes below its pointer (+0/+1) every one whose byte $40 higher is 0 is cleared. F6's buffers at $F12C; the pointers lead into the map work space.
$AEEF ResetRecord ; +8 = 0, +6/+7 = $85E4
$AF08 ResetRecordByte ; the byte $40 above is 0: clear this one
$AF0C ResetRecordNext
$AF16 c CmdEnemyEscape ; command 16: when the enemy record $F398 is active, EnemyEscaped $994A and FreeEnemySlot $992A (5 uses)
$AF26 c CmdEnemiesLeft ; command 17: NZ while one of the five enemies at $F398 lives (15 uses)
> A record counts when +8 has bits 5 and 7 set and +$0D is not $FF. The flags go to ScriptFlags: the script's 'jump NZ' back waits for the wave to be shot down.
$AF2F EnemiesLeftNext ; bits 5 and 7 of +8: a live enemy
$AF40 EnemiesLeftSkip
$AF45 EnemiesLeftDone ; NZ: one found, Z: none
@module rounds ; the level start, the player records, the title pages
@about LevelStart prints the level's banner and scrolls the playfield to where the level (re)starts: the checkpoint keeps the number of columns already scrolled ($81DB), and the start replays them, the ones more than $40 back without drawing (ReplayFast $7F76 = $FF), the last $40 drawn. So a restart after a lost life is the same scroll run again, not a saved screen.
@about PlayersSetup builds the two player records ($7F9C and $7FCC, 48 bytes each: +1 the score's high part, +4 the lives, +5 the deaths, +6 the level order index, +7 the pod selector, +8 the side weapon, +9 the double plasma, +$0A the laser level 0-3, +$0B the speed $40-$A0, +$0C the extra-life index, +$0D-+$2F the script checkpoint) and shuffles the middle levels: LevelOrder ($8003, 12 bytes) keeps levels 0-2 and 9-11 in place and draws a new order of 3-8 for every game from Random $8544. SetLevelStart puts a level's 8-byte entry ($6B2D + 8 n, F5's table) and the stack set-up ($81FA) into a record's checkpoint. RoundSetup does the rest of a level's start.
@about The title pages: CreditsPage (message $15, both players' last scores, the title tune, the menu's key wait) and HiScorePage (message $16, the table of five at $8261, 500 frames); MainLoop shows them in turn until fire starts a game.
$AF49 c LevelStart ; the level banner and the scroll to the start point
> LevelSetUp $8C3B (the playfield model, the map buffer), MenuScreenSetup $A685, sets the print position ($80B0 = $60, $80B9 = $4B), prints message $18 ($A423), sets $801F = $10 and $801E = $60, StartScript $8D35 (the scenery script); then ReplayScroll. Called by RoundSetup; 48K c2: 1 entry.
$AF4F ; the print position
$AF59 ; message $18: the level banner
$AF6B ReplayScroll ; the columns already scrolled before the checkpoint
$AF6E ; zeroed: the scroll counts them again
$AF79 ; up to $40 of them drawn
$AF80 ; $40 or fewer: all drawn
$AF85 ReplayFastPart ; the ones before the last $40 with ReplayFast = $FF: nothing drawn
$AF8C ; then the last $40 drawn
$AF8F ReplayDrawn ; ReplayFast = 0
$AF90 c ReplayColumns ; scroll BC columns, ReplayFast = A
> A is stored in ReplayFast ($7F76, read by $8DCA: $FF skips the drawing). Each column: StartColumn $894D and three rounds of ResumeScript $8D45 (the scenery script) and BuildColumn $896D, then ScrollStep $8AED scrolls one column (and counts $81DB up). 48K c2: AF9F 192 rounds at a level start.
$AF94 ; the first column without the scroll
$AF96 ReplayColumn
$AF97 ; one column scrolled
$AF9A ReplayStep
$AF9F ReplayRound
$AFA3 ReplayRoundEnd
$AFB0 c InterruptSetup ; the IM 2 table and a clear screen, once at the start
> SetUpInterrupts $8516 (the IM 2 table) and ClearScreen $858B; called once by MainLoop (48K c1: 1 entry).
> On a 128K: the glue makes the CALL $858B at $AFB3 CALL ClearThenMusic ($5B46) (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$AFB7 c PlayersSetup ; both player records and the level order of a new game
> CurrentPlayer $7F98 = 1, PlayerRecord $7F99 = $7F9C; both records (96 bytes from $7F9C) zeroed; player 1's lives = 2, player 2's = 3 when Players ($7F9B) is 2, else 0; the speed +$0B = $40 and the laser level +$0A = 1 for both; SetLevelStart for each (both at level 0). Then LevelOrder $8006-$800B: six different numbers 3-8 drawn from Random $8544 (one game measured: 07 06 05 04 08 03, ../zynaps/work/f4/invuln.py). Called by MainLoop.
$AFD4 ; Players: two players give player 2 three lives (the extra one since player 1 is in play first)
$AFE0 ; the speed $40: the first speed step
$AFE8 ; the laser level 1
$AFFE ; LevelOrder + 3: the six shuffled levels
$B003 ShuffleDraw ; the slot zeroed, a new number 0-5 from the random generator
$B00E ; 3-8
$B014 ShuffleCheck ; against the slots filled so far (and the zeroed one)
$B01A ; a new number: kept
$B023 c RoundSetup ; a level's start: the checkpoint, the objects, the banner, the panel
> RestoreCheckpoint, EnemiesSetup, ShotsSetup, PlayerSetup; the player's digit '0' + Player at $84BB (the banner's text), LevelStart; then RoundPlaySetup ($B03A). Called by MainLoop before each play loop (48K c2: 1 entry).
> On a 128K: the glue makes the CALL $AD56 at $B023 CALL LevelStartSound ($5B6D): the music off, sound $10 (the engine's $C9A5), then $AD56 (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$B02F ; '1' or '2' into the banner
$B03A RoundPlaySetup ; the panel and the frame state
> WaitMenuRasterEnd $A595, ClearHitGrid $91C6 with interrupts off, Fuel = $5A, DeathTimer = $FF (the script runs), ClearScreen $858B, RestorePanel $A979, both scores (PrintPlayerScore $A4AE with 1 and 2), the lives (DrawLives $A4EF), WeaponRating $9B75.
$B042 ; Fuel: $5A steps ($9A6D counts it down when bit 0 of $7F63 is set; about 29 s, F2's measure)
$B047 ; DeathTimer: the script and the controls run
$B052 ; both scores
$B062 c SetLevelStart ; a level's start into a player record's checkpoint
> In: IX the record. +5 = 0, $81E3 = 0; the record's +$0D gets the 8 bytes of the level entry $6B2D + 8 * LevelOrder[+6] (the ScriptState $81D7-$81DE: +1 the PC, +3 the scroll flags $FF, +4 the replay count), then the 5 bytes at $81FA (the stack pointer $81F6, zeros). Called by PlayersSetup for both players and by LevelDone.
$B06F ; LevelOrder[+6]
$B078 ; $6B25 + 8 * (order + 1): the entry of that level
$B07B LevelEntryFind
$B08C ; the script stack's start ($81FA: the pointer $81F6 and 3 zeros)
$B095 c CreditsPage ; the title page with both players' scores and the tune
> ClearScreen $858B, PrintMessage $15, the scores of both players at $5AA0 and $5AB8 (PrintBcd $A43E of record +0, C = '0') with the colour $05, plays the title tune (TitleTune $B446, until fire or keys 1-5), MenuKeys $A76F, then TitleFireCheck ($A748: carry = fire). 48K c1: 2 entries. Called by MainLoop.
$B09D ; the colour of the scores
$B0A3 ; player 1's score at row 21, column 0
$B0B1 ; player 2's score at row 21, column 24
$B0BF ; the tune: back at fire or a key 1-5
$B0C9 c HiScorePage ; the high score table, 500 frames or fire
> ClearScreen, PrintMessage $16, the five entries of HiScoreTable $8261 (14 bytes each: the 10-byte print string with the initials through PrintString $A401, then the 4 BCD bytes through PrintBcd $A43E with C = '.') in $46; then 500 HALTs with TitleFireCheck polled. Out: carry when fire was pressed. 48K c1: 1 entry, 30 permille of the recording (the page's 10 s wait).
$B0D6 ; the table: five entries
$B0DB HiScoreEntry
$B0EE ; 500 frames
$B0F1 HiScoreWait
$B0FE b HiScoreSpare ; a lone RET ($C9) no code reaches
@module mainloop ; the game's top loop, the play loop, the end of a life or level, the object set-up
@about MainLoop: InterruptSetup once, then CreditsPage and HiScorePage in turn until fire (carry); PlayersSetup; then for every life or level RoundSetup, PlayLoop, EndOfPlay; NC from EndOfPlay goes on with the next round, carry (the game is over) back to the title pages. AbortGame (key Q in the pause) comes back with SP reset and lands on the title pages.
@about PlayLoop: the frame rate counters, the events (only bit 2 posted, the pod icon), the play state zeroed, the play handler $A9ED installed, then EventDispatch until EndCode ($7F7D) is set by CheckPauseAndEnd. Everything else in play runs in the interrupt and in the jobs. EndOfPlay waits 50 frames, fades the screen and dispatches on the end code: 1 PlayerDied, 2 LevelDone, 3 and 4 AbortGame.
$B0FF c EndOfPlay ; after the play loop: dispatch on the end code
> In: A the end code (1-4). WaitFrames $A6AE for 50 frames, FadeAttributes $85AD, then EndHandlers ($B111) through $91E2 with code - 1. Out: carry when the game is over (from PlayerDied). Called by MainLoop.
$B10A ; EndHandlers, jump-by-index
$B111 w EndHandlers ; the end codes 1-4: PlayerDied, LevelDone, AbortGame, AbortGame
$B119 c PlayerDied ; end code 1: a life lost
> The record's deaths +5 + 1, +8 and +9 zeroed (the side weapon and the double plasma are lost), FuelOut $9C03 (Fuel = $5A, the laser level and the speed one step down). With no lives left: HighScoreEntry $A7F7, NextPlayer, and carry when the lives went negative (the game is over); with lives: NextPlayer and NC. 48K c2: 1 entry (indirect).
$B12A ; no lives left: the high score check
$B13A ; NextPlayer took a life: negative = no player has one
$B142 PlayerDiedLives
$B147 PlayerDiedOver ; carry: the game is over
$B149 c NextPlayer ; the other player's turn, one life less
> One player: the life is taken from the current record. Two: when the other record has lives, it becomes PlayerRecord and Player flips (1 <-> 2); a life of the record in play then is taken ($B170).
> With INF.LIVES (on either machine) the set-up makes the DEC (IX+4) at $B170 OR (IX+4): the lives never drop (src/game/setup.asm).
$B15E NextPlayerCheck ; the other player's lives
$B170 TakeLife ; DEC lives: -1 is the game's end
$B174 c LevelDone ; end code 2: the next level
> The record's +6 + 1, wrapping from 12 to 0, SetLevelStart. Not in 48K c1 or 48K c2; 48K r1 (the long bot run, 75001 frames) ran it 11 times (frames 6257-67115); seen in ../zynaps/work/f4/invuln.py (Invulnerable on, 30000 frames): level 0 ended near frame 6500, 1 near 14500, 2 near 20500, 3 near 27500, and with LevelOrder 00 01 02 07 06 05 04 08 03 09 0A 0B the scripts ran $6B8D, $6C1A, $6C97, $70F6 (order 7), $7068 (order 6).
$B183 LevelDoneStore ; 12 wraps to level 0
$B18A c AbortGame ; end codes 3 and 4: back to the title pages
> DI, TitleInterrupt $A9E4, SP = $5C00 (whatever the stack held), JP MainLoopTitle: CreditsPage comes next. Code 4 is set by key Q in the pause.
$B197 c Pause ; the pause: fire resumes, Q aborts
> StopAndSettle, then a loop: a delay of $2000 rounds, TitleFireCheck ($A748: fire, back to play), key Q (half row $FBFE, bit 0): EndPending = 4 (the play loop ends, AbortGame). Called by CheckPauseAndEnd on key 4, inside the play interrupt: the whole game stands. Measured (../zynaps/work/f4/pause.py): after key 4, PassCount $7F62 stayed $4D and the script PC $6B96 for 400 frames; Q gave $7F7C/$7F7D = 4/4, Handler $A9E4 and the credits page.
> On a 128K: the glue makes the LD ($7F7C),A at $B1B0 JP Key4MusicOn ($5B82): the store, then the music on (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$B19A PauseLoop
$B19D PauseDelay
$B1A6 ; key Q
$B1AE ; EndPending = 4: abort
$B1B4 c PlayLoop ; a life or a level of play
> FrameRate $7F6A = $C8, $7F6B = 8; EventRequests = 0 and EventMask = $FF, then bit 2 posted (the pod icon); $7F65 (the interrupt phase), $7F64, CurrentEffect $7F97, EndPending, EndCode and $974C zeroed; one frame, the play handler $A9ED; then EventDispatch round after round (48K c2: 68083) until EndCode is set. The play interrupt does the frame's work meanwhile. Then TitleInterrupt, SavePanel $A98D, and A = EndCode. Called by MainLoop.
$B1BE ; L = 0 the requests, H = $FF the mask
$B1C4 ; bit 2: draw the pod icon first
$B1DF ; the play handler
$B1E6 PlayLoopPass ; one pass: the highest job
$B1E9 PlayLoopCheck ; EndCode: set by CheckPauseAndEnd
$B1EC PlayLoopTest
$B1ED PlayLoopBranch
$B1EF PlayLoopAgain
$B1F1 PlayLoopEnd ; TitleInterrupt again
$B200 c MainLoop ; the game's top loop
> Entered from Start ($B72C). InterruptSetup, then the title pages until fire, PlayersSetup, and the rounds of RoundSetup, PlayLoop and EndOfPlay. Never returns.
> On a 128K: the glue makes the CALL $AFB0 at $B200 CALL MusicOn ($5B49, the music on); the menu loop's JR $B203 at $B21A becomes JR $B200, so after a game it passes that CALL again: the music starts again (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
> With K-MOUSE the CALL $B1B4 at $B212 becomes CALL MouseReset ($FE00) at a life's start (src/game/setup.asm).
$B203 MainLoopTitle ; NC: the title pages (AbortGame and the end of a game come here)
$B20C ; fire: a new game
$B20F MainLoopRound
$B212 MainLoopPlay
$B215 MainLoopEnd
$B218 ; NC: the next life or level
$B21A ; carry: the game is over
$B21C c PlayerSetup ; the player's ship and the objects tied to it
> The ship's record $F36C: X 0/$14, Y 0/8, active (bit 7 of +8), +$25 = $68; $F37C = $20, $F380 = $40; the record $F600 inactive; $80BF = 0. Then eight pairs of words below $80E0 written by PUSHes with SP moved there (DE = $0800, HL = $1400: 32 bytes $80C0-$80DF) with interrupts off. $F5D4 and $F5A8 inactive. When the record's side weapon +8 is 2 or more (2 plasma, 3 homing missiles, 4 seeker), +8 = 0 and its pod action runs again through the pod table $80F0 (PodPlasma, PodHoming, PodSeeker), which arms it anew for the new ship. Called by RoundSetup.
$B230 ; active
$B24E ; interrupts off: SP is borrowed
$B24F ; SP into the operand of LD SP at $B262
$B256 ; eight times the pair $1400, $0800 down from $80E0
$B25E FillPairs
$B262 FillRestoreSP ; LD SP,old: the operand written at $B24F
$B276 ; the side weapon +8: 2 or more
$B284 ; the pod actions at $80F0, JumpTable on the side weapon
$B28B c EnemiesSetup ; the five enemy records inactive, then the pod objects
> The five records of $2C bytes at $F398: inactive (bit 7 of +8), +$26 = $E0, +$27 = $80. Then PodsSetup. Called by RoundSetup.
$B291 EnemiesSetupNext
$B2AA c PodsSetup ; the three objects at $F474 tied to the ship
> Three records of $2C bytes at $F474: +$25 = $48, kind +7 = 5, inactive, +$29/+$2A = $F36C (the ship's record).
$B2B0 PodsSetupNext
$B2BD ; the ship's record: a pointer
$B2CF c ShotsSetup ; the four shot records inactive
> $80BA = 4; four records of $2C bytes at $F4F8: +$0B = $80, +$25 = $78, inactive.
$B2DA ShotsSetupNext
@module effects ; the sound effects: requests by priority, a beeper player inside the scheduler
@about On a 128K none of this module runs: the glue puts a RET at RequestEffect and EffectPlay, the hook stubs overwrite $B30D-$B3A8, and the game's events reach the page-4 engine through the stubs (docs/09-sound.md). On a 48K the 2 in 1 plays these effects as the 48K did.
@about An effect is asked for with RequestEffect (A = the effect, 0 = stop): it is taken only when not lower than the one playing (CurrentEffect $7F97), which gives the effects their priority by number. The request posts event bit 0, and EffectJob runs EffectPlay in the play loop's time, not the interrupt's: the beeper loop then fills the frame and returns to the scheduler at every interrupt (EffectTick $7F96 = $FF, set by the play interrupt at $AA18), so the jobs go on under the sound.
@about The effect table is F5's at $7E5A, 8 bytes an effect from effect 1: +0 pitch A, +1 its step, +2 pitch B, +3 its step, +4 the change of both steps at each segment's end, +5 the segments, +6 the flags, +7 the segment lengths (low nibble A, high nibble B, in frames). The flags: bit 7 two-tone (each period's high half on pitch A, its low half on pitch B), bit 6 the pitches back to their start at each segment, bit 5 segment A one frame shorter each time (down to 1), bit 4 / bit 3 step B / step A reversed at each segment, bits 0-2 (one tone) which pitch a segment plays: bit 0 on even segment counts, bit 1 on odd, bit 2 the last.
@about The tone: a square wave on the beeper ($FE bit 4: OUT $18, then $00, the border black), each half a count of NOP rounds (three NOPs and DJNZ at $B360-$B363, 25 T a round): a pitch value p gives about 3500000 / (2 x 25 p) Hz, a little lower for the loop's overhead. 48K c2: 479160 entries at $B360 against 9788 periods at $B358.
$B2F0 c RequestEffect ; ask for effect A (0 = stop), taken when not below the one playing
> In: A the effect, 0 is stored as $80 (above every effect: a stop always wins). Taken when A >= CurrentEffect ($7F97): CurrentEffect = A, EffectTick = $80 (new effect), event bit 0 posted. HL kept. Callers (48K c2): FireLaser $9C1D x61, $A2D6 x2, $A0EB x1, StopAndSettle x2; in the listing also A = 1 ($A1C2), 2 ($9C75, the laser: FireLaser), 4 ($A31B), 5 ($A224, a pod) and 6 ($A21D).
> On a 128K: the glue makes its first byte a RET: the beeper effects are off (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$B2F7 ; 0: the stop, above all
$B2F9 RequestEffectCheck ; lower than the one playing: dropped
$B2FD ; EffectTick = $80: a new effect
$B302 ; event bit 0: EffectJob
$B30A RequestEffectDone
$B30C c EffectPlay ; play CurrentEffect until it ends or a new request
> Entered from EffectJob (event bit 0; 48K c2: 106 entries in 73 frames, 45 from EffectJob and 61 restarts by a new request at $B388). n = CurrentEffect & $1F, 0 ends at once; the entry $7E5A + 8 (n - 1) is copied to the effect state $7F8A-$7F90 (+0-+6) and its byte 7 split into SegLenA $7F91 and SegLenB $7F92; the pitches PitchA $7F93 and PitchB $7F94 start from +0 and +2, the frame counter $7F95 from SegLenA. The tone loop runs until an interrupt sets EffectTick, then calls EventDispatch (the jobs run) and steps the pitches; at a segment's end the segment's changes apply. Ends (CurrentEffect = 0) when the segments run out or a job ran (the carry from EventDispatch: an effect stops for the redraw jobs and the next effect request starts it again). EffectTick = $80 restarts with the new effect.
> On a 128K: the glue makes its first byte a RET, and the set-up copies the hook stubs over $B30D-$B3A8 (src/game/stubs.asm) (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$B311 ; effect 0: nothing to play
$B314 ; (n - 1) * 8
$B31B ; the effect table, F5's
$B322 ; +0-+6 into the state
$B327 ; +7: SegLenA the low nibble, SegLenB the high
$B332 ; C = the flags for the whole effect
$B336 ; PitchA = +0, PitchB = +2
$B342 ; the first segment's frames
$B345 EffectFirstLen
$B348 ; two-tone: the low half on PitchB (DE = $7F94), else both halves on PitchA
$B355 EffectFirst ; B bit 0 = 1: the first segment plays pitch A
$B358 EffectPeriod ; the alternate set: HL the high half's pitch, DE the low half's
$B359 EffectHalves ; two halves
$B35B EffectSpeakerOn ; the speaker bit on
$B35D EffectHalf ; B = the half's count
$B35E EffectOut
$B360 EffectWait ; three NOPs and DJNZ: 25 T a round
$B361 EffectWait1
$B362 EffectWait2
$B363 EffectWait3
$B365 EffectSwap ; the other half's pitch
$B366 EffectLow ; the speaker bit off
$B368 EffectHalfNext
$B369 EffectHalfLoop
$B36B EffectTickCheck
$B36C EffectTickRead ; EffectTick: 0 until the interrupt
$B36F EffectTickTest
$B370 EffectTickBranch
$B372 ; the registers of the loop kept over the scheduler
$B376 ; the jobs run in the effect's time
$B379 EffectAfterJobs
$B37A EffectRestore
$B37D ; a job ran: the effect ends
$B380 ; EffectTick back to 0
$B386 ; $80: a new effect was asked for
$B38A ; two-tone: both pitches step
$B39E EffectStepOne ; one tone: the pitch this segment plays steps
$B3AE EffectStepAdd
$B3B0 EffectFrame ; the segment's frames
$B3B6 ; a segment over: one fewer
$B3BD ; both steps + the change (+4)
$B3CD ; bit 5: SegLenA one shorter, not below 1
$B3D9 EffectReverseA ; bit 3: step A reversed (two-tone, or a pitch-A segment)
$B3E5 EffectNegateA
$B3EC EffectReverseB ; bit 4: step B reversed (two-tone, or a pitch-B segment)
$B3F8 EffectNegateB
$B3FF EffectRestart ; bit 6: the pitches back to their start
$B40F EffectNextSeg ; the next segment's halves and length
$B41A ; two-tone: both halves, SegLenA
$B41E ; one tone: the segment count's parity picks a flag bit
$B42A EffectPickBit
$B42C EffectPick ; bit 0 of B: pitch A (SegLenA) or pitch B (SegLenB) on both halves
$B438 EffectPitchB
$B43B EffectSegLen ; the segment's frames
$B441 EffectEnd ; CurrentEffect = 0
@module tune ; the title tune: two detuned square waves, the interrupts off
@about On a 128K the tune is silent: its DI is a NOP and its OUT a NOP: NOP, so its loop only times the credits page while the interrupt plays the engine's music. On a 48K it plays as in the 48K.
@about TitleTune plays the tune of the credits page with the interrupts off: TuneOrder ($FF1C) lists 7 patterns (A A B A C C D), a pattern is note bytes and duration commands up to $FF. A command $C0+n sets the note length to n x 16 x 256 loop passes (TuneDuration $B4AE, the low byte $B4AD stays 0); a note n takes the period p = TunePeriods[n] ($FF01) and runs two counters, p and p + 1 passes: each wrap toggles the speaker (both at once toggle it once), so one note sounds as two tones a pass apart, a slow beat. A pass is 81 T (uncontended code at $B486-$B49B), so the speaker, the XOR of the two counters, gives a pulse train at 3500000 / (81 p) Hz on a 48K (two pulses per counter period; the 48K case's beeper capture confirms it, ../zynaps/docs/09-sound.md 9.3): from 179 Hz (p = 242, note 1) to 800 Hz (p = 54, note 26). The periods fall by about a semitone a step (notes 24 to 25 a whole tone); the patterns use notes 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9-14, 16-21, 23 and 25, never 3, 5, 8, 15, 22, 24 and 26 (src/res/music/titletune.tune.txt).
@about Between notes it checks fire (TitleFireCheck $A748) and the keys 1-5 (half row $F7FE): either ends the tune and the credits page goes on to the menu. 48K c1: 2 entries, 230 permille of the attract recording (269491882 T in the tune's own code). The text form: src/res/music/titletune.tune.txt (tools/res_tune.py).
$B446 c TitleTune ; play the title tune until fire or a key 1-5
> DI; for each of the 7 patterns of TuneOrder: the notes until $FF, the length commands, the keys checked before each byte. EI and return. Called by CreditsPage ($B0BF). Reads $FF1C-$FFD3, TunePeriods, TuneDuration; ports: IN $F7FE, OUT $FE (bit 4 only: $00, $10).
> On a 128K: the glue makes its DI a NOP (the tune runs with the interrupts on, so the engine keeps playing), and the OUT ($FE),A at $B496 NOP: NOP: the tune is silent (src/game/setup.asm, glue.asm; docs/09-sound.md 9.2).
$B447 ; TuneOrder: 7 pattern pointers
$B44C TunePattern ; DE = the pattern
$B450 TuneByte ; fire ends the tune
$B457 ; the keys 1-5 end it too
$B460 TuneRead
$B463 ; $FF: the pattern's end
$B466 ; $C0+: a length
$B46A ; (cmd & 15) * 16: the passes' high byte
$B473 TuneNote ; the note's period
$B47E ; the second counter one pass longer
$B480 ; DE = the length in passes
$B484 ; H, L: the two counters
$B486 TunePass ; counter H: at 0 it reloads and toggles
$B48C TuneCounterL ; counter L likewise
$B492 TuneMix ; + 8 and AND $10: a wrap flips the speaker bit, A >> 1 keeps it for the next pass
$B49A ; the length
$B4A5 TuneNextPattern
$B4A7 TuneEnd
$B4A9 b TuneSpare ; AND A; RET and two zeros no code reaches
$B4AD w TuneDuration ; the note length in loop passes: the high byte set by the $C0+ commands, the low byte 0
@module icons ; the five icons of the pod selector
@about Five 16 x 16 pictures, one for each pod action the selector offers (record +7): 0 speed-up, 1 laser, 2 plasma, 3 homing missiles, 4 seeker (the pod table $80F0), drawn by PodIconJob into the panel at rows 20-21, columns 6-7 in bright yellow over bright cyan. A preview: ../zynaps/work/f4/icons.preview.png.
$B4AF b PodIcons 8 ; five 16 x 16 icons, 32 bytes each: the left cell's 8 lines, the right's, then the lower row's two
> Drawn by PodIconJob at rows 20-21 ($5086/$50A6), columns 6-7, icon n = the player record's +7 (the selected pod, 0-4). Bytes in the order IconRow reads them: TL, TR, BL, BR, each top line first.
$B4AF ; icon 0: speed-up
$B4CF ; icon 1: laser
$B4EF ; icon 2: plasma
$B50F ; icon 3: homing missiles
$B52F ; icon 4: seeker
@module font ; the game's own glyphs for the print codes $80-$B5
@about The print routine's characters above $7F: 54 glyphs, the title logo's pieces. Rows 1-4 of the PNG (codes $80-$A3) are the pieces of the ZYNAPS logo, rows 5-6 (codes $A4-$B5) the rest of the logo and the HEWSON name in its lettering (row 5). The editable form is src/res/fonts/glyphs.font.png (tools/res_font.py, 9 glyphs a row); the codes below $80 print with the ROM's character set.
$B54F b Glyphs 8 ; 54 glyphs of 8 x 8, the print codes $80-$B5
> The print routine ($A321) takes HL = code * 8 + $B14F, so code $80 is $B54F and $B5 the last ($B6F7). The editable form: src/res/fonts/glyphs.font.png (tools/res_font.py), 9 glyphs a row.
@res fonts/glyphs.bin
@module start ; the start-up from the set-up
@about Start runs once, from GameEntry. It moves the block block 2 put at $BC00-$C7FF down to $5C00-$67FF and turns the two graphics areas block 2 holds inverted the right way round, then enters MainLoop. Its own bytes after $B6FF are gone at the first level: $B700-$BFFF is the playfield's map buffer, cleared by $8C3B at every LevelStart ($8CC0: 2304 bytes from $B700) and written by the scroll ($8AED, 48K c2: writes $B700-$BFFF).
$B6FF c Start ; the start: the code below the screen, the complemented graphics, the main loop
> Entered by GameEntry's jump ($B6FF). DI, SP = $5C00; $BC00-$C7FF (3072 bytes) copied to $5C00-$67FF (the code and data block 2 put above, now in their run place below $6800); the bytes $E000-$E747 (1864) and $F048-$F107 (192) complemented (block 2 holds them inverted); JP MainLoop. Overwritten from $B700 on by the playfield buffer at the first level start, so no restart comes back here (AbortGame jumps to MainLoopTitle).
$B700 ; the stack under the copied block
$B703 ; 3072 bytes from $BC00 to $5C00
$B70E ; 1864 bytes from $E000 complemented
$B714 StartInvertA
$B71D ; 192 bytes from $F048 complemented
$B723 StartInvertB
$B72C ; MainLoop
@module tunedata ; the title tune's notes: the periods, the order, the four patterns
@about The data TitleTune plays, at the top of memory after the screen buffers: TunePeriods (27 periods, index 0 unused), TuneOrder (7 pattern pointers), the patterns A-D. 211 bytes; the editable form is src/res/music/titletune.tune.txt.
$FF01 b TunePeriods 9 ; the period of each note number, in loop passes (index 0 unused)
@res music/titletune.bin
$FF1C w TuneOrder 7 ; the patterns in play order: A A B A C C D
@res music/titletune.bin
$FF2A b TunePatternA 8 ; pattern A (played 3 times): the opening phrase
@res music/titletune.bin
$FF61 b TunePatternB 8 ; pattern B: the climbing arpeggios
@res music/titletune.bin
$FF9C b TunePatternC 8 ; pattern C (twice): the ostinato
@res music/titletune.bin
$FFAE b TunePatternD 8 ; pattern D: the closing phrase
@res music/titletune.bin
@module topcells ; the developer switch and the work cells at the top of memory
@about The tune data ends at $FFD3. $FFD4-$FFD8 are the 48K tape's bytes (what its loader left of the ROM font's 'P' and the first line of 'Q'); from $FFD9 to $FFFE the 2 in 1 holds zeros, where the 48K tape had the rest of the font's 'Q'-'U', and $FFFF (past the 48K tape's block, which ends at $FFFE) holds $73. The game uses three parts as variables: Invulnerable ($FFD7, 0), WorkGlyph ($FFD8, 8 bytes) and ColumnFlags ($FFE0, 32 bytes to $FFFF).
$FFD4 b FontLeftover ; the lines 4-6 of the ROM's 'P' ($7C, $40, $40), no code reads them
$FFD7 b Invulnerable ; the developer switch: not 0 = the ship cannot be killed
> Read only at $9B4C, the collision's death path: when the ship is hit and Invulnerable is not 0, the death count-down ($9B5F, DeathTimer = $16) is skipped and the ship flies on. Never written by the game: 0 in block 2 (the 'P' glyph's empty last line), so a POKE 65495,1 makes the ship immortal; on a 128K into RAM page 0 (a write while the engine's page 4 is in at $C000 misses it). Witnessed here in r1 and r2: no ship lost (docs/09-sound.md 9.2). Measured (../zynaps/work/f4/invuln.py from ../zynaps/work/explore/play-start.szx, no input): with 0 the idle ship died at frames 300-400, 900-1000 and 1400-1500 (deaths 3); with 1 no death in 30000 frames, through levels 0-3 into level 4.
$FFD8 b WorkGlyph 8 ; an 8-byte work cell: $8EC5 copies a glyph of $72A9 + 8 n here, $8EA1 draws it (zero in block 2)
$FFE0 b ColumnFlags 8 ; 32 flags a screen column (to $FFFF, the image's last byte): cleared by $8C3B at each level start ($8CD9, LDIR of 32 bytes $FFE0-$FFFF), $89C9 sets bits in (H = $FF, L = the object's +$11); zero in block 2 but the last byte, $73 at $FFFF (past the 48K tape's block; the level start's clear makes it 0 before any read)
@isub $B1BE LD HL,$FF00
# F6: the data $B72F-$FF00 (buffers, sprite images, row and object records, pools, tables, the interrupt vectors).
# Evidence: tools/l1info.py over ../zynaps/work/c1 (load, attract, 1 game) and ../zynaps/work/c2 (1500 frames of level 1); the image as
# the loader leaves it (../zynaps/work/mkgame/original.bin) against the snapshots ../zynaps/work/images/title.bin and play-start.bin.
# Details and numbers: ../zynaps/work/ann/60-notes.md.

@module buffers ; The map ring, the start-up copy's source and the glyph cache
@about $B700-$DFFF is work space in play, but in block 2 it carries the 3072 bytes the start-up ($B6FF) copies down to $5C00-$67FF before anything else runs: the 256 scenery tiles ($BC00-$C3FF) and the cells of the wave sprites ($C400-$C7FF). The rest of the range is zero in block 2.
@about In play the map ring takes $B700-$BFFF (18 rows of 128 bytes: a tile code and a flag byte a column, cleared by LevelSetUp $8C3B, its first 47 bytes the start-up code itself) and the glyph cache $C000-$DFFF (256 glyphs in four shifts, pages $C0, $C8, $D8, $D0 for 0, 2, 4, 6 pixels; line k of glyph n at page + k*256 + n; built by BuildGlyph $89C9, read by the span handlers $873E, $8797, $87E0, $8819).

$B72F s MapRingSpace ; The map ring from $B72F on (zero in block 2)
> In play: the map ring $B700-$BFFF, the playfield's 18 rows of 128 bytes, two rows a page; a row's record holds its pointer (+0/+1 = $B71F + $80 * row, set by LevelSetUp $8C3B). The scenery scripts write tile codes into it, RowStart ($86CE) pushes a row onto the attributes, BuildGlyph ($89C9) and ScrollStep ($8AED) read it (48K c2: $86CE 11862 entries reading $B700-$BFFF).
> Its first 47 bytes ($B700-$B72E) are the start-up routine at $B6FF, dead once it has run; LevelSetUp clears $B700-$BFFF (LD HL,$B700, BC = $08FF) before the first level.
> In block 2 these 1233 bytes are zero: no leftover.
$B730 ; ScrollColumnB starts here ($8022 = $B730 at level start)
$B760 ; ScrollColumn starts here ($801C = $B760 at level start)

$BC00 b TileSource 16 ; The 256 scenery tiles, as the start-up copies them to TileGraphics $5C00
@res gfx/tiles.bin
> 2048 bytes: 8 line pages of 256 bytes, line y of tile t at $BC00 + y*256 + t. The start-up ($B6FF: LD HL,$BC00, DE = $5C00, BC = $0C00, LDIR) copies them with the wave cells to $5C00-$67FF, where BuildGlyph ($89C9) reads them through TileSet $7F6D (48K c2: $5C00-$63BA, tile codes up to $BA in level 1); a tile drawn mirrored goes through BitReverse $FB00, flipped through the line pages +$700 down.
> In play $BC00-$BFFF is the map ring's last four rows and $C000-$C3FF the glyph cache's first page: the copy is dead after the start-up. Editable as src/res/gfx/tiles.gfx.png (16 x 16 tiles).
$C000 GlyphCache ; the glyph cache $C000-$DFFF in play (shift 0 page; BuildBigSprite $8F0C builds into it too)

$C400 b WaveCellSource 16 ; The cells of the wave sprites, copied to $6400
@res gfx/wave-cells.bin
> 1024 bytes copied to $6400-$67FF by the start-up: 64 columns of 16 lines (two 8-line cells), column-major, a set bit the sprite's pixel. A wave sprite of kind A < 16 is two columns at $6400 + A*32; from 16 up three columns at $6620 + (A-16)*48 (LoadCellSprite $ACD9: HL = $6400 + (A>>3)*256 + (A&7)*32, plus (A-16)*16 + $20 from 16 up).
> BuildCellSprite ($8EDB) complements them into a sprite cache image (SpriteCacheImages $E748) with a blank column after each; 48K c2 reads only $64E0-$64FF and $65E0-$65FF (kinds 7 and 15, level 1). $6600-$661F (two columns) is no sprite of that addressing. Editable as src/res/gfx/wave-cells.gfx.png.

$C800 s GlyphCacheUpper ; The glyph cache pages $C8-$DF (zero in block 2)
> In play: the glyphs shifted by 2, 6 and 4 pixels (pages $C8, $D0, $D8; BuildGlyph $89C9 writes them, the span handlers read them). 6144 zero bytes in block 2.

@module graphics ; Sprite images
@about The sprites are drawn column by column: a column is the sprite's height in bytes, the columns follow each other, and a sprite comes as 4 or 8 copies pre-shifted by 2 or 1 pixel. A sprite descriptor (16 bytes in $7E94-$7F5x: the image, the frame and shift strides, the shift mask, the lines and columns) says which.
@about In block 2 the images of $E000-$E747 and $F048-$F107 are stored complemented; the start-up ($B6FF) inverts them in place, so in play a clear bit shows the object's colour (its attribute +$25 is a paper colour) and a set bit keeps the cell black. The sprite cache images ($E748-$F047) are built in play from the wave cells; the tape bytes there are a leftover of the mastering session.

$E000 b SpritePad ; 8 bytes above the player's image (blank in play)
@res gfx/sprites.bin
> Zero in block 2, $FF after the start-up. BuildObjectStrips ($8FC2) moves an image pointer back by the sprite's Y within its cell (0-7), so the first column of the first image reads up to 7 bytes before it: these keep that blank.

$E008 b PlayerSprite 24 ; The player's ship: 8 shifts of 3 columns x 24 lines
@res gfx/sprites.bin
> Descriptor $7E94 (the ship's record $F36C +0/+1). 576 bytes, a shift every 72; lines 16-23 of each column are blank. Complemented in block 2.

$E248 b SightSprite 24 ; The homing sight: 8 shifts of 3 columns x 24 lines
@res gfx/sprites.bin
> Descriptor $7EAE (the sight's record $F600, kind 6, SightHandler $9E03): two pairs of brackets 11 lines apart. Complemented in block 2. Not drawn in 48K c1/c2 (the homing missile was never taken).

$E488 b ExplosionFrames 24 ; The explosion: 8 frames of 2 columns x 24 lines
@res gfx/sprites.bin
> Descriptor $7EA1 (StartExplosion $A293 sets it with frame 1): the image base $E458 + frame * 48, frames 1-8 here; ExplosionStep ($A1C8) ends at frame 9. A ball that grows, bursts and scatters. Complemented in block 2.

$E608 b LaserSprite 8 ; The laser shot: 4 shifts (2 pixels apart) of 2 columns x 8 lines
@res gfx/sprites.bin
> Descriptor $7EC8 (the four shot records from $F4F8, kind 1, LaserHandler $9E32): a dashed line on the first line. Complemented in block 2.

$E648 b BallSprite 16 ; The enemy shot and plasma ball: 8 shifts of 2 columns x 16 lines
@res gfx/sprites.bin
> Descriptor $7ED5 (the enemy shot records from $F474, kind 5; the plasma balls $F5A8 and $F5D4, kind 9): a 7-line ball. Complemented in block 2.

$E748 b SpriteCacheImages 16 ; The sprite cache images, also the panel's save area
> 2304 bytes, two uses in turn:
> - the images of the cached enemy sprites: the descriptors from $7EF2 point at $E748, $E988, $EBC8 and $EE08 (8 shifts of 3 columns x 24 lines, $240 each), the 4-column ones at $E748, $EBC8 and $E988 ($300 each). BuildCellSprite ($8EDB) writes the unshifted copy from the wave cells, complemented; ShiftSprite ($8F5A-$8F65) and the copy at $AD44 make the other 7 shifts (48K c2: $E748-$E987).
> - SavePanel ($A98D) saves the screen third $5000-$57FF here and the attribute rows 19-23 ($5A60-$5AFF) at $EF48; RestorePanel ($A979) puts them back (48K c2: saved frame 610, restored 780 at the player change, sprites rebuilt at frames 102-1274).
> In block 2: a saved panel left from the mastering session (1578 of the 2048 bitmap bytes equal the 48K c2 screen of frame 250 at $5000; 135 of the 160 attribute bytes) and zero after $EFE8. Nothing reads them before SavePanel or BuildCellSprite writes: a leftover.
$E988 ; second cache image
$EBC8 ; third cache image (the second 4-column one)
$EE08 ; fourth cache image
$EF48 ; the saved attribute rows 19-23 (160 bytes)
$EFE8 ; zero in block 2 from here

$F048 b PodSprite 16 ; The weapon pod: 4 shifts (2 pixels apart) of 3 columns x 16 lines
@res gfx/pod.bin
> Descriptor $7EBB (set by ExplosionStep's end $A2D6 when the dead enemy leaves a pod: kind 4, colour $08, PodStep $A1FD). A round ball of 13 lines. Complemented in block 2.

@module records ; The row records, the object records and their strip buffers
@about The playfield's 18 rows each have a 32-byte record (RowRecords, the renderer's SP walks it); the 16 moving objects each have a 44-byte record (from the player at $F36C to the sight at $F600); every object has a buffer of 12-byte strip entries, one per character row it covers, which BuildObjectStrips links into the rows' strip lists.

$F108 s RecordsGap ; 36 bytes nothing uses
> Zero in block 2, read or written by nothing in 48K c1 or 48K c2.

$F12C b RowRecords 32 ; The 18 row records of the playfield, 32 bytes each
> Zero in block 2; LevelSetUp ($8C3B) fills them. Fields (10-notes.md, F1): +0/+1 the map ring pointer ($B71F + $80 * row), +2/+3 the attribute address ($583F + $20 * row), +4/+5 the screen address, +6/+7 the head span node (SentinelNode $85E4 when empty), +8 the glyph count, +$0A the node count, +$0B/+$0C the glyph-slot bit and pointer into GlyphBitmap $FFE0, +$0D/+$0E the last node, +$0F the cache slot, +$10/+$11 the column builder's bit and pointer, +$12 the scroll countdown, +$13, +$14 flags, +$15 the strip count (ClearStripCounts $8F70), +$16/+$17 the strip list, +$18-+$1F the row's star (InitStars $8846 PUSHes from SP = $F14C, StarsStep $8894 from $F144).

$F36C b PlayerObject 44 ; The player's ship record (kind 0)
> The first of 16 object records of 44 bytes ($F36C-$F62B). Fields: +0/+1 the sprite descriptor, +2 the frame, +3/+4 the X (+3 the fraction, its bits 5-7 the shift; +4 the column + 16), +5/+6 the Y (+5 the fraction, its bits 5-7 the line; +6 the row), +7 the kind (KindHandlers $80FA), +8 flags (bit 7 active, bit 5 drawn), +9/+$0A the strip buffer, +$0B the row mask of the strips, +$0D the hit mask, +$0E/+$0F the X speed, +$10/+$11 the X acceleration, +$12/+$13 the Y speed, +$14/+$15 the Y acceleration (PathOpAccelerate $9529), +$24 the slot's collision bit, +$25 the colour, +$26/+$27 counters.
> Block 2 holds every record's start values: the descriptors, the strip buffers, the masks +$0B, the collision bits +$24 and the colours +$25 (the ship: $7E94, $F62C, $E0, $01, $45; play sets its colour again); ResetPlayers and the level start rewrite the moving fields.
$F379 PlayerHitMask ; +$0D: the object bits the ship touches this pass
$F37C PlayerAccelX ; +$10/+$11: the X acceleration (PlayerSetup $B21C sets $20)
$F380 PlayerAccelY ; +$14/+$15: the Y acceleration ($40)

$F398 b EnemyObjects 44 ; The five enemy records (kinds 2, 8, 11 and the cache kinds)
> $F398, $F3C4, $F3F0, $F41C, $F448; with the three enemy shots they are the 8 slots of the hit grid (bit n = slot n, +$24 = $02, $04 ... $80). In block 2 all five are empty (kind 0, no descriptor) with their strip buffers ($F650 + 36 n), +$0B = $E0 and the collision bits.

$F474 b EnemyShots 44 ; The three enemy shot records (kind 5, BallSprite)
> Descriptor $7ED5, colour $48, strip buffers $F734, $F74C, $F764 (two strips each).

$F4F8 b LaserShots 44 ; The four laser shot records (kind 1, LaserSprite)
> Descriptor $7EC8, colour $78, a strip each ($F77C, $F788, $F794, $F7A0).

$F5A8 b PlasmaBall2 44 ; The second plasma ball (the double plasma)
> Kind 9, descriptor $7ED5, strip buffer $F7AC.

$F5D4 b SideShot 44 ; The side weapon's shot: plasma ball, homing missile or seeker
> Kinds 9, 10, 7; strip buffer $F7C4.

$F600 b SightObject 44 ; The homing sight (kind 6, SightSprite)
> Descriptor $7EAE, colour $78, strip buffer $F7DC; RunObjects starts here and walks down by 44 bytes.

$F62C b StripBuffers 12 ; The objects' strip entries, 12 bytes each
> 39 entries: the ship 3 ($F62C), the five enemies 3 each from $F650, four spare from $F704, the enemy shots 2 each from $F734, the laser shots 1 each from $F77C, the plasma ball 2 ($F7AC), the side shot 2 ($F7C4), the sight 3 ($F7DC).
> An entry: +0/+1 a link word, +2/+3 StripDraw ($8603), +4/+5 the image pointer, +6 the colour and +7 the columns, +8 the attribute column and +9 a byte StripDraw patches into $866D, +$0A/+$0B the next strip's +2 (StripDraw returns into it). BuildObjectStrips ($8FC2) fills +4-+$0B with PUSHes and steps over +2/+3 (DEC SP, DEC SP), and RowEnd returns into +2: the $8603 words are never written by the code, they come from block 2. So does the link word (the same in the title and play snapshots).
$F650 ; the enemies' entries
$F704 ; four entries no record points at in the image
$F734 ; the enemy shots' entries
$F77C ; the laser shots' entries
$F7AC ; the plasma ball's entries
$F7C4 ; the side shot's entries
$F7DC ; the sight's entries

@module tables ; The span node pool, the bit reverse table, the hit grid, the script stack and the interrupt vectors
@about All built in play: block 2 holds zeros here. (The 48K tape had a leftover of its mastering machine at $FDE8-$FE4A, a receive routine over the ZX Interface 1 RS-232 port; the 2 in 1 cleared it.)

$F800 s NodePool ; 95 span nodes of 8 bytes
> LevelSetUp ($8C3B) links them into the free list (head NodeFreeList $7F68) and ends it with $FFFF at $FAF8. A node: +0/+1 the span handler, +2 the column, +3 the countdown, +4/+5 the next node, +6 the width. PopNode $8AD0, PushNode $8ADF, the scroll step $8AED and $8C1C walk it (48K c2).
$FAF8 ; the end marker ($FFFF)

$FB00 s BitReverse ; 256 bytes, each the bit reverse of its index
> Built by LevelSetUp ($8C3B, loop $8CE9). BuildGlyph ($89C9) patches its own load to look tiles up here for a mirrored tile (the row's flag bit 1); DrawSpinner ($A8BE) mirrors the panel's spinner with it.

$FC00 s HitGridX ; The collision grid: one byte a column (play) / the menu's raster colours
> Play: ClearHitGrid ($91C6, SP = $FD90, 200 bytes of PUSHes) clears $FC00-$FD8F in phases 2-4, MarkEnemyGrid ($90CB) ORs the collision bit of each enemy slot into the X bytes (step 4) and the Y bytes $FD00 (step 8), TestHitGrid ($9150) ANDs them for the ship and its shots.
> Menu: ClearColourBuffer ($A5A7) zeroes it, ExpandColours ($A5D1 and its callers $A685, $A790) writes a colour per scan line from $FC20, the menu raster interrupt ($A512) reads $FC20-$FC7F.
$FC20 MenuColours ; the menu's raster colours from here, one a scan line

$FD00 s HitGridY ; The Y half of the collision grid
> $FD00-$FD8F, cleared with HitGridX; MarkEnemyGrid ($90CB) ORs the enemies' collision bits in, TestHitGrid ($9150) reads them (48K c2: 331 and 494 entries).

$FD90 b ScriptStack 16 ; The scenery script's stack (down from $FDFB)
> The scenery coroutine runs on its own stack: StartScript ($8D35) sets its SP ScriptSP ($801A) to $FDFB and puts the script's entry $8D72 there; ScriptYield ($8D45) and ScriptResume ($8D69) swap SP between it and the main stack. 48K c2 play-start holds return addresses at $FDD9-$FDFA.
> Zero in block 2. (The 48K tape had a download routine of its mastering machine from $FDE8 to $FE4A here: a count, an address and bytes over the ZX Interface 1 RS-232 port, never used by the game.)

$FDFB w ScriptStackTop ; The scenery script's first return address
> StartScript ($8D35) writes $8D72 here (the script entry) before the first resume. Zero in block 2.

$FDFD b InterruptJump 3 ; JP to the frame handler, where every IM 2 interrupt lands
> SetUpInterrupts ($8516) writes JP $A9E4 here; the handler word $FDFE is switched by the menu and play code. Zero in block 2.
$FDFE InterruptHandler ; the JP's address: the current interrupt routine

$FE00 b VectorTable 16 ; The IM 2 vector table: 257 bytes of $FD
> SetUpInterrupts ($8516) fills $FE00-$FF00 with $FD and sets I = $FE, so any vector the bus gives reads $FDFD. Reads at $FEFF/$FF00 in the recordings are interrupts taken inside routines (48K c1: $A321 1566 entries, 48K c2: $899E 3948).
> Zero in block 2. With K-MOUSE the set-up copies the mouse driver to $FE00-$FEDC (src/game/mouse.asm) and makes SetUpInterrupts fill the table from $FEE8: I = $FE still, and the vectors the bus gives on a Pentagon ($FF) read $FFFF/$FF00 inside the fill.
